## Part 2: Evaluating Markets, Sectors, and Strategies

**Part overview (p. 83-84):**
- Analyze mood swings in the market
- Understand chart advantages, select sectors with strength, and project price movement
- Practice paper trading
- Design a killer trading plan

---

## Chapter 5: Analyzing Mood Swings in the Market

**Chapter topics:**
- Digging deeper into market results
- Using option activity to determine market mood
- Assessing indicator levels

### Introduction [MUST KNOW]

- When trading stocks, gauge whether a market has sustainable momentum or reversal likelihood using **breadth tools** and **sentiment analysis**
- Examples: NYSE advance-decline line (breadth), put/call ratios (sentiment)
- By examining advancing vs. declining stocks, you understand **why** markets behave certain ways and whether gains/losses were driven by a handful of stocks or broad participation
- Market advances last longer and have better "health" when more stocks are advancing
- Options market can also gauge stock market health — monitoring option activity gives sense of degree of fear or greed associated with an advance or decline
- Success in market sentiment analysis: be aware of when each indicator delivers **extreme readings**
- Past data identifies levels associated with unsustainable advances or declines

### A Few Words About Select Macro Factors [SHOULD KNOW]

Before analyzing markets technically, consider these macro factors:

- **Interest rates:** Low or falling rates are bullish for stocks; rising rates eventually negative
  - General interest rate trend affects underlying stock prices, which affects option prices
  - U.S. **Federal Reserve** is still the most influential central bank globally
  - Other central banks now matter too: **People's Bank of China**, **European Central Bank**
  - After 9 years of near-zero rates post-subprime mortgage crisis, rate reversal was anticipated — when stocks become less attractive vs. bonds, significant market decline expected
- **Global economic trends:** First 16 years of the 21st century, global economies mostly synchronized (China, Europe, U.S. as a block)
  - Changed significantly after **Brexit** (U.K. exit from EU) and election of **Donald Trump** as U.S. President
  - These game-changing events reversed decades-long trade doctrines and pacts
  - Economic activity in all three areas will still eventually lead to central bank action, affecting stocks and options
- Bottom line: Chapter is geared toward technical analysis, but keep an eye on macro external factors

### Assessing the Market's Bias [MUST KNOW]

- The market's main job (per market old-timers) is **to make investors look foolish as often as possible**
- Market has a mind of its own — weak economic numbers can trigger rallies; strong earnings can trigger declines
- Can't be 100% certain about any market event or response, but can be **better prepared** by following market breadth and sentiment data
- Familiar use of these indicators lets you sense the market's mood **before** crazy swings alert you to pending changes

### Judging the Strength of a Move [MUST KNOW]

- All market advances and declines are NOT the same
- Advances can occur at a moderate pace (sectors rising together), frenzied manner (some sectors strongly outperforming), or any variation
- **Waste of time to predict what market will do at any one time** — instead, assess odds of a trend continuing
- Trade in the direction of the **dominant trend**, whether up or down
- Market breadth helps decide with high certainty whether:
  - An advance is improving (more room to run)
  - More room on the downside as market deteriorates

> "Keep the odds in your favor by using a variety of tools to confirm your market assessments and then use strategies consistent with such assessments." — *Remember box*

> "Large profits are often the product of vigilance, patience, and execution of your trading strategies and overall plan. If you can spot a potential change in the trend early, you can plan for it and gain from following a new trend for a longer period of time." — *Tip box*

### Defining Market Breadth [MUST KNOW]

- **Market breadth** = the market's heartbeat; gives in-depth look at internal components of market rises and declines
- Track number of **advancing vs. declining stocks** for a specific index to gauge health of the move
- During rising markets: want gains spread out among the **largest variety of companies possible**
- During declines: look for signs that **participation is so broad the bear exhausts itself**

**Breadth indicators use statistics based on:**
- Number and volume for advancing and declining issues
- Number of issues reaching new highs or lows
- Up and down volume
- Issues trading above or below moving average lines

**Bearish divergence** = advancing market with declining stocks outnumbering advancing stocks in both number and volume — a select group does well but overall market is not healthy

- **Bullish sign:** Market advances where stocks in variety of sectors are trading above their **200-day moving average** (the line that divides bull and bear markets) — suggests healthy rise likely to continue

### The Advance-Decline Line Breadth Indicator [MUST KNOW]

**Formula:**
```
Adv-Dec Line = # of Advancing Issues – # of Declining Issues
```
(Kept as a daily cumulative total)

- The **advance-decline (A-D) line**, used in combination with the **NYSE Composite Index (NYA)**
- NOT looking for a specific bullish/bearish number — use it as a **confirming or diverging tool via visual inspection**
- Most important aspect: the picture of the line itself — whether it's trending up, down, or sideways **relative to** the market/index being tracked
- Unchanged issues are excluded from this indicator
- Focus on **visual trends**, not absolute values

**Tip:** Find the A-D line graphic at StockCharts.com under Market Breadth Indicators — use the Cumulative View option

[CHART: chart_p090_01.png]
**Figure 5-1** — Daily Advance-Decline Line with NYSE Composite Index (NYA) overlay. Built in Microsoft Excel, showing 2007 data.

What the chart shows:
- Two lines plotted on dual y-axes: A-D Line (left axis, ranging ~10K–100K) and NYA (right axis, ranging ~8500–10500)
- X-axis spans January 2007 through November 2007
- The NYA (right axis) shows a rising trend from Jan to July 2007, with a rising trendline drawn
- The A-D Line (upper, thicker line) also rises initially but shows a **descending trendline** through the same period — it peaks earlier and starts falling
- The A-D Line diverges from NYA: NYA made higher highs while the A-D Line made lower highs

**Author's teaching:** This is a **classic warning sign** from the A-D line prior to a major decline. From June to mid-July 2007, NYA was rising, but this move **was not confirmed** by a large number of advancing issues. The A-D Line was diverging from NYA — a **significant warning sign** that correctly predicted serious declines.

> "Any significant decline since 1987 has been preceded by a negative divergence in the NYSE Advance-Decline line. This indicator remains intact as of this printing." — *Tip box*

**Trading rule illustrated:** When NYA (or any index) makes new highs but the A-D line does not confirm (fails to make new highs or is falling), this **bearish divergence** signals the advance is narrowing and vulnerable to reversal.

### Calculating the Advance-Decline Line as a Ratio [SHOULD KNOW]

- Can also calculate A-D line as a **ratio** (Adv/Dec) rather than cumulative value
- Results in an **oscillating indicator** with these characteristics:
  - When more advancers: ratio > 1 (more common)
  - When more decliners: ratio moves between 0 and 1
- Monitor oscillator crosses of the center line, divergent movement, or movement into extreme ranges for potential turns
- **Contrarian strategies** = counter to existing market trends; represent higher risk trades because they anticipate a change in momentum and direction

**Tip:** Adv-Dec indicators can be calculated on any index or ETF that provides daily advancing and declining statistics
- In rising markets: look for advancers to outpace decliners
- In falling markets: look for decliners to outpace advancers
- When indicator **diverges** from index action, the current trend may be in trouble
- Reliable Adv-Dec numbers for Dow 30, S&P 500, and several international indexes available at Investing.com (Real Time Financial Markets: World Indexes section)

### Refining the Adv-Dec Line with Technical Indicators [MUST KNOW]

- Analyze the A-D line as if it were an index or tradable stock by adding simple but useful indicators to its price chart
- Duarte's preferred combination: **20-day simple moving average (SMA)**, **RSI (Relative Strength Indicator)**, and **Rate of Change (ROC)**

[CHART: chart_p093_02.png]
**Figure 5-2** — NYSE Advance Decline line (SNYAD) with 20-day moving average, RSI(14), and ROC(12) indicators. Five-year chart from StockCharts.com, April 2013 – May 2017 (as of 31-Mar-2017).

What the chart shows:
- **Top panel:** SNYAD Cumulative line (blue/dark) with MA(20) (red/pink) overlaid. Y-axis ranges from ~130K to 280K+. Both lines generally trend upward over the 5-year period with multiple consolidations and pullbacks.
- **Middle panel:** RSI(14) oscillating roughly 10–90 range. Horizontal dashed lines at approximately 30 and 70. RSI reading shown as 65.31 at chart date.
- **Bottom panel:** ROC(12) oscillating around zero, ranging approximately -7.5 to +7.5. ROC reading shown as 1.21 at chart date.

**20-Day Moving Average interpretation:**
- When A-D line is **above** the 20-day SMA → broad market trend is UP
- When A-D line **falls below** the SMA → broad market trend is DOWN
- When line **chops above and below** the SMA → broad market is looking for direction

**RSI interpretation (applied to A-D line):**
- **Very low RSI readings** generally mark a **market bottom** (or upside reversal)
- **Very high RSI readings** generally mark a **market top** (or downside reversal)
- Gives early clues about whether the broad market is oversold or overbought

**ROC interpretation:**
- Gauges whether **momentum** in the current direction is rising or falling
- A change in ROC trend can warn of a change in the A-D line trend
- Useful in combination with RSI for marking meaningful tops and bottoms
- Best signals: when RSI and ROC **confirm each other** (e.g., both signaling a top is close)

**Chart analysis — key periods:**
- **April to October 2015 / through February 2016:** A-D line rolled over in conjunction with RSI and ROC declining — a warning period
- **July to October 2016:** Two double bottoms in the A-D line; line struggled to stay above 20-day SMA
- **April 2016 onward:** Pattern changed — A-D line tended to stay above 20-day SMA → **bullish period**
- **November 2016 (U.S. presidential election):** A-D line made a double bottom (with RSI and ROC also bottoming), then moved higher with series of higher highs
- **Post-election rally (through May 31, 2017):** A-D line stayed mostly above 20-day SMA — rally still alive

> "Technical indicators such as RSI and ROC are useful tools to confirm price trends and important price turning points. I use RSI and ROC along with the 20-day moving average because this indicator combination makes the Adv-Dec line a much more useful tool to keep you on the right side of the trade." — *Remember box*

### Taking a Long-Term Look Back [SHOULD KNOW]

- Best practice: track indicators' **long-term track record** (five years of NYSE Adv-Dec data in this example)
- Looking at periods when 20-day SMA, RSI, and ROC were **all in sync** (especially at bottoms and tops) would have kept you on the right side of the trend
- More data = better off; compare any current situation to one major top and one major bottom in the dataset
- In the 5-year chart, two key examples:
  1. **October 2014–April 2015** → continued bullish conditions
  2. **Early 2017** → major trading bottom
- **Risk management principle:** Focus on managing risk for existing conditions rather than betting on anticipated moves
- Breadth indicators confirmed by momentum indicators and moving averages are **extremely useful** in identifying bottoms (and often tops)
- **Bottoms are easier to identify** because market declines occur much faster

> "The NYSE Advance Decline line, especially when combined with RSI, ROC, and the 20-day moving average, is an excellent combination of indicators to help you pinpoint the dominant trend in the market and important trend changes." — *Tip box*

### (Psycho)-Analyzing the Market [MUST KNOW]

- Some want to know **why** markets do what they do — but over the long haul, it's better to make money and let someone else answer "why"
- **Markets act on whatever information is available at any one time** — whether the response is right or wrong, it is based on the opinion of the majority of players
- When the most money moves in any direction, the market follows
- **Key point:** It doesn't really matter why markets do what they do — you want to be on the right side as often as possible
- Human nature is about self-preservation and self-interest — buying/selling securities = making/losing money → market participants bring **irrationality** (driven by self-preservation) to the game
- Multiply one irrational person by many irrational people → crowds move prices up or down quickly or slowly, depending on the day
- Crowds feed on each other's greed or fear → very strange behavior
- Useful step: identify which human emotion is in command at the time — **greed or fear**

### Defining Sentiment [MUST KNOW]

- **Sentiment** = overriding bias for the market — bullish or bearish
- **Greed** (with a touch of fear) generally drives bullish markets
- **Fear** drives declines
- These greed-fear patterns repeat month after month, year after year, decade after decade regardless of economic changes
- Think of market as an ocean full of waves — sentiment = undulation of waves
- Only variation cycle to cycle: length of time each emotion rules and underlying dynamics of each wave

**Sentiment tools** use stock and options statistics to provide information about crowd activity during advances and declines:
- Data available from exchange websites or charting packages
- Sentiment analysis tries to identify periods when **greed has gotten unsustainable** or **fear is just about exhausted**
- Analogy: weird game of musical chairs — at some point music stops, everyone scrambles; you want to be prepared to respond quickly when direction changes

### Measuring Investor Actions [MUST KNOW]

- Sentiment analysis makes you a "Doubting Thomas" — measures bullish/bearish **actions** vs. what's being **said** about the market
- Watch for factors that don't make sense together, such as:
  - Bullish commentary contradicted by **unusually high put volume**
  - A mild economic report that produces a wild swing in the market
  - A major interest rate policy change (e.g., Fed raising rates after years of zero rates) that triggers an unexpected 3% rally in a single day

**Options market gives quick indications about trader sentiment:**
- Are traders bullish? → buying calls / selling puts
- Are traders bearish? → buying puts / selling calls

> "Option data primarily provides insight to fear. Historical volatility and volume measures give you a feel for how much emotion was involved with moves in the past. Implied volatility levels let you know what's anticipated for the road ahead." — *Tip box*

---

## Watching Call and Put Activity [MUST KNOW]

- Professional traders are generally optimistic — that's why markets spend more time going up than down
- **Baseline fact:** Call volume typically exceeds put volume, reflecting the market's tendency to advance
- When investors get nervous, **put volume increases**
- Monitoring the **put-to-call relationship** lets you identify extreme levels corresponding with market reversals
- Indicators may use call and put volume combined, or put volume alone, to measure fear or complacency

> "Consider using extreme sentiment readings to reduce positions in the direction of the trend and slowly establish counter-trend positions." — *Tip box*

### Understanding Put-to-Call Ratios [MUST KNOW]

- **Martin Zweig** (late) is credited with creating **put-to-call (P:C) ratios**
- Formula: divide put contract volume by call contract volume
- Zweig famously **predicted the 1987 stock market crash** on national television the Friday before "Black Monday" and made money during the crash by being short
- P:C ratios are **contrarian measures** — implications are opposite to current market sentiment

**How to read P:C ratios (contrarian interpretation):**
- **Extremely low readings** → bearish (everyone is bullish/complacent → danger of decline)
- **Extremely high readings** → bullish (everyone is excessively bearish → conditions right for a rally)

### Key P:C Ratio Instruments [MUST KNOW]

- **CBOE equity P:C ratio:** Total volume for all stock options on the **CBOE**
  - Less comprehensive than before given gains from **International Securities Exchange (ISE)**
  - Readings above **0.90** → increased fear / oversold conditions
  - More recently, readings above **1.0** reflect rising fear

- **CBOE index only P:C ratio:** Total volume for all index options on the CBOE (includes **SPX** and **OEX** index volume)
  - Distinct: reflects the **index options trader** — considered more sophisticated and on-target with market moves
  - High readings reflect **pending bearishness** for the market (NOT a contrarian interpretation — sophisticated traders who buy puts tend to be right)
  - Readings above **2.4** → approximately 3 standard deviations above mean → have occurred **immediately before** short-term and intermediate-term tops
  - *Remember: Drops in the market typically happen faster than rallies*

- **ISE Sentiment Index (ISEE):** Actually a **call/put ratio** (inverse of P:C)
  - Focuses on **new buys only** (vs. total volume, which reflects short sellers too)

<!-- pp. 101-120 -->

## Chapter 5: Using Breadth and Sentiment Indicators (continued)

### ISE Sentiment Index (continued) [MUST KNOW]

- The ISE index **excludes market maker and other professional trader
  activity**, leaving only customer activity (money managers and retail)
- Because this index is an **inverse** of the typical P:C ratio, **low
  readings coincide with extreme bearishness** and are interpreted as
  predictive of market advances on a contrarian basis
- Since 2002, readings less than **85** are 2SD below the average and
  have coincided with short-term and intermediate-term bottoms
- Updated every 20 minutes during the trading day

**ISE index and ETF put/call ratio:** Total volume for all index and ETF
options trading on the ISE. Augments the CBOE index-only ratio to round
out assessment of broad hedging activity.

**Three keys to getting the most from sentiment tools:**
1. Know basic indicator construction information
2. Understand the historical extremes and implications for the tool
3. Recognize significant market changes and impact on indicator data

- For individual security sentiment, capture data from **all exchanges**
  that trade options for that underlying
- Rather than a specific reading, identify **atypical readings** by
  calculating the average value and standard deviations, then add lines
  to identify extreme levels

---

### Figure 5-3: SPY P:C Ratio with 5-day SMA [MUST KNOW]

[CHART: chart_p102_03.png]
Daily chart for the S&P 500 ETF (SPY) showing SPY price (upper line,
ranging from ~120 to ~157), the raw put/call ratio (light gray spiky
line at the bottom), and a 5-day simple moving average of the P:C ratio
(thick dark line). A horizontal line marks +1SD above the mean P:C
ratio. Time range: 11/14/2006 to 10/22/2007.

**What the chart teaches:**
- The raw P:C ratio oscillates erratically around the mean
- The 5-day SMA smooths the noise and reveals extremes more cleanly
- **Trading rule:** When the 5-day SMA for the SPY put/call ratio
  reached more than **+1SD**, a near-term bottom was signaled **six of
  eight times**
- A rising P:C ratio = increasing put buying = increasing fear = often
  a contrarian bullish signal once extreme is reached

> TIP: Indicators may behave differently during bull and bear markets
> and even during different stages of bull or bear markets (early, mid,
> late). When using a new indicator, check its performance during
> similar periods in the past.

- Many widely followed ETFs and their options trade until **4:15 p.m.
  Eastern time** — be sure to track the correct closing time and price

---

### Using the Put Volume Indicator (PVI) [MUST KNOW]

- The **put volume indicator (PVI)** is a sentiment tool created by
  **John Bollinger** that measures relative put activity levels
- Similar to a P:C ratio, with extreme readings identifying periods of
  excessive fear or complacency
- Can be applied to individual stocks, indexes, or an entire exchange

**PVI calculation:**
- Divide **daily put option volume** by the **10-day simple moving
  average (SMA)** of that volume
- A **rising ratio** indicates:
  - Put volume is increasing
  - Bearish sentiment is increasing

---

### Figure 5-4: Daily SPY PVI with SPY [MUST KNOW]

[CHART: chart_p104_02.png]
Dual-panel chart titled "Daily SPY PVI with SPY (Mean and +2, -1 Std
Dev Lines)." Upper portion: SPY daily price line ranging from ~122 to
~158. Lower portion: PVI line oscillating around a central mean of
approximately 1.0. Three horizontal reference lines are drawn:
- Central mean line at ~1.25
- +2SD line above (at ~5.25 level)
- -1SD line below (at ~0.25 level)
Vertical lines highlight moments of extreme PVI readings. Time range:
11/21/2006 to 10/9/2007.

**What the chart teaches:**
- Very high PVI readings (spikes above the mean) identify **extreme
  pessimism**, which sets the tone for a reversal
- Four of six extreme highs coincided with, or occurred shortly before,
  an **acceleration of selling and a subsequent bottom reversal** in
  the market
- **Trading rule:** High PVI readings are NOT necessarily immediate
  contrarian buy signals — they signal that bears are gaining the upper
  hand and an acceleration of the downtrend may be near. Pay even
  closer attention to market action when PVI spikes, because:
  - Down trends tend to be shorter in duration than up trends
  - A big move to the downside becomes increasingly likely
  - But a reversal may also be close at hand

> TIP: To build the PVI yourself, download CBOE put/call ratio data
> (free in spreadsheet format) and compute daily put volume divided by
> its 10-day SMA.

---

### Using Volatility to Measure Fear [MUST KNOW]

- Fear is a **stronger emotion than greed** — panic is more dramatic
  than elation
- Markets generally **fall much faster than they rise**
- Increased volatility (larger daily/weekly price swings) accompanies
  fear-driven selling
- Volatility sentiment measures help recognize **declines nearing
  exhaustion**

### Measuring Volatility

**Historical volatility (HV):**
- Measures the price range for a security over a given period
- Calculated using various trading periods, annualized so different
  securities can be compared
- Can be plotted on a chart to view trends and assess whether current
  HV is high or low relative to history

**Implied volatility (IV):**
- An option pricing component referred to as a **plug figure**
- The volatility level that accounts for the current option price after
  all other tangible factors (price, time, interest rates) have been
  valued
- IV incorporates HV because it's reasonable to expect the stock to
  move similarly to the past — but IV is not necessarily equal to HV
- Can be plotted on a chart, revealing trends and strong **seasonal
  tendencies** for certain stocks

### Recognizing Impact from Changing Volatility [MUST KNOW]

- **Goal:** Buy options when IV is relatively low; sell when IV is
  relatively high — though no guarantee low or high conditions won't
  persist

**Rules:**
- When IV is **relatively low and increases quickly**, it adds value to
  both calls and puts
- When IV is **relatively high and decreases quickly**, it decreases
  value for both calls and puts

- Pending news and reports, along with unexpected events, can **spike
  IV suddenly**
- After the news/event is past and an initial reaction occurs, IV
  declines as quickly as it spiked
- Gradual changes in IV can also occur in either direction

---

### Spelling Fear the Wall Street Way: V-I-X [MUST KNOW]

- **VIX** stands for **volatility index**
- A **blended implied volatility value** calculated using specific
  S&P 500 Index option contracts
- Used as a **sentiment indicator**; CBOE publishes VIX closing values
  daily; because VIX is an optionable index, trading programs graph
  values in real time

**VIX reading rules:**
- **Climbing VIX** → bearish conditions in SPX and typically the
  broader market
- **Declining VIX** → neutral to bullish conditions in SPX and
  typically the broader market

- Overly bearish sentiment → high VIX levels → eventually bearish fear
  exhausted → reversal in stocks → VIX declines

> Technical note: The VIX was previously calculated using the S&P 100
> (OEX) Index. That former VIX measure can now be accessed using the
> symbol **VXO**.

---

### Figure 5-5: Weekly VIX Chart with SPX Overlay [MUST KNOW]

[CHART: chart_p107_03.png]
Weekly chart (platform: Optionetics) showing the VIX as a jagged gray
line and the S&P 500 Cash (SPX) as a thick dark line. Title: "Market
Volatility (CBOE-no options) – Weekly Line Chart [Close] / S&P 500
Cash – Weekly Line Chart [Close]." VIX ranges from the low teens to
high 30s. SPX ranges from ~840 to ~1,560. Covered period spans
multiple years from mid-1990s through late 2007. A dramatic VIX spike
appears at the far left (bear market/crash period) while SPX collapsed.
As SPX trended up over subsequent years, VIX trended down to low teens.
A renewed VIX spike appears at far right coinciding with a SPX pullback
in late 2007.

**What the chart teaches:**
- SPX and VIX are **negatively correlated** — when SPX goes down, VIX
  goes up, and vice versa
- **Trading rule:** Look for VIX reversals to confirm market bottoms
- The relationship is strong enough to use as a confirmation signal

**Other volatility indexes to watch:**
- **VXN** — corresponds to the Nasdaq Composite Index (COMPQ)
- **VXO** — corresponds to the S&P 100 (OEX)

> Technical note: When viewing a VIX chart, readings prior to the
> revised SPX methodology are constructed using historic data.

---

### Applying Breadth and Sentiment Tools [MUST KNOW]

**Common logic for most breadth/sentiment indicators:**
- Weakness in the reading = bearish sign
- When the reading becomes **extremely bearish**, it becomes **bullish**
  (contrarian logic)

Notable exception: the CBOE index-only P:C ratio (does NOT follow
contrarian logic in the same way)

**Practical guidelines:**
- Exchanges, trading platforms, and market products change over time →
  the nature of extremes for different indicators can also change
- Don't use rigid set-in-stone values; combine **qualitative and
  quantitative assessment** periodically
- Use **2 or 3 option sentiment tools** at most — not 5 or 6. More is
  not better; redundancy causes "indicator burnout"
- Backtest different combinations to find what works best for you
- Use tools that provide **different or complementary information** to
  maximize time efficiency

> TIP: To calculate standard deviation for a column of data in Excel,
> use the formula: `=stdev(data)`

---

### Locating Neutral Areas for Indicators [SHOULD KNOW]

- It might seem that **1.0 would be neutral** for a put/call ratio, but
  it doesn't work that way
- Under normal conditions the ratio will be **less than 1.0** because
  markets typically move upward, reflecting a bullish bias

**Average ±SD approach for finding neutrals and extremes:**
- Requires a data set representative of the series
- Risk: moving too far forward in time means applying outdated
  conditions to current conditions
- Consider calculating values for clearly defined bullish and bearish
  periods to see if the indicator behaves differently in each

> Technical note: Stock and index price data is **not normally
> distributed**, so commonly used statistical rules for this data won't
> apply. You need to use returns.

- By calculating mean + SD, you create bands around the central value
  to identify a region where most data falls
- Points **outside that area** are atypical — these are times when
  emotions are running high and decisions are far from cool and calm

---

### Identifying Indicator Extremes [SHOULD KNOW]

**Statistical tests applicable to sentiment data:**
- Mean and median calculations for central tendencies
- Standard deviation calculations to identify extreme levels

- Use **mean** in most cases; switch to **median** if one or two very
  extreme outliers skew the results (when outliers are present, mean
  and median will be far apart)

**Applying to moving averages:**
- You can apply mean/SD calculations to smoothed segments of the data
  (moving averages) rather than raw data
- Keep the MA period **short** so you don't over-smooth values and miss
  important turning points — the goal is to view extreme behavior, not
  obscure it

> An **outlier** is a data point that is a good distance away from
> other data points in the set and cannot be dismissed.

- When applying SD lines, use **multiples of 1, 2, and 3** to see what
  extremes are highlighted
- It may be necessary to use **one multiple for high-end spikes** and
  a **different multiple for low-end extremes**, depending on how fast
  the indicator values respond to market changes

---

### Figure 5-6: Advance-Decline Ratio with NYA [SHOULD KNOW]

[CHART: chart_p111_01.png]
Dual-line chart titled "Advance-Decline Ratio." Upper area: NYSE
Composite Index (NYA, thick dark line) ranging from approximately 8,500
to 10,500. Lower area: A-D Ratio (light gray jagged line) oscillating
near 0, with occasional upward spikes, centered around a mean near 1.0.
Three horizontal reference lines: average line, +1SD line, and -1SD
line near the bottom of the chart. Time range: 1/5/2007 to 11/17/2007.
Two vertical lines are drawn (one darker, one lighter) to highlight lag
between A-D ratio spikes and market bottoms.

**What the chart teaches:**
- The A-D ratio oscillates around **1.0** (reached when advancers on
  the NYA = decliners)
- Extreme high values for the A-D ratio **lag behind P:C ratio
  extremes** — put buying reflects pending doom while breadth
  improvement happens only after a rally is underway
- **Upward spikes confirm the reversal after the fact** (lagging
  confirmation, not leading signal)
- The **darker vertical line** (earlier) captures strong fear and
  coincides with the short-term market low — this low extreme is more
  timely than the high extreme
- **Trading rule:** For A-D ratio, low extremes (extreme fear) are more
  actionable; the upward spikes merely confirm a reversal has occurred

> REMEMBER: When an indicator **diverges** from the index or stock
> you're evaluating, it may be an early warning of trend trouble.

---

## Chapter 6: Sector Analysis: Technical and Fundamental

**In this chapter:**
- Understanding chart advantages
- Selecting sectors with strength
- Projecting price movement

In an optimally functioning market, broad advances include most sectors
and stocks. During downtrends, indexes, sectors, and stocks tend to
fall. Exceptions exist — sometimes economic conditions favor one group
over another for a period of time, and as conditions change, so do the
sectors displaying strength or weakness.

Focusing on **strong or weak sectors** lets you tailor options
strategies to prevailing market conditions. **Technical analysis**
provides tools for identifying relative strength and weakness.

---

### Getting Technical with Charts

**Chart analysis** focuses on visual cues to identify price and volume
information that leads to dominant trends in the market. The best tools
are the most basic, reliable techniques.

### Chart Basics [MUST KNOW]

Charts, more formally known as **price charts**, are visual records of
price activity — pictures formed by the periodic plotting of price data
over time (days, weeks, years, or minutes).

**Types of charts:**

**Line chart:**
- Documents price movement versus time
- A single price data point (dot) per period connected by a line
- Typically plots **closing price** values (considered most important
  for the period)
- Easy to interpret; great "big picture" view of trends by filtering
  out minor intra-period noise
- **Disadvantages:** No information about trading strength during the
  day, or whether price gaps occurred period-to-period
- A **price gap** is created when trading for one period is completely
  above or below trading for the previous period — happens when
  significant news comes out when markets are closed

**Open-high-low-close (OHLC) bar chart:** [MUST KNOW]
- Pictures price versus time
- Trading range (low to high) displayed as a **vertical line**
- Opening price as a **horizontal tab on the left** of the range bar
- Closing price as a **horizontal tab on the right** of the range bar
- Four price points per bar: Open, High, Low, Close
- More complete and useful than line charts — provides information
  about **trading period strength and price gaps**
- A relatively long vertical bar = big trading range for the day =
  high volatility = important signal for option traders
- Closes **near the high** = strength; closes **near the low** =
  weakness

**Candlestick chart:** [MUST KNOW]
- Most commonly used charts by professional traders
- Similar to OHLC — price range displayed between open and close
  highlighted by a **thickened bar (body)**
- Patterns unique to candlesticks can enhance daily analysis
- Distinct pattern interpretations describe the battle between bulls
  and bears
- Best applied to a **daily chart**
- Also display price ranges and gaps

**Best practice:** Use **both**:
- Longer-term **line charts** for noting price trends (big picture)
- **OHLC or candlestick charts** for better understanding of price
  action during the period, including security strength and volatility

> TIP: Many technical charting packages are available as independent
> software programs or web-based applications. Cost ranges from free
> to thousands of dollars. Start with a free web-based package; your
> online broker will often have a "home" charting program available.

---

### Adjusting Your Time Horizon for the Best View [MUST KNOW]

Your **timeframe** = your investment or trading horizon. What you want
to view for a 401(k) is different from active trading focus.

**Key principle:** Longer-term trends are considered **stronger** than
shorter-term ones.

When evaluating charts, use **multiple time intervals**:
- **Long-term major trends** — monthly charts on indexes and sectors
- **Intermediate-term major and minor trends** — weekly charts on broad
  market indexes and sectors
- **Short-term minor trends** — daily charts on sectors

By first recognizing major and intermediate trends, you're less likely
to get caught up in emotion associated with shorter-term moves.

---

### Visualizing Supply and Demand [MUST KNOW]

Charts are visual displays of supply and demand:
- **Buying demand** pushes prices upward
- **Supply** creates selling pressure that drives prices downward
- **Volume** displays the magnitude of supply or demand

Price variations are a direct result of the constant and dynamic battle
between the bulls (demand) and the bears (supply).

---

### Areas of Support and Resistance [MUST KNOW]

**Support:**
- A chart area where **buyers come into a falling trend**
- Represents a transition from declining prices (supply-driven) to
  climbing prices when renewed demand kicks in at that price level

**Resistance:**
- A chart area where **sellers come into a rising trend**
- Represents a transition from climbing prices (demand-driven) to
  declining prices when selling pressure comes in at that price

**Drawing rules:**
- A horizontal **support line** can be drawn after price touches a
  price level **twice**; confirmed when a **third touch** holds and
  buying demand returns, sending price up
- A horizontal **resistance line** can be drawn once price moves up to
  touch a level **twice**; confirmed when a **third touch** holds and
  selling demand returns, sending price down

**Key properties:**
- The **longer** a price level serves as support or resistance, the
  **stronger** it's considered
- Transitions line up over time, sometimes creating **sideways trading
  channels** as price moves between the two
- Price areas that previously served as support **often serve as
  resistance** in the future and vice versa

**How to use support and resistance in trading:**
- Useful for identifying **entry and exit points**
- Use in **price projections** to identify stop-loss and profit-taking
  exits
- Useful for **calculating risk-reward ratios**
- When prices rise **above resistance** or fall **below support** →
  signal that a new price trend may be on the way

---

### Trend Analysis [MUST KNOW]

**Upward trend:**
- Prices climb and pull back such that a rising line can be drawn
  **under the pullbacks**, which display **higher lows**
- Higher highs are also characteristic

**Downward trend:**
- Prices fall and retrace such that a declining line can be drawn
  **above the tops of retracement peaks**, which display **lower highs**
- Lower lows are also characteristic

**Drawing trend lines:**
- Connect **two higher lows** (uptrend) or **two lower highs**
  (downtrend)
- When price **successfully tests the line a third time**, the trend is
  confirmed
- Use these lines as **entry and exit points** (same technique as
  support/resistance levels)

> TIP: Consider drawing **two trend lines** using a longer-term chart
> (e.g., monthly): one using closing data, one using market lows. This
> highlights a **trading channel** — a nice visual record of trading
> activity that lets you plan entry/exit points and monitor open
> positions.

---

### Moving Averages [MUST KNOW]

**Moving averages** are lines constructed on a chart using an average
value of closing prices over a certain number of days. They are
considered **lagging indicators** because historical data follows
price action.

**Two main types:**

**Simple moving average (SMA):**
- Uses a basic average calculation
- Equally weights all closes for the selected time period

**Exponential moving average (EMA):**
- Incorporates all available price data
- Provides **greater weight to more recent data**
- More responsive to price changes; more sensitive for trading
  decisions

Both SMA and EMA can be built using a variety of settings and chart
intervals (e.g., 5-day SMA on a daily chart; 10-week EMA on a weekly
chart). Moving averages are considered **unbiased trend indicators**
because lines are derived from objective calculations.

**Standard moving average settings:** [MUST KNOW]
- **20-day MA** — short-term trends
- **50-day MA** — intermediate-term trends
- **200-day MA** — long-term trends

A break of the **200-day moving average** is considered significant
and may confirm a **trend reversal**. Other popular averages: 10-, 30-,
100-day. Some stocks are better analyzed with one MA vs. another —
try different settings.

> TIP: EMAs incorporate all available price data, with more recent data
> weighted more heavily. As a result, they are **more responsive** to
> price changes and can be considered more sensitive when making trading
> decisions.

---

### Identifying Relatively Strong Sectors [MUST KNOW]

- Major market moves up or down generally result in gains or losses for
  most sectors and securities
- During **more moderate trending**, certain sectors perform better
  than the market while others perform worse — some may move in the
  **opposite direction**
- Objective: find **relatively strong and weak groups** to apply
  profitable sector strategies

<!-- pp. 121-140 -->

## Chapter 6: Sector and Volatility Analysis (continued)

### Relative Ratios [MUST KNOW]

- A **relative ratio line** is constructed by dividing one security's price into another
- The line rises when the primary security **outperforms** the second, falls when it **underperforms**
- Adding an **overlay chart** to a relative ratio allows viewing both securities on one chart
- **Log scales** typically provide a better view of movement for each
- Relative ratio lines are also called **relative strength comparisons**

> TIP: Trend lines drawn on a log chart will appear differently when you switch to an arithmetic scale.

#### Figure 6-1: XLF vs. SPY Weekly Relative Ratio Chart

[PAGE IMAGE: page_122.png]
This is a weekly log chart for **XLF** (Financial Select Sector SPDR Fund, dark solid line) overlaid with **SPY** (S&P 500 Index ETF, lighter thinner line). Two dashed lines represent the **10-week EMA** (intermediate trend) and **40-week EMA** (long-term trend) for XLF. The bottom panel displays the **relative ratio line for XLF/SPY**.

- **What the chart shows:** At first glance, XLF and SPY appear to track similar price trends, but the relative ratio tells a different story — SPY **outperformed XLF** throughout much of the three-year period shown (relative ratio line in a general downtrend)
- **Key period:** June 2007 through October 2007 — SPY significantly outperformed XLF, preceding the **first down leg of the U.S. market** related to the subprime mortgage crisis
- **Practical signal:** Within a month after the deterioration in the relative ratio line, XLF dropped below its **200-day EMA**, with the 50-day EMA crossing below shortly after
- **EMA identification tip:** The shorter EMA moves more closely with price (more jagged); in a downtrend, price data appears lowest, followed by the shorter EMA, then the longer EMA

> REMEMBER: Shorter moving averages measure a shorter time period and move more closely with price — used to smooth out shorter-term trends. They are identified on charts because they are somewhat jagged.

#### Trading Rules from Relative Ratios [MUST KNOW]

- **Moving average crossovers** are better used as **trend confirmation**, not as timing tools — note that price is near its lowest when the cross occurs
- **Relative strength lines** can serve as **leading indicators**; moving averages confirm
- Once a **negative EMA cross** occurs (shorter EMA below longer EMA), favor bearish strategies
- Trend lines can be applied to relative ratio lines using the same rules:
  - Draw **uptrends** using the **low points** in the trend
  - Draw **downtrends** using the **high points** in the trend
- Previous areas of support can become resistance and vice versa

> TIP: When using overlay capabilities on a chart, indicators added are based on the primary security.

#### Sector Rotation and ETF Strategy [MUST KNOW]

- **Sector rotation**: cash flows from one outperforming market/sector to another as economic conditions and perceptions change; portfolio allocations should favor outperforming sectors
- ETFs tracking different asset classes (stocks, bonds, U.S. dollar, oil) enable an **asset allocation plan across markets** using a single security type
- Options on many ETFs provide **reduced-risk access** to commodity and foreign exchange markets
- Practical approach: use one optionable **ETF fund family sector group** (e.g., the ten **Select Sector S&P Depository Receipts / SPDRs**) to evaluate and compare multiple sector trends
  - **SPY** tracks the entire S&P 500 Index
  - Ten other individual ETFs each track one of the nine major sectors that make up the index
- Collectively, 11 charts give a **complete broad market and sector assessment**

> TIP: Trend lines can be used on relative strength comparison lines to better identify changing conditions and areas of support and resistance. Support that has been broken will often serve as resistance.

> REMEMBER: A relative ratio line only compares performance of two securities — it does NOT indicate the trend for either security. A rising line means the primary security is trending upward FASTER or trending downward SLOWER than the second.

---

### Rate of Change Indicator [MUST KNOW]

- **Relative ratios** give a visual approach; **Rate of Change (ROC)** lets you quantify and rank performance across sectors
- **ROC for a security** = the speed at which it moves; calculating security returns is one type of ROC
- ROC can also be drawn as an indicator on charts for analyzing, trading, or ranking

#### ROC Formula

**10-day ROC:**
> (Today's price ÷ Price 10 Trading Days Ago) × 100

- For the **14-day ROC** used in this chapter: the "14-day" refers to **10 trading sessions** within a 14-calendar-day period
- Practical tip: use **Friday closing values** as your starting point when tracking manually; note holidays may affect cadence

#### Table 6-1: One-Day ROC Sector Rankings (2-17-17) [MUST KNOW]

[PAGE IMAGE: page_126.png]

| ETF  | Sector            | 14-day ROC | Rank |
|------|-------------------|------------|------|
| XLF  | Financials        | 4.84       | 1    |
| XLP  | Consumer staples  | 4.37       | 2    |
| XLI  | Industrials       | 4.14       | 3    |
| XLV  | Healthcare        | 3.56       | 4    |
| XLK  | Technology        | 3.43       | 5    |
| XLU  | Utilities         | 2.57       | 6    |
| XLY  | Consumer cyclicals| 2.29       | 7    |
| XLRE | Real estate       | 0.87       | 8    |
| XLB  | Materials         | −0.15      | 9    |
