**What the spreadsheet shows:**
- As MSFT price falls from ~$29.30 to ~$27.93, call value falls from $1.10 to $0.35
- Put value rises as underlying falls (from $1.61 to $2.25)
- **Call options decline as DTE decreases AND as underlying falls** — double headwind
- **Put holds value better** when underlying is falling (directional benefit offsets
  time decay somewhat), but still loses time value
- Option prices do not change by the exact amounts projected by the Greeks —
  the Greeks are approximations [MUST KNOW]

---

### Changing Volatility and Option Prices [MUST KNOW]

Volatility impact on option prices is challenging because **implied volatility (IV)**
is an expression of an option's *expected* volatility in the future.

> REMEMBER: Keep volatility terminology straight:
> - **Historical volatility** (HV / SV = Statistical Volatility) = a measure of the
>   underlying stock's *actual past* price movement
> - **Implied volatility** (IV) = a measure of the underlying stock's *expected future*
>   price movement, derived from option prices [MUST KNOW]

---

### Getting a Grip on Volatility [MUST KNOW]

- Historical volatility can be a predictor of future/implied volatility
- A major factor in actual stock volatility is how the prices of options affect it
  (circular relationship — options price in expected moves which then influence actual
  behavior)

---

### Implied Volatility (IV) [MUST KNOW]

**Definition:** IV is the volatility in the stock's price *implied* by the option's
price. It is a measure of expected future stock price movement, derived from current
option prices.

**Key rules of thumb for IV:**
- **High IV** (as measured by a stock's 52-week high-low range of IV) → correctly
  predicts large future price movements
- **Low IV** (as measured by a stock's 52-week high-low range of IV) → correctly
  predicts small future price movements
- Periods of **low IV + technical analysis + event timing** → can offer excellent
  **entry points** into options
- Extended periods of **high IV** → can often offer excellent **exit points** [MUST KNOW]

> TIP: A volatility of 30% for a stock priced at $100 means you may see the stock
> trade between $70 and $130 over the next year. The actual price movement is dependent
> upon one standard deviation of the stock's price, which when normally distributed
> is 68.2%. [MUST KNOW]

**Practical note on high IV environments:**
- When holding a long-term stock position and put IV is high, you should NOT throw
  caution to the wind
- Increasing IV often translates to **increasing fear in the market**
- When faced with buying options in a high-volatility environment, you may need to
  evaluate a broader range of expiration months and strike prices [SHOULD KNOW]

---

### IV Crush [MUST KNOW]

> TECHNICAL STUFF: When IV is relatively high and then drops significantly, it's
> referred to as an **IV crush**. This is usually event-driven — a response to news
> such as earnings reports, a major change in company leadership, or product launches.
> When the event becomes reality, it is no longer an uncertainty, which reduces its
> effect on option prices.
>
> Warning: Selling high IV options in hopes of an IV crush is a **dangerous strategy**
> because the news itself may have caused a significant price move in the stock —
> so the option value could move significantly due to actual stock price movement,
> even though IV declines after the news event. [MUST KNOW]

**How IV varies:**

1. **By time to expiration:**
   - Due to inherent pricing model design
   - A higher IV in one expiration means the stock is likely to move by a greater
     percentage during that expiration than other expirations
   - This affects option values of different strike prices in that expiration

2. **By strike price:**
   - Usually **ATM (at-the-money) IV is the lowest**
   - **Skew charts** (discussed in Chapter 15) provide IV by strike price — can speed
     up option-selection process when IV is relatively high
   - An option's price = **intrinsic value + extrinsic (time) value**
   - Intrinsic value = determined solely by moneyness (strike vs. current stock price)
     — IV does NOT affect intrinsic value
   - The deeper ITM the option, the **less impact IV has** on total premium
   - IV is the primary determinant of **time value** [MUST KNOW]

---

### Time Value Decay — The "Sweet Spot" [MUST KNOW]

> REMEMBER: When using **short option strategies**, time-value works in your favor
> because deep ITM options have little time value. **Selling options with 30 to 45
> days to expiration** accelerates time-value decay for you. This is known as the
> **"sweet-spot" of time-value decay** because it combines:
> - An accelerating rate of decay
> - A sizeable amount of remaining time value that has not yet decayed
>
> Time-value decay is **not a linear constant**. The closer you get to expiration,
> the faster it drops — similar to a waterfall. [MUST KNOW]

---

### Paper Trading a Trading Strategy [MUST KNOW]

**Why paper trade:**
- Continually seeking and implementing new strategies develops trading skills
- Paper trading makes risk-free progress on the new strategy learning curve

> TIP: When paper trading, be sure to incorporate trading costs associated with the
> position to get the best value for strategy profitability.

---

### Trading on Paper: Pluses and Minuses [MUST KNOW]

**Philosophy:** Masters of any craft practice constantly. Options traders are no different.

Paper trading is excellent for:
- Working out a method of analysis
- Developing record-keeping habits
- Learning to respond to the market
- Training yourself to minimize losses as you develop new strategy mechanics
- Making changes to trading routine without real financial pain
- Watching a long OTM option deflate as IV drops — much less painful on paper

**Honest limitation:** Paper trading is not real trading and doesn't fully prepare you
for the battle of greed and fear within — but it forces you to address situations prior
to having real money on the line by laying down **mental grooves** that help accelerate
the brain rewiring process. [MUST KNOW]

---

### Table 7-1: Advantages and Disadvantages of Paper Trading [SHOULD KNOW]

[PAGE IMAGE: page_159.png]

| Advantages                                    | Disadvantages                                         |
|-----------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|
| Provides feedback via profits/losses          | Does not prepare you emotionally for losses           |
| Allows you to incorporate all trade costs     | There are no assignments                              |
| Identifies issues you may not have considered | Typically does not address potential margin problems  |
| Avoids account losses                         | Does not help trade execution understanding           |

**Key gap:** Paper trading doesn't address the emotional component — the fear and greed
that occur when real money is at risk. Real trading introduces assignment risk (for
short options), margin requirements, and execution nuances (bid-ask spreads, fills) that
paper trading cannot fully replicate. [MUST KNOW]

---

### Implementing Electronic Paper Trades [SHOULD KNOW]

- Paper trading can be done on a spreadsheet, electronic platform, or actual paper —
  whatever works best for you
- If building your own log, incorporate the option Greeks too

> TIP: Scientific studies suggest rewiring of the brain is **maximized when you write
> things down on paper**.

**Electronic tracking options:**
- Many financial websites allow entering different positions in a portfolio tracker
  that updates end-of-day or intra-day on a delayed basis
- Warning: not all accept option symbols
- A basic tracker provides: price changes, profits and losses
- A more advanced platform includes: risk chart displays and other trade-management tools

---

### Figure 7-2: Electronic Portfolio Tracker (Optionetics.com) [NICE TO KNOW]

[PAGE IMAGE: page_160.png]
Screenshot of Optionetics.com "My Portfolio" page showing a "Volatility Change"
portfolio tracker. The portfolio displays:
- **MSFT** (shares): Last Price 33.73, Today's Change -1.01 (-2.91%), Current Value $3,373.00
- **MSQ=AK** (Jan 30 call): Last Price 4.35, Today's Change -0.85 (-16.35%)
- **MSQ=MK** (Jan 30 put): Last Price 0.48, Today's Change +0.18 (+60%)
- Portfolio totals: Current Value $3,377.83, Change -$101.67, Simple Return +13.35%

The tracker shows columns: Symbol, Last Price, Today's Change, Comm, Current Value,
Change, Today's % Change, Option Chain link

**What this illustrates:**
- Electronic paper trading shows real-time P&L for option positions
- You can see how calls and puts move in opposite directions with the underlying
- The call lost 16.35% while the put gained 60% on a ~3% move down in MSFT
- This asymmetry is a direct result of delta (puts gain more when stock falls steeply)
- "Simple Return +13.35%" shows the overall portfolio performance metric available
  in tracking tools

---

### Using Trading Systems [MUST KNOW]

**Definition:** A **trading system** is an approach with specific rules for entry and
exit. More rigidly defined than a general systematic approach (like "only buy calls
when IV is low").

**When using a trading system, you must:**
- Establish a position for **all** buy signals generated by the rules
- Exit each position **when the exit signal is generated** (not before, not after)

**Why this discipline matters:** A trading system's historical performance is only valid
if you execute every single signal. Cherry-picking signals invalidates the backtest
results. [MUST KNOW]

<!-- pp. 161-180 -->

## Chapter 7 (continued): Building and Testing a Trading System

### Knowing What You're Getting [MUST KNOW]

- **Trading systems are mechanical** — once designed and tested, there is NO decision-making during implementation; you never debate whether to accept an entry or exit signal
- If the system is frequently producing losses or something seems amiss, completely stop the system
- The two best things about a formal trading system:
  1. It minimizes trade emotions
  2. It allows for backtesting to get a sense of expected performance
- If you start using **discretion** (deciding which trades to take), both advantages disappear: emotions creep in and results diverge from test results
- Building in **flexibility** is common — by varying indicator speeds or adding **filters** (extra rules for entry or exit)
- Indicators and system components are called system **parameters**

**Characteristics of a good trading system:**
- Profitability across a variety of markets, securities, and market conditions
- Outperforming a buy-and-hold approach
- Stability with manageable **drawdowns** (trading losses)
- Diversifying your trading tools
- Suiting your style and time availability

> **TIP:** Getting used to following your new trading system rules can lead to more frequent trading, especially during volatile markets. Factor your time commitments and other potential changes into your daily routine.

> **WARNING:** Be extremely careful about creating a system and putting it on autopilot. Always monitor trades and make adjustments as needed based on timely reviews of the system's performance.

---

### Performing a Backtest [MUST KNOW]

- A **backtest** uses past data to determine whether a system generates stable profits
- Can be done via data downloads, manual trade tracking, or dedicated backtesting software
- Critical: be sure you're testing what you think you're testing
- **Include bullish, bearish, and sideways periods** — this generates results under worst-case conditions and realistic drawdowns
- **Drawdown** = cumulative account losses from consecutive losing trades; evaluating drawdown is a core risk management technique

> **TIP:** A robust trading system can work for a variety of markets (commodities, stocks) under a variety of conditions (bull/bear). At the same time, backtest the system in each individual environment before using it.

**When reviewing backtest results, look for:**
1. **Profitability** — do wins outpace losses?
2. **Stability** — consistency of results; you want profits spread over a variety of trades (not just a handful)

**A stable system:**
- Has winning trades with average profits that exceed the average losses of losing trades
- Has an average system profit close to the **median** system profit (low standard deviation)
- Sustains manageable drawdowns
- Does NOT rely on a handful of trades for profitability

- Note: a system doesn't need more winning trades than losing trades — many trend systems let profits run on fewer trades while cutting losses quickly on losing trades
- The goal is **consistency** — not getting lucky big a few times

> **TIP:** A great way to design systems is to base them on the common traits of your best trades.

**Forward testing:**
- After backtesting, run the rules on a shorter, more recent period (from the latest backtest date forward to just before implementation)
- Expect **diminishing returns** during forward testing
- System trading is NOT a secret key to profits — it is a way to minimize harmful trading emotions and deliver more consistent results

---

### Following the Right Steps — Backtesting Process [MUST KNOW]

The complete 9-step backtesting sequence:

1. Identify basis of strategy (for example, capture trending conditions)
2. Identify trade entry and exit rules
3. Identify market traded and period to backtest
4. Identify account assumptions (system and trade allocations)
5. Test system, evaluate results
6. Identify reasonable filters to minimize losing trades (number and/or size)
7. Add filter based on conclusions from Step 6, test system, then evaluate results
8. Add risk-management component
9. Test system, evaluate results

> **WARNING:** Check the average value of losing trades, as well as maximum and consecutive losses to determine if a system is suitable.

---

### ROC Trending System — Backtest Example [SHOULD KNOW]

**System design:**
- Long-only, **rate of change (ROC)** momentum system
- Entry signal: **SMA crossover** — ROC: 34, SMA: 13
- Exit signal: faster signal — ROC: 21, SMA: 8
- Because exit uses a faster signal, a second parameter required: the 21-day ROC must be HIGHER than its 13-day SMA at time of entry (otherwise the exit signal may never be triggered)
- This is a **trending system** seeking to capitalize on longer-term momentum pushes upward
- A faster momentum signal is used to exit to limit losses and profit erosion

**Backtest parameters:**
- 6-year period including bullish and bearish phases
- Universe: 6 semiconductor stocks including **SMH** (semiconductor ETF)
- Account size: $20,000 with 50% of cash allocated per trade
- Commission: $10 per trade
- No stops in initial test

---

### Figure 7-3: ROC System Sample Trade — Intel Corporation (INTC)

[CHART: chart_p165_02.png]

**Figure 7-3:** ROC trending system sample trade — INTC, 12/30/1999–1/5/2000.

The chart shows **two side-by-side panels for Intel Corporation (INTC)**:
- **Left panel (Entry):** Daily bar chart dated 12/29/1999. Below the price bars, the ROC indicator is shown with two lines (purple/oscillating ROC line and blue/smoother SMA line). The ROC line has crossed above its 13-day SMA, generating the **trade entry signal**.
- **Right panel (Exit):** Daily bar chart dated 1/5/2000. The faster ROC [21, Close, 8] indicator is shown. The ROC line crosses back below its 8-day SMA, generating the **trade exit signal**.
- Price scale runs from ~33 to ~44 on both panels
- ROC oscillator scale shows approximately 0.8 to 1.3

**What the author is teaching:** This dual-panel view shows the mechanical nature of the system — entry and exit are both triggered purely by the ROC indicator crossing its SMA, with no discretion. The position was entered on 12/29 and exited six calendar days later for a **gain of 3%**.

**Trading rule illustrated:** Use the slow ROC/SMA crossover for entry and the fast ROC/SMA crossover for exit. The faster exit signal captures profits and limits erosion when momentum fades.

> **TIP:** A system does not have to be robust or complex to be effective. Because volatility and trending characteristics vary for different securities, some are better suited to certain types of systems. Less-volatile stocks are generally better suited to less-complicated systems geared more toward trend analysis rather than more frequent trading.

---

### Reviewing System Results [MUST KNOW]

- Work **backward** when reviewing system results — evaluating a system with no stops may seem counter-intuitive, but stop levels are somewhat arbitrary (the market doesn't care if you entered at $45)
- Support may be 5% or 10% below entry — allow the system to identify a viable stop-loss point during backtesting and then decide if it represents suitable risk
- For the ROC system test: initial results were favorable on a variety of measures — no filter was added
- **Max Adverse Excursion %** was reviewed to determine if a reasonable stop level could be added; a **15% stop** was included and the system was re-run — results only slightly less favorable, so the stop was incorporated
- Two forward tests were run (with and without the stop) over a 2-year period — the system remained viable but with **much lower profitability** (expected; due to changing conditions and inefficiencies getting worked out of markets)
- This is why you need to **periodically review system performance** and incorporate reasonable stops whenever possible

> **TECHNICAL STUFF:** Charting packages may use different calculations for the same indicator. If changing platforms, compare indicator values that provide signals so you're trading the same system as tested. Always consider re-testing the system on the new platform.

---

### Table 7-2: ROC System Review [SHOULD KNOW]

| System Run | Avg Return | Median Return | Gain:Loss (number) | Avg Gain | Avg Loss | Max Loss |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Test1 | 4.7 | 3.0 | 1.96 | 10.1 | 5.8 | 35% |
| Test2 | 4.6 | 3.0 | 1.91 | 10.0 | 5.9 | 16% |
| FTest1 | 1.6 | 1.0 | 1.56 | 4.0 | 2.3 | 10% |
| FTest2 | 1.6 | 1.0 | 1.56 | 4.1 | 2.3 | 10% |

- **Test1** = initial backtest (no stop); **Test2** = with 15% stop; **FTest1/FTest2** = forward tests
- The 15% stop (Test2) reduced Max Loss from 35% to 16% with only minor reduction in profitability
- Forward tests show profitability drops significantly (avg return ~1.6 vs 4.6-4.7 in backtest) — this is normal and expected
- A lower percentage stop could bring average and median return closer, but since average gains are outpacing average losses (letting profits run), first compare average and median for winning trades and losing trades separately

> **TECHNICAL STUFF:** You can use a standard deviation calculation to assess the stability of profits for any market approach.

---

### Adding Risk Management to a Backtest [MUST KNOW]

- All trade approaches must take risk management into account
- Focus on the **largest adverse moves** for a strategy when identifying stops that still allow the strategy to work
- If adding a stop maintains profitability and stability AND is consistent with your risk tolerance, you can consider implementing the strategy

#### Cutting Losses

- A systematic (but not fully mechanical) approach can still be backtested
- Keep an eye on **large adverse moves** that occurred for the trades generated — this allows you to identify reasonable, systematic filters and stops geared toward minimizing losses

> **WARNING:** A stop-loss order can result in a larger percentage loss by the time a trade is executed. A worst-case scenario occurs when a signal is generated at the close of trading one day and the security has a price gap at the open the next day.

#### Taking Profits

- You may focus so much on risk that you forget to identify favorable **price targets**
- Or you identify a profitable exit point but conditions deteriorate before that level is reached
- **Solution:** In addition to a stop-loss level, identify a **trailing stop percentage or dollar amount** to minimize the number of profitable trades that turn into losses
- The trailing stop should be incorporated into your system and tested
- To generate a trailing amount, evaluate trades with large favorable moves that yielded significantly less in profits (or turned into losses)
- After reviewing, you may:
  - Add a filter that accelerates your exits
  - Generate a trailing percentage using **max favorable excursion %** data

#### Letting Profits Run [MUST KNOW]

- An effective trading approach doesn't necessarily need more winning trades than losing trades — it just needs **profits to outpace declines**
- Many trend-oriented systems end up with more losing trades, but the average loss value is much smaller than the average gain value

> *Be sure to cut your losses while letting profits run.*

> **TIP:** Sorting trades by greatest lost to greatest profit allows you to more easily review statistics for both.

- You must avoid cutting profit levels in such a way that they no longer outpace losses
- Trading successfully requires focusing on three things (in order):
  1. Cutting losses
  2. Preventing profits from turning into losses
  3. Letting profits run

> **TIP:** It's never a bad idea when trading options to set a percentage or dollar gain as a target area in which to take profits. This works best when you own more than one contract. If each contract reaches your target gain — such as 20% — you can sell one or more contracts and let the other(s) ride.

---

## Chapter 7 (continued): Shifting from Knowledge to Mastery

### Strategy Mastery — What It Really Means [MUST KNOW]

- **Strategy mastery** does NOT mean every trade is profitable — it means the appropriate conditions were in place when placing the trade, putting the odds in your favor
- Managing the position correctly (exiting if conditions change) is another component of mastery that highlights discipline
- Strategy mastery can take **years** to evolve
- Goals: stay in the trading game long enough to achieve mastery; constantly adjust your system based on past mistakes and successes
- The best trades are often the ones that take the longest to find — look for the right setup, not the highest trade frequency
- Trading is "hours of boredom combined with minutes of sheer panic or pleasure"

> **TIP:** By focusing first on basic concepts and mechanics, you create a strong foundation that allows you to grasp advanced techniques more quickly. Implement new strategies via paper trading to avoid the most costly mistakes. When ready to go live, further minimize mistakes by reducing position size and remembering to take profits. This keeps you in the markets longer.

---

### Setting the Right Pace [SHOULD KNOW]

- Start by paper trading a couple of the more straightforward approaches (buying puts and calls) and then transition to live trading
- After that, explore strategies with the most appeal — paper trade them first
- No guarantee market conditions will be conducive to a particular strategy, so prolong paper trading days until conditions change or you're ready to explore a new strategy
- **Key principle:** Focus on strategies that make sense to you and suit your style — that's how you develop mastery

#### Starting with a Few Strategies

- If new to option trading: stick with **one or two basic strategies** to develop a solid understanding of premium changes and mechanics
- Experienced option traders: identify current market conditions and explore one or two strategies that excel given those conditions; start by paper trading

> **REMEMBER:** A variety of strategies allow you to make money in the markets. You'll develop a preferred list of strategies that work for you.

#### Adding Strategies as Market Conditions Change [MUST KNOW]

- Markets are a lifelong pursuit because conditions are always changing — the continuous cycle of bullish/bearish phases means the market is never exactly the same

> **TIP:** Do a strategy checkup if things aren't following the norm. When strategies that typically work well start weakening, take time over the weekend to complete a comprehensive market assessment — you may detect early signs of a change in conditions.

**Option strategies matched to market condition and volatility:**

- **Bullish, low volatility** → basic long call, married puts: Buy at-the-money call option, buy out-of-the-money married put, buy at-the-money call debit spread
- **Bullish, high volatility** → covered calls, credit spreads: Buy at-the-money call option, buy out-of-the-money married put, buy at-the-money call debit spread (see Ch. 11)
- **Bearish, low volatility** → basic long put, debit spreads: Buy at-the-money put option, buy out-of-the-money put calendar spread, buy at-the-money put debit spread (see Ch. 11)
- **Bearish, high volatility** → credit spreads: Sell at-the-money call credit spread, sell out-of-the-money naked call (see Ch. 11)
- **Range-bound, high volatility** → butterfly, condor: Sell two short options of the same type combined with one long lower strike price option and one long higher strike price option (see Ch. 16)
- **Range-bound, low volatility** → calendar spreads, diagonal spreads: At-the-money calendar spreads, at-the-money diagonal spread (long option later expiration than short option — see Ch. 12)

- The biggest influence on the strategies you choose is your **financial goal** — some strategies are best suited for capital gains, some for income, others to hedge the risk of a stock or ETF portfolio
- The best approach for you is the one that **intuitively makes the most sense** — when conditions change and things get tougher (and they will), you need the confidence to stick to your plan

---

### Achieving Mastery Through Longevity [MUST KNOW]

- **Longevity** = developing patience and staying power; showing up to work every day; maintaining a consistent routine that accommodates inevitable changes in volatility and market trends
- Bull markets can run for years; volatility conditions can remain stable — but things can change in a hurry
- Expect to incur additional losses when markets transition or when implementing a new strategy
- Managing risk by using **limited-loss, unlimited-gain strategies** whenever possible sets a foundation for longevity
- Paper trading minimizes learning curve losses
- **Proper position sizing** — starting with smaller initial positions makes losses manageable
- Adding profit-taking rules is "the icing on the cake"

> **REMEMBER:** Successful trading does not happen overnight. Be prepared to spend time making low-cost mistakes, observing different market conditions, experiencing varying levels of emotion, and developing your trading skills.

#### Determining Appropriate Trade Sizes [SHOULD KNOW]

- Two easily incorporated position-sizing methods:
  1. Identifying a **maximum dollar amount** allocated per trade
  2. Identifying a **maximum percentage amount** allocated per trade
- The percentage method automatically adjusts as your account size changes
- Some markets may be better suited to the fixed dollar approach
- Because options represent a **leveraged position**, you do NOT need to allocate the same amount of money to options as you would to stocks — and it's probably not a good idea to do so
- Starting point: identify an option position that controls the same amount of stock as your stock allocation, then use that as a baseline for your option allocation — test and review it
- **Establish trade allocation amounts BEFORE analyzing a specific trade** — you need to know in advance the maximum amount available for an individual trade to minimize account risk
- When trying a new strategy (after paper trading), **further reduce trade sizes** so mistakes are more forgiving — even if that means trading one-option contract sizes
- As skills develop, increase position sizes toward tested allocations — this also improves profits because option trading costs are a higher percentage of smaller positions (economies of scale with trading costs)

#### Emphasizing Profit-Taking [MUST KNOW]

- It's not enough to have a high number of profitable trades — your profits must:
  - Exceed trading costs
  - Exceed conservative investment approaches (i.e., beat buy-and-hold)
  - Exceed your losses
- This requires a plan that includes reviewing strategy and trade results to put the best profit-taking rules in place
- Such rules should minimize the number of profitable trades that turn into losses AND allow profits to run
- Developing these skills = evolving as a trader

> **REMEMBER:** There are a lot of different price points that can invoke an emotional response while in a trade. Be sure to identify exit points for a loss AS WELL AS exit points for profits.

---

## Chapter 8: Designing a Killer Trading Plan

**Chapter preview:**
- Developing a reliable plan
- Identifying business costs
- Minimizing learning curve costs
- Understanding order execution

- Trading options is a **unique situation** that requires its own management style and specific language
- **No matter what you trade, you are running a business** — developing as a trader means evolving as a business manager
- Understanding costs helps you budget accordingly
- Early in your career: higher education costs and higher learning curve (losses); as you evolve, those costs go down while subscriptions to analysis platforms and data services go up
- **Losses are part of operating expenses** — minimizing them by managing risk is your goal, not eliminating them
- This is done by determining proper trade allocation amounts and maximum loss per trade
- Effectively executing trades is another step toward minimizing losses

---

### Developing a Reliable Plan [MUST KNOW]

- The **actual developing of the plan** itself is crucial — having a big picture AND a detailed set of goals
- Development may be as simple as: "I'd like to make money on a consistent basis"
- Build more detail into the plan once you have the big picture
- Designing a trading plan around **a single concrete purpose** (e.g., making your car payment from trading proceeds) is a good way to measure success
  - If you make your goal: you've been successful
  - If you don't: it signals your expectations are too lofty OR your trading is not compatible with your goal
- Key: have a **reliable and quantifiable** way to measure success or failure beyond traditional measures (yearly percentage gains/losses)

**Plan development guidelines:**

- **Write it down:** A verbal trading plan is no good. Put it in writing and keep it somewhere visible as a reminder of your purpose.
- **Set realistic goals:** If you have a small account, don't set the goal too high. Instead of a car payment, make your goal going out to a casual restaurant once a month.
- **Use the goal as a measuring stick:** If you don't meet your goal but still made money, you're on the right track. Look for ways to improve technique but don't make too many changes without giving yourself a good chance to prove whether you're right or wrong.
- **Keep excellent records of your trades, results, and expenses:** The trades and results will guide the evolution of your plan. The expenses will come in handy during tax time.
- **Let your plan and your goals evolve over time:** If you become a profitable trader, consider transitioning into trading full time or a significant portion of the time.

> **TIP:** In the early days of your trading career you may be able to use some of your expenses and losses to reduce your taxes.

---

### Managing Your Costs [MUST KNOW]

- View trading as a business and manage expenses to minimize their effect on your earnings
- Some costs are higher when you start; many continue throughout your career

**Expense categories:**

- **Education:** Materials, courses, and learning curve costs for new markets and strategies. Will decrease over time but remain ongoing (books, periodicals, continuing to develop new strategies).
  - The largest education cost is your **learning curve** — it declines as you figure out:
    - How to trade under best conditions for each individual strategy
    - How to use options with appropriate liquidity
    - How to develop paper-trading skills
    - How to allocate the appropriate amount to each trade
    - How to effectively enter orders for the best exit
    - How to take profits

- **Analysis costs:** As skills progress and trading generates regular profits, you may add analytical tools (charting platforms, data services). One of the few costs that may **increase** over time. Limit subscriptions — get to know each tool well to maximize its value.

- **Trading costs:** Must account for both **commission** AND **slippage**
  - **Slippage** = the cost associated with the market spread (the difference between the bid and the ask)
  - Good exercise: calculate commission and slippage percentages for different option position sizes (1, 5, 10 contracts) at different price points ($1, $5, $10)

- **Taxes:**
  - Identify what types of trading will be completed in different account types
  - Options trading in **retirement accounts** defers taxes (limited options strategies allowed in retirement accounts — check IRS at www.irs.gov)
  - In non-retirement accounts, pay taxes on profits
  - Establishing certain option positions when you already hold a position in the underlying may trigger a **tax event** — consult your account about option-trading tax considerations
  - Bottom line: cumulative trading costs must outpace a buy-and-hold approach in the long term

- **Margin:** If you borrow from your broker via margin, add monthly margin interest charges
  - Short option positions have margin requirements that can get complicated
  - Main consideration: is the option **covered** or **naked**?
  - If using margin strategies, contact your broker to fully understand all calculations and account requirements — then add these costs to your expense tracking

**Losses** = another trading cost — consider them part of doing business; will likely be higher at first, reduced with time and experience

**Trading plan guidelines to minimize initial costs:**

- **Determining trading allocations:** Identify both your total trading assets and your maximum allocations for different assets and strategies. Stock and ETF trading will require larger allocations than option positions. Consider a maximum allocation amount for new strategies based on paper-trading results.

- **Calculating trade size:** Determine guidelines for maximum position size prior to entering any trade. Divide the option price by an allocation amount below your maximum — don't anticipate using the max allocation.

- **Identifying maximum acceptable loss on trades:** Can be defined as a dollar value or a percentage. A percentage is often preferable because a fixed dollar amount can become disproportionately significant with smaller trades or if your trading assets decrease. Periodically analyze trade results to determine if losses remain at reasonable and sustainable levels.

- **Focusing on entry and exit rules:**
  - Option entries often driven by trending and volatility conditions; may also be time-oriented (positions created prior to specific scheduled events)
  - Option exits can be time-driven (post-event or pre-expiration) or triggered by movement in the underlying security
  - These methods must support risk management and maximum allowable loss

> **WARNING:** Exiting with technical indicators typically does not provide you with a price for use with risk calculations. A maximum loss price should also be identified.

<!-- pp. 181-200 -->

## Chapter 8 (continued): Optimizing Order Execution

### Optimizing Order Execution — Overview [MUST KNOW]

- Successfully trading options means gaining proficiency with order execution
- Key factors in order execution:
  - Understanding order placement rules unique to options
  - Knowing how different order types work
  - Learning how to use combination orders for multi-leg positions
  - Gaining skill using the underlying to identify option exits
  - Recognizing your broker's role in execution quality
- There is a learning curve for executing option trades, but these are mechanical steps
  masurable with practice and paper trading
- TIP: Consider setting up a **separate brokerage account** used just for options trading
  — simplifies record keeping considerably
- TIP: The **ask** (best price available from sellers) is also referred to as the **offer**

---

### Understanding Option Orders [MUST KNOW]

- Options are **not limited** to a certain number of contracts the way stock is constrained
  by its float — contracts are created by the marketplace
- An option is **created** when two traders create a new position (called **opening** a trade)
  — this increases **open interest** for that specific option
- **Open interest** decreases when traders close existing positions
- TIP: **Float** = the number of shares outstanding and available to trade for a stock
- **Open interest** doesn't update on a trade-by-trade basis — it's an **end-of-day
  reconciliation** through the **Options Clearing Corporation (OCC)**
- Because the OCC needs to keep accounting straight, option orders are placed in a
  specific manner — you'll be communicating more information than for stock orders

---

### Knowing Basic Option Order Rules [MUST KNOW]

- Buying or selling options can be done in any order (long or short)
- Choosing long (buy) or short (sell) depends on strategy and your **account's approval level**
- You can't create unlimited-risk short positions until your broker approves you (after
  checking your trading experience/financial situation — "temperature check")
- REMEMBER: The current bid and ask price for a security = its **current market**
- Because contracts are created and retired based on market demand, you must enter orders
  using specific language to support the OCC's end-of-day reconciliation:
  - A new position you're creating = an **opening order**
  - An existing position you're exiting = a **closing order**

**TABLE 8-1: Option Order Entry Process**

| Position   | Entry              | Also | Exit               | Also |
|------------|--------------------|------|--------------------|------|
| Long Call  | Buy Call to Open   | BCO  | Sell Call to Close | SCC  |
| Short Call | Sell Call to Open  | SCO  | Buy Call to Close  | BCC  |

- When **exercising** or getting **assigned** on an option, there is no closing transaction
- Same for options **expiring worthless** — contracts are simply removed from your account
  after the transaction or expiration weekend ends

---

### Reviewing Order Types [MUST KNOW]

- Two categories of order guarantees:
  - **Execution guarantee** — you will get filled, but not at a guaranteed price
  - **Price guarantee** — you will get a specific price, but execution is not guaranteed

**TABLE 8-2: Order Types by Guarantee**

| Order                        | Guarantees |
|------------------------------|------------|
| Market order                 | Execution  |
| Limit order                  | Price      |
| Stop order or stop-loss order| Execution  |
| Stop-limit order             | Price      |

- **Limit orders** are good for entering a position — only establish positions within
  your trading allocations
- If you need to **guarantee an exit**, only a **market order** accomplishes that
- A **stop order** is your risk-management tool for trading with discipline:
  - The stop level triggers a market order if the option trades or moves to that level
  - The stop represents a price less favorable than the current market
  - Typically used to minimize losses when emotions run high
  - Placing a stop order is similar to monitoring and placing a market order when
    certain market conditions are met
- **Stops are superior to stop-limit orders** for managing risk because they guarantee
  execution if the stop condition is met [MUST KNOW]

**Order Duration:**
- Two primary duration periods:
  1. The current trading session or following session if the market is closed
  2. Until cancelled by you or the broker clears the order (possibly 60 days — check
     with your broker)
- Duration identified by adding **day** or **good 'til canceled (GTC)**
- Market orders are good for the day only (since they guarantee execution)

**Canceling and Changing Orders:**
- To cancel: submit a **Cancel Order** → receive a report confirming cancellation
- If the order was already executed, you receive a "too late to cancel" report
- You cannot cancel a market order
- Two ways to change an order:
  1. Cancel the original order, wait for cancellation confirmation, then enter a new order
  2. Submit a **Cancel/Change or Replace Order** — replaces existing order with revised
     qualifiers unless the original was already executed (if so, replacement is canceled)
- Even though electronic order processing is fast, prefer the Change/Cancel approach
  when replacing an order — avoids risk of duplicating an executed order

> "It is absolutely, positively your responsibility to understand order types and how
> they are executed (or not) in the market." — Duarte

- **WARNING: Read the order ticket *carefully* before hitting Execute.** Also read the
  confirmation ticket after execution. Filling in the wrong box on an electronic ticket
  means the trade goes through — you could be in a very risky trade without realizing it

---

### Identifying Option Stop Order Challenges [MUST KNOW]

- Option stop orders have unique complications beyond stock stops
- An option stop order can be **triggered in two ways**:
  1. If a trade is executed at the stop price
  2. If the bid or ask **moves** to the stop price

- **Key difference from stocks:** Because option contract volume is much lower than
  stocks, the **quote frequently is the trigger** for a stop order — not an actual trade
  - Otherwise, hours could pass before an actual trade triggers the stop
  - At that point, who knows where the underlying would be trading and what the
    option quote would be when the market order is triggered

- **Uncertainty in option stop orders:**
  - Due to low volume and the derivative nature of options, you must factor in uncertainty
  - When placing a stop on an option, you use a **maximum risk amount** to target an
    exit price — it's an **estimated** amount
  - The order may be triggered by the option quote; you won't know the spread amount
    in advance when it's triggered

- **Worst-case scenario:** The underlying gaps up or down (against you) at the open →
  order triggered well below your risk target
  - But worse: having no order in place and being left with a position that keeps declining

- **One Cancels Other (OCO) order:** [MUST KNOW]
  - Allows two different active orders for the same position
  - When one executes, the system automatically cancels the other
  - Used to have both a stop-loss order (risk management) and a limit order (profit-taking)
    active simultaneously
  - If your platform supports OCO, use it — it's much safer than having two live orders
  - **Without OCO:** A strong swing could execute BOTH orders — potentially leaving
    you with an unlimited risk position
  - TIP: Too often casual traders used to stocks fail to pay attention to options trades
    and pay dearly for not being vigilant about detail

- **NBBO — National Best Bid-Offer:** The best markets for bidding and asking (offering)
  prices; composite information from the various option exchanges

- **Sell-stop vs. buy-stop mechanics:**
  - A **sell-stop** is triggered when the option trades at or below your stop *or* if
    the ask reaches your stop
  - A **buy-stop** is triggered when the option trades at or above your stop *or* the
    bid reaches your stop
  - Because you sell on the bid and buy on the ask → **account for the bid-ask spread
    when determining an option stop level** [MUST KNOW]

- **Stop order duration for options:** Some option contracts may only allow **day-stop
  orders** — you'll need to enter a new stop order each evening after market closes

> "You are in a different world with options. If you're used to trading stock, don't
> assume option orders work the same exact way. Be sure you know the implications of
> all orders you place." — Duarte (REMEMBER callout)

---

### Entering a New Position [MUST KNOW]

- Option positions can include:
  - Single option contracts
  - Options contracts and stock
  - Multiple option contracts

#### Creating a Single Option Position

A single contract option order requires information about:
1. The **transaction type** (buy or sell)
2. **Position information** (open or close)
3. **Contract specifics** (underlying, month, strike price, and option type)
4. **Order type** (market, limit…)
5. **Order duration** (day, good 'til canceled…)

- REMEMBER: When you have multiple potential orders for different strategies, some orders
  may be filled but others may not — because the bid and ask may move from your stop

- After entering your order, it goes from the broker's system to one of **13 option
  exchanges**; exchanges are linked so your order can be executed on the receiving
  exchange or forwarded to the exchange with the best market
- Some brokers allow you to direct the order to an exchange of your choice — but this
  may or may not offer you the best price

#### Creating a Combination Position [MUST KNOW]

- Combination positions can be entered:
  1. As a **single combined order** — better chance of execution between the bid-ask spread
  2. As **individual orders for each portion** (called **legging in**)
- Applies to both **option-stock combinations** and **option-option combinations**

**Example — Married Put Combination Order:**
- ABC trading at $33.12 by $33.14; ABC Jan 30 Put trading at $1.00 by $1.05
- Enter as a limit order:
  - Buy 100 Shares of ABC
  - Simultaneously Buy to Open 1 ABC Jan 30 Put
  - For a Limit (Net Debit) of **$34.17**, Good for the Day
- REMEMBER: All legs for a combination order will **either be executed or not executed** —
  you cannot get filled on only one portion

**Example — Bull Call Spread Combination Order:**
- ABC $30 Call quotes: Bid $3.10 by Ask $3.30
- ABC $35 Call quotes: Bid $1.00 by Ask $1.05
- Net debit at the quote: buying $30 call at Ask $3.30, selling $35 call at Bid $1.00
  = **$2.30 net debit**
- You can enter at $2.30 or try to reduce cost by shading slightly lower:
  - Buy to Open 1 ABC Mar 30 Call
  - Simultaneously Sell to Open 1 ABC Mar 35 Call
  - For a Limit (Net Debit) of **$2.25**, Good for the Day
- Again: qualifiers are the same for each leg; you can only get filled on both portions

**Spread Order Execution Tips:**
- TIP: **Fill** = another term for order execution
- Exchange traders (market makers) manage risk on single option orders by buying/selling
  the underlying or other options to hedge
- Spread trades are **naturally hedged positions** — appealing to the floor
- When trading spreads:
  - **Debit spread:** Moderately reduce the limit **below** the market
  - **Credit spread:** Moderately increase the limit **above** the market
- REMEMBER: Spreads have high appeal on the trading floor — try to shave a little off
  the market price for these orders
- **Spread orders have higher execution risk** — less automated on exchanges, takes more
  time to receive an execution report
- If executing the spread is more important than shaving price, stay closer to the
  current market — prices can move significantly in the time it takes to receive a
  confirmed cancellation report for an unexecuted order

---

### Executing a Quality Trade [MUST KNOW]

- **Execution quality:** A broker's ability to provide speedy order executions at or
  better than the current market for the security
  - Example: Bid $22.95, Ask $22.98 → your order should be filled at $22.98 or better
    in a timely manner
- Good execution quality is **as important as reasonable commission costs**
- Not all brokers are the same — if you're having significant option-execution problems,
  your broker may not handle many option-trading accounts → consider using a different
  broker for options

#### Fast Markets [MUST KNOW]

- A security is in a **fast market** when very large order volume is flowing to the market
  and it's difficult for the **market maker** or **specialist** to maintain an orderly market
- Characteristics: high volume, delayed quotes and execution reports
- Technology has reduced the number of securities placed in fast markets, but:
  - Standard rules for execution are **waived** during a fast market
  - Prices at which orders are filled may not be close to expectations
  - You could have unexpected losses
- REMEMBER: When a stock goes into a fast market, **so do the options** derived from it
- If you place a market order in a fast market, the trade may execute minutes later
  at a significantly different price — could cost much more than anticipated
- If you must exit in a fast market, consider a **marketable limit order** that provides
  a cushion — movements can occur quickly in both directions
- **The lesson**: no matter what, **managing your risk comes first**

#### Trader-Driven Conditions

- Your trading platform and connectivity affect execution quality
- If time to get a quote and submit an order is lengthy, the delay may be **on your end**,
  not the broker's or exchange's
- A **slow computer or connection** could put you behind the trading curve
- TIP: If you can't upgrade, keep real short-term trading to a minimum and consider using
  **marketable limit orders** instead of market orders to control costs

#### Booked Order

- When an order better than the current bid or ask enters the option market, the exchange
  can fill it or post it as the **current best bid or best ask**
- If posted, the order is considered a **booked order**
- Depth of market at this price may be very small (just one or two contracts)
- **Execution quality rules don't apply** to booked order quotes

#### Electronic Review

- Your broker may have an electronic order review process that delays routing your orders
  to an exchange — this delay can be several minutes
- Only a very small fraction of retail orders are reviewed during any given trading day
- Check with your broker if this appears to be an issue

---

### Exiting an Existing Position [MUST KNOW]

- Review the possible order varieties your broker offers — platforms may offer a wider
  variety of approaches to exit an option position
- In addition to placing an order for the specific option contract, you can place
  **contingent orders** based on movement of the underlying security
- Contingent orders: extremely helpful for protecting your downside, establishing exits
  based on technical levels, and managing risk in volatile markets
- Broker should always welcome your call when clarifying exchange rules or proper order
  entry for their trade platforms
- SEC rules require brokers to provide execution statistics on different orders (primarily
  covers market and marketable limit stock orders, but includes some reporting for options)

---

### Managing Risk with Single Options [MUST KNOW]

Two ways to manage risk when holding a single option position:
1. Using a **stop order on the option itself** (discussed above)
2. Placing a **conditional or contingent order** on the underlying stock

**Conditional / Contingent Orders:**
- **Conditional** or **contingent orders** = those that rely on movement in the underlying
  or an index to trigger an option order
- A variety of criteria can be established on the underlying or index, including:
  - Closing price equal to, greater than, or less than a certain value
  - Intraday price equal to, greater than, or less than a certain value
  - Percentage changes in price
  - Quote levels equal to, greater than, or less than a certain value
- After setting the trigger criteria, you enter the specifics for the option order
  (with standard qualifiers available)

**Key Distinction: Stop Order vs. Contingent Order** [MUST KNOW]
- **Stop order** = active on the **exchange** (visible to the market)
- **Contingent order** = active on your **broker's system** (the market doesn't see it)
- This is an advantage: the less the market knows about your stops and overall strategies,
  the better off you are
- TIP: Advantage of stop orders over contingent orders on the underlying: you can
  **better estimate the trade value** with the option stop order
- Downside of contingent orders: when triggered, you have much less of a handle on
  where the option is trading — you can estimate expected option value using the price
  of the underlying and the option Greeks
- **Best reason to use contingent orders:** They allow you to identify **technical (or
  fundamental) exit points** for the stock — exit the option when conditions in the
  underlying have changed
- Test multiple scenarios through paper trading to find which approach works best for
  your particular market conditions
- TIP: Consider using an **option calculator** to estimate an option's value when a
  contingent order is triggered
- Be aware of all active orders — potential to duplicate them if your platform lacks
  safeguards. Having triggers set above AND below the market simultaneously is dangerous
  → use **OCO (one cancels other)** order type

---

### Exiting a Combination [SHOULD KNOW]

- Combination orders are exited the same way they are created:
  - By **legging out** of the position, or
  - By entering a **combination order** for a credit or debit
- When presenting a hedged position to the floor:
  - Shave a little from the debit, or
  - Add a bit to the credit
- Unless you're very close to expiration, you'll likely get the order executed at the
  more favorable limit

---

### Rolling an Option Position [MUST KNOW]

- **Rolling an option** = closing one position and opening a similar one for the same
  stock; common near expiration as protective or income positions are pushed further out
- Three types of rolls:

**1. Rolling Out** (extending expiration):
- Pushing back the expiration date for a strategy
- Place a combination order similar to any other combination
- Near expiration, you may not be able to get a more favorable execution for the combination
- You pay an extra commission to close the original option (which probably would have
  expired worthless), but you gain some **time value** for the new option sold

**2. Rolling Up** (higher strike):
- Roll up in price to avoid **assignment risk**
- Or to capitalize on **atypically high implied volatility** for a higher strike option

**3. Rolling Down** (lower strike):
- Roll down the strike price to avoid assignment risk
- Or to capitalize on atypically high implied volatility for a lower strike option

**Combination Rolls:**
- Rolling combinations can be combined: roll out AND up, or roll out AND down
- Decision depends on: price of the underlying, your market outlook, and implied
  volatility conditions

> REMEMBER: "Keep it simple. If you have too many moving parts in your trading system,
> you may end up doing a lot of work and going nowhere. Worse, you may miss something
> important and lose a lot of money in a hurry." — Duarte

---

## Part 3: What Every Trader Needs to Know about Options

*(Part divider — pages 197-198)*

**This part covers:**
- Getting to know different option styles
- Protecting your portfolio with options
- Increasing your profit potential and decreasing risk
- Discovering spreads and other combination strategies
- Understanding exchange-traded funds and their strategies

---

## Chapter 9: Getting to Know Different Option Styles

**Chapter topics:**
- Understanding index and index option details
- Distinguishing option styles
- Exercising option rights and meeting obligations
- Knowing what to do for expiration

> "When trading any instrument, it's infinitely more important to know how much you can
> lose than it is to count on profits. And options present you with a unique challenge
> because they are leveraged and they come with an expiration date — which gives you the
> opportunity to manage a security that can be volatile and that eventually 'goes away.'"
> — Duarte

- Chapter focuses on: key points about indexes and index options that impact trading;
  exercise style; assignment issues; what to be wary of going into expiration

---

### Nailing Down Index Options [MUST KNOW]

- **Monthly listed stock options** can generally be grouped together when applying
  strategies or managing a position — their basic features match (same last trading date
  and exercise cutoff time for all monthly stock options)
- **Index options** are slightly more challenging — the same important components
  **can vary by contract** (last trading date, exercise cutoff time)

---

### Getting to the Nitty-Gritty of Indexes [MUST KNOW]

- An **index** = a pool of a singular type of financial instrument (stocks, bonds, or
  commodities) grouped into a single value to track the health of a particular market
  as one entity
- Indexes are one-stop pictures of the general trend of the underlying assets collected
  into a single group
- Offer a way to analyze the general trend of the market in which you're looking to invest

#### Taking a Peek at Popular Indexes

**S&P 500 (SPX):**
- The pros' tool for measuring the trend of the U.S. stock market
- Measures the health of a diverse group of U.S. **large-cap stocks**
- Used by professional money managers and individual investors globally
- Companion ETF: **SPY** — popular trading vehicle; settlement based on **share delivery**
  (unlike index options, which are **cash settled**)

**The Dow Jones Industrial Average:**
- Tracks 30 **blue chip** companies in the U.S. stock market
- Widely followed; often used by mainstream news media
- Excellent for trading via its **Diamonds (DIA) ETF** — despite relatively small
  number of components

**Nasdaq-100 (NDX):**
- Made up of the 100 largest **nonfinancial companies** trading on the Nasdaq exchange
- Primarily **technology industry** stocks — e.g., **Alphabet (GOOGL)** and **Apple (AAPL)**
- Most frequently used to measure the tech sector
- Usually swings up and down more widely than the S&P 500 or the Dow
- Companion ETF: **QQQ**

<!-- pp. 201-220 -->

## Chapter 9: Trading Index Options (continued)

### Shifting to a More Specific Focus — Sector and Specialty Indexes [SHOULD KNOW]

- **Market capitalization** = current stock price × total shares outstanding
  - Market cap sizes: small, mid, and large
- **PHLX Semiconductor Sector Index (SOX):** Tracks semiconductor stocks such as Intel (INTC); made up of 19 different companies in the semiconductor industry — narrower than all-tech indexes
- **Russell 2000 Index:** Tracks stocks that are both small-cap and growth-oriented; expect big moves in both directions because small-cap names take less volume than large-cap names to move price

> Tip: Because an index is made up of a group of stocks, declines in one stock can be offset by increases in another. Indexes tend to be less volatile than individual stocks. But if price change in one stock becomes "contagious," the index could become as volatile as a single stock. Technology and energy stocks often trade "as a block," meaning similar patterns and trends.

---

### Creating Indexes — Three Construction Methods [MUST KNOW]

Not all indexes are created equally. The three ways to construct an index:

1. **Price-weighted:** Favors higher-priced stocks
2. **Market cap-weighted:** Favors higher-cap stocks
3. **Equal dollar-weighted:** Each stock has same impact

**Implications of construction method:**

- **Price-weighted index (e.g., Dow Jones Industrial Average):** A high-priced stock declining causes bigger index moves than a lower-priced stock declining. Example: Boeing (trading ~$175) has more impact on the Dow than Pfizer (trading ~$35)
- **Market-cap-weighted index (e.g., S&P 500 / SPX):** Impacted more by higher market-cap stocks regardless of price. Example: Alphabet/GOOGL (market cap ~$585 billion) moves SPX far more than Xilinx/XLNX (market cap ~$15 billion), even though both are S&P 500 members
- **Equal dollar-weighted index:** All stocks have same impact on index value; requires quarterly rebalancing to prevent any one stock from dominating after large gains

> Technical Note: The best way to obtain specific construction information for an index is by accessing the website of the company that created it. For Dow Jones indexes: www.djindexes.com

---

### Who Creates Which Indexes — Table 9-1 [MUST KNOW]

| Name | Symbol | Company | Generally Used For |
|---|---|---|---|
| S&P 500 | SPX | Standard and Poor's | Trading or hedging a diverse U.S. large-cap stock portfolio |
| S&P Midcap 400 | MID | Standard and Poor's | Trading or hedging a U.S. mid-cap stock portfolio |
| CBOE Volatility Index | VIX | Chicago Board Options Exchange | Trading or hedging a diverse global stock portfolio |
| Financial Times Stock Exchange 100 | UKXM | FTSE Group | The largest 100 stocks on London Stock Exchange |
| Thomson Reuters CRB Index | CRBI | Commodity Research Bureau | Trading or hedging a diverse, commodity portfolio |
| CBOE 30-year Yields | TYX | Chicago Board Options Exchange | Trading focused on U.S. 30-year treasury yields |

---

### Capitalizing on an Index with Options [MUST KNOW]

- Options can be based on indexes, not just individual stocks
- **S&P 500 (SPX)** is one of the most widely traded option series for all stock and index options — very liquid, easy to enter and exit
- Key distinction: **you can't actually own an index**, so you can't deliver it at expiration
- Index options are **cash-settled** — exercise/assignment involves the transfer of cash, not a security
- Cash amount = the **intrinsic value** (moneyness) of the option

---

### Determining Index Option Value [MUST KNOW]

Despite differences between stocks and indexes, index options work similarly to stock options — same calls/puts, different expiration months, different strike prices. Factors that determine the option's value:

1. **Its type (call or put):**
   - Calls increase in value as the index increases in price
   - Puts increase in value when the index price falls

2. **Value of the index level relative to the option's strike price:**
   - Call has intrinsic value when the strike price is BELOW the index level (in-the-money)
   - When index is trading below the call strike price, option only has time value
   - Put has intrinsic value when the index is trading BELOW the put strike price
   - Reverse is true for puts

3. **Time to expiration:**
   - Time passed erodes the option's value (time decay)
   - More time until expiration = greater chance option has value at expiration = higher premium

4. **Historical volatility:**
   - Past performance doesn't directly affect option price — only future expected volatility matters
   - In practice: an index that has made bigger moves in the past will have more expensive options because there is more uncertainty about where it will be at expiration

5. **Volatility expected in the future:**
   - Expected future movement affects option value the same way past movement does (and is partially based on it)
   - Greater potential move = more expensive option

---

### Detailing Index Option Components [SHOULD KNOW]

Main components of an index option are the same as a stock option, with one key difference: an index isn't a physical asset, so settlement is only in cash.

- **Underlying:** Name of the index the option is based on
- **Strike price:** Level that determines where the owner has rights and the seller has obligations
- **Premium:** Total cost of the option based on the current market price and the option multiplier
- **Multiplier:** Number used to determine the total value of the option premium and the cost of the deliverable package
- **Exercise/Assignment value:** Amount credited to the option owner and debited from the option seller; determined by multiplying the strike price by the option multiplier
- **Settlement value:** Index closing value used to determine intrinsic value

> Remember: **Moneyness** = the amount an index closes above a call option strike price, or below a put option strike price. Moneyness is zero for out-of-the-money options at expiration.

**Cash settlement example — SPX closes at 1,523 at June expiration; you own one June 1,520 call:**
- Settlement price is based NOT on the index closing price but on the **opening price of each stock component** as measured by the SET (ticker symbol)
- Short option holder can't deliver the SPX, so they satisfy obligation in cash
- You receive a $300 credit; short contract holder is debited $300

Formula:
```
(Index Settlement Value – Call Strike Price) × Multiplier
(1,523 – 1,520) × 100 = $300
```

---

## Watching Out for Style Risk [MUST KNOW]

An option's **style** primarily refers to the way the contract is exercised — and it also impacts the end of trading for the option. You must know where to look to find an index option's style and how it affects you.

> Warning: If you don't know the style for a particular option, you could end up with an unpleasant surprise — such as missing an opportunity to sell an index call contract before it takes a tumble on the settlement value day.

**Two styles for option contracts traded in U.S. markets:**

- **American style:** Lets you exercise your rights *at any point up to* the exercise cutoff time
- **European style:** Can only exercise your rights on a designated date

---

### American-Style Options [MUST KNOW]

- All stock and ETF options that use stock as the deliverable package are **American style**
- In this book, "option contract" refers to American-style stock options unless stated otherwise
- Determine option's style by checking its **product specification sheet** from the **Options Clearing Corporation (OCC)** or the different option exchanges (www.optionsclearing.com)

**American-style stock option characteristics:**
- Trades from when initiated by the OCC until the last trading day prior to option expiration
- Option expirations can occur on any Friday of the month — first, second, third Friday; if there's a holiday on that Friday, options expire on Thursday (e.g., Good Friday)
- After purchase, contracts can be exercised by the holder at any point during the life of the contract
- Retail brokers have different requirements for submitting exercise instructions — check your broker's specific rules
- In most cases, you can exercise a long contract at least an hour after the close on the last trading day prior to expiration
- After assigned, the option seller must fulfill their obligation by delivering or taking delivery of the option package (usually 100 shares of stock)
- Option seller can "buy to close" the option prior to the close on the last day of trading to offset the position and alleviate the obligation — **unless** the option has been assigned early (American-style options can be assigned at any time)

---

### European-Style Options [MUST KNOW]

- Options that use an index to derive value are *often, but not always*, European-style
- **European-style options have a specified exercise date** — you can't exercise the option prior to that date
- The exercise date also **varies by index** — there's not one common index exercise day each month
- Critical: **Check the product specification sheet prior to trading** European-style index options

**Key dates for European-style index options:**
- **Last trading date:** The last date the contract can be traded is one day prior to expiration
- **Settlement date:** The date (and time) used to determine the index closing value at expiration
- **Exercise date:** The date on which a long contract holder can exercise their rights; exercise is automatic if the option is in-the-money

- Some index options stop trading on a **Thursday** rather than a Friday — you need to know the specifics to properly manage your position

> Remember: Index options settle in cash rather than a physical asset because a trader can't deliver an index. **This cash-settlement approach applies to index options regardless of exercise style.**

**European-style index option characteristics:**
- Trades from when initiated by OCC until the specified **Last Trading Date** (usually last business day prior to option expiration) — check contract specifications
- After purchase, exercise is **automatic** if the option closes in-the-money (no discretion or action required by the option's owner)
- When assigned, the index option seller satisfies their obligation with a **cash settlement** (appropriate amount debited from account)
- Option seller can "buy to close" the option prior to the exercise date and close of trading to offset the position and alleviate the obligation

**Exception — American-style index options:**
- **S&P 100 Index (OEX):** Top 100 stocks in the SPX; an American-style index option — can be exercised at any time during the life of the option
- OEX sits somewhere between the Dow Jones Industrial Average and the S&P 500 in content, but is less universally known
- Other American-style cash-settled index options: **Gold/Silver Index (XAU)** and **Semiconductor Index (SOX)**

> Tip: Indexes are generally less volatile than stocks, and a diverse index such as the S&P 100 is generally less volatile than a sector-oriented index. That's because stocks in the same industry tend to respond the same way to news, pushing the index in one direction. A diverse index won't necessarily do that — some news is bullish for one industry and bearish for another.

---

### SPX Option Specifications (from CBOE) [MUST KNOW]

The **S&P 500 Index (SPX)** is one of the most widely followed indexes; options are offered by the **Chicago Board Options Exchange (CBOE)** as a proprietary product — high-volume, highly liquid, widely used by institutional traders.

Specifications available at www.cboe.com:

- **Underlying symbol:** SPX
- **Multiplier:** 100
- **Expiration date:** Friday morning on third Fridays and Friday afternoon on other Fridays; also Monday and Wednesday afternoons; Saturday following the third Friday of the expiration month
- **Exercise style:** European-style — automatic exercise date
- **Last trading day:** For options expiring on the third Friday, trading usually stops on the business day (usually a Thursday) prior to the day the exercise-settlement value is calculated
- **Settlement of option exercise:** The **exercise-settlement value (SET)** is calculated on the last business day before expiration for options expiring on the third Friday, using the **first reported sales price** for each component stock from the market where the stock is listed. All other Friday expirations, plus Monday and Wednesday expirations, use the **closing index value in the afternoon**
- **Margin:** Check the specification margin rules AND your broker's rules — broker rules may be more stringent
- **Trading hours:** 8:30 a.m. to 3:15 p.m. Central Time

> Warning: Not all European-style options have the same specifications. One may calculate the settlement value using an opening price, whereas another may use closing values from the previous day. **Before trading any index option, first check the specs.**

---

## Exercising Your Options, American Style [MUST KNOW]

**How to exercise:**
- Provide exercise instructions to your broker → broker provides instructions to OCC → OCC randomly assigns a broker with accounts holding the same option short → that broker assigns one of those accounts
- When you own an American-style call or put, you have the right to exercise **at any point up until your broker's exercise cutoff time**
- Exercise by submitting **exercise instructions** to the broker, either by phone or electronically

> Warning: **Do not assume that you have the same exercise cutoff time as your trading partner, a clearing firm, or anyone else.** Always check the specific cutoff time and exercise process with your broker. Leave sufficient time to reach them and provide instructions.

> Key rule: **Early exercise is rarely desirable because it forfeits the option's time value.**

---

### Knowing the Nuts and Bolts [SHOULD KNOW]

Best practice: **Contact your broker ahead of time** to check their exercise process — you want this information in advance.

Before exercising a stock option contract, check:
- **For calls:** Sufficient money in the account to pay for the stock purchased
- **For puts:** Shares are in the account, or you are able (and want) to create a short stock position — this would be prohibited in a retirement account

> Tip: Don't just point and click. Know what you're getting into and how it will affect your account. Even if you can submit exercise instructions electronically, contact your broker directly the first few times.

---

### What You See Is What You Get [SHOULD KNOW]

- Exercise transactions typically appear in your account by the **next trading day**
- **Exercise instructions are final and irrevocable** — after you submit, you cannot cancel
- When you exercise, the option position is reduced by the number of contracts exercised, and a stock buy or sell transaction appears
- Risk: if you exercise your call rights at 10 a.m. and the stock drops dramatically at 2 p.m. that same day, you **cannot cancel** the exercise instructions

> Warning: When you exercise a put and do not have the underlying stock in your account, you create a **short position**. This means a previously limited-risk position is now technically an **unlimited-risk position** because a stock can continue to rise indefinitely. *Be sure you consider the exercise ramifications before submitting instructions.*

---

### To Exercise or Not, That Is the Question [MUST KNOW]

Before exercising, calculate how to maximize profits by checking **two alternatives:**

1. **Exercising the rights** under the contract to buy or sell stock at the strike price
2. **Selling the option** and then buying or selling the stock in the market

> Remember: The option-exercise decision is different from the stock-ownership decision. Consider the most profitable way to execute your transaction in the market — calculate both alternatives.

**Worked example:**
- Own 100 shares of ABC purchased at $23/share
- Also purchased one $21 put for $2.00
- Later: stock is at $27, but ABC is under investigation for accounting fraud
- Stock plummets to $20/share; the 21 put immediately moves to $5.00
- **What to do?**

Author's recommendation: **Sell both the stock and the put**
- Rationale: the stock may rise or fall but will be "tainted for a while"; the put has a nice gain that it would likely lose if the stock rallied briefly
- **Never exercise an option if it still has time value** — sell the option instead to capture time value

Numerical analysis:
- Put gained $3 in price = $300 per contract gain
- Stock lost $700 from best levels ($27 → $20), but only $300 measured from initial investment ($23 → $20)
- Put gains brought the cost of the entire trade back to the initial investment
- This illustrates **risk management through a simple option strategy**

> Tip: Generally, when an option has more than $0.20 time value remaining, selling the option (alternative 2) will result in a credit that exceeds the extra transaction cost, including commissions and bid-ask spreads.

---

## Exercising Your Options the Euro Way [MUST KNOW]

When trading **European-style index options**, you must be well-versed with the contract specifications. The index-settlement process determines option moneyness at expiration.

### Tracking Index Settlement (the SET) [MUST KNOW]

- Stock index values are calculated using a group of stocks — determining option moneyness at expiration is more complicated
- Not all stocks have opening and closing trades at the exact same time; the opening level for an index won't necessarily include the opening price for all components — some prices may include closes from the previous day
- Solution: access the **index settlement value (SET)** — calculated using only opening values
  - SPX options: settlement value ticker = **SET**
  - RUT index: settlement ticker = **RLS**
  - NDX: settlement ticker = **NDS**
- Find the inclusive list of settlement symbols at: www.cboe.com/data/historical-options-data/index-settlement-values

---

### Cashing In with Exercise — European-Style Cash Settlement [MUST KNOW]

- European-style index options are **cash-settled** — no securities change hands; just cash
- Cash amount is determined by **option moneyness × option multiplier**

**Formulas:**
```
Index – Option Strike Price = Call Moneyness
Option Strike Price – Index = Put Moneyness

Option Moneyness × Option Multiplier = Cash Settlement Amount
```

- When moneyness falls below zero, the option has no intrinsic value — no cash changes hands

**Complete worked example — SPX SET = 1523, multiplier = 100, strike = 1520:**

| | Calculation | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Call Moneyness | 1523 – 1520 | 3 |
| Call Exercise Amount | 3 × 100 | $300 Credit |
| Call Assignment Amount | | $300 Debit |
| Put Moneyness | 1520 – 1523 | < 0 |
| Put Exercise Amount | 0 × 100 | $0 Credit |
| Put Assignment Amount | | $0 Debit |

- Because no asset changes hands during this process, your **market risk over the weekend due to good or bad news is nonexistent** — critical advantage over stock option exercise

---

## Satisfying Option Obligations [MUST KNOW]

When you sell an option short, you have an **obligation, not a right**. You have no choice about assignment:
- The only way to avoid assignment is to **purchase the option back** to offset your position
- This applies to both option styles (American and European)

> Remember: Although all stock options are American-style contracts, not all American-style contracts are stock options. There are also **American-style index options that settle in cash**.

---

### American-Style Stock Options — Assignment Mechanics [MUST KNOW]

- Automatic assignments occur at expiration, though American-style options can be assigned **early**
- Assignment is satisfied through the transfer of shares into or out of your account

When assigned on a stock option, two transactions appear:
- **Assigned (ASG):** The short option is removed from your account
- **Buy/Sell:** The stock transfer appears the same as a regular stock order

**Important nuances:**
- You may not have been assigned on ALL contracts — check your positions to see if short contracts remain
- A commission is usually applied to the buy/sell transaction — known as the **assignment fee**, which can be very different from the regular trading commission

**When does early assignment happen?**
- You get assigned when your short option no longer has time value — this occurs when the option becomes **deep in-the-money**
- Time value is greatest at-the-money and falls significantly the further away the stock price moves from the strike price
- **Puts** generally have a better chance of being assigned early (the person exercising gets cash immediately)
- **Calls:** risk of early assignment jumps significantly when an **ex-dividend date** is a day or two away AND the dividend amount is greater than the option's time value that would be forfeited through early exercise
  - For dividends, it's the **ex-dividend date** that matters, not the payment date

> Tip: All options that are in-the-money (ITM) by a penny or more at expiration date get **automatically assigned/exercised**.

---

### Short Put Assignment [MUST KNOW]

- If assigned on a short put, you are now the owner of ABC stock at a cost likely **higher than the current market**
- Decisions to make: keep the assigned shares, or sell them in the market?
- If you didn't have enough money for the transaction, options:
  - Bring in more cash before stock settlement
  - Buy shares **on margin** (borrowing money from your broker — Duarte strongly discourages this)
- Margin availability depends on: cost and margin requirements for the stock, cash in your account before assignment, and your broker's rules and rates

> Warning: Margin is dangerous and should be avoided if at all possible. When buying on margin, your expected returns should exceed the risk-free rate (U.S. Treasuries) plus the interest rate charged by your broker for using margin. Because the market value of an assigned position is likely below what you paid, you need to consider cutting your losses and selling the position.

---

### Short Call Assignment [MUST KNOW]

- If you already owned the stock (covered call): it simply gets sold at the option strike price, which is **likely below the current market price**
- If you did NOT own the stock: assignment of the short call results in a **short stock position**
  - Exposes you to significant risk because the stock can keep going up
  - Your broker may not have access to shares for lending — in that case, the stock may be bought at whatever price it's trading in the market to close the short position, with or without your knowledge

> Warning: The only thing worse than shares bought back without your knowledge is being short the stock **without knowing it**. Always monitor your accounts regularly when holding short option or stock positions. Beware of conditions that may trigger assignments: deep in-the-money options or news events that significantly impact the underlying.

> Warning: To create a short position, your broker must go out and borrow the shares. These may or may not be available. As a result, even if you have enough money and want to hold a short position, it may not be a viable alternative.

---

### Expiring Options [MUST KNOW]

If you actively manage your account, expiration will typically come and go without incident. But you should know what to expect when holding a position into expiration weekend.

**Long option positions — what to consider as expiration nears:**

- If the option is **out-of-the-money (OTM)** or roughly **at-the-money (ATM)** and you don't want to exercise: try to sell it when the credit you receive exceeds your commission for the transaction
- If you want to buy or sell the stock: calculate whether selling the contract or exercising it is more cost-effective
  - Check if time value still exists: if yes, **sell the option**; if no, **exercise**
  - Exception: when an ex-dividend date is involved and the dividend is larger than the time value

- Never assume a slightly OTM or ATM option will expire worthless — it's possible for the stock's last trade to get reported late, resulting in an ITM option
- Even if the stock closes exactly at the strike price or is OTM, **monitor the news after the close** — you may decide to exercise if you expect a big change in the company's value over the weekend

---

### Exercise by Exception (Auto-Exercise) [MUST KNOW]

- Currently, when a stock or index option is **ITM by $0.01 or more** at expiration, the OCC assumes you did not want an option with value to expire worthless
- The OCC **exercises it on your behalf** over expiration weekend — even without specific instructions from you
- Stock shares are bought or sold for you automatically
- **This may create a short position in your account without your knowledge** — be aware of this risk when holding near-the-money short options into expiration

<!-- pp. 221-240 -->

## Chapter 9 (continued): Managing Risk at Expiration

### Short Option Positions [MUST KNOW]

- **Short option positions** obligate you to buy or sell shares of the underlying stock at
  expiration — you are reactive, not active
- More potential capital risk than long positions; **short options are usually assigned when
  market conditions are against you**
- The only way to prevent assignment is to exit the position before the market closes on the
  last trading day
- Broker may force liquidation if holding an ITM short option exceeds your account's margin
  capacity

> "Monitor news after the close to see if you can benefit from exercising expiring
> out-of-the-money options." — Duarte (Tip callout, p. 221)

### Reasons NOT to Auto-Exercise (Long Positions) [SHOULD KNOW]

You can instruct your broker to not allow auto-exercise for specific contracts (before their
cutoff). Reasons:
- The exercise fee is greater than the in-the-money amount
- News after the close moves the stock adversely, wiping out the ITM close
- You don't want to risk owning stock over the weekend (gap-down risk on Monday)

- **OCC trigger level** for auto-exercise: ITM by $0.01 or more (these thresholds have
  declined over the years)

### Table 9-2: Stock Option Expiration Summary [MUST KNOW]

| Option Type | Typical Action | What You Should Consider |
|---|---|---|
| Long: OTM | Expires worthless | If OTM has value on last trading day, close it for a credit > commission. If hovering near strike and OTM is in doubt, sell the option prior to expiration to avoid auto-exercise and stock exposure over weekend. |
| Long: ATM | ?? | Similar to OTM. If stock hovers around strike and OTM is in doubt, sell prior to expiration. If ATM, sell for same reasons. |
| Short: OTM | Expires worthless | Monitor news and account after close. Even when option closes OTM, anyone can choose to exercise, resulting in assignment for you. If hovering near strike, buy back prior to expiration. |
| Short: ATM | ?? | Monitor news and account after close. Once ITM by $0.01, assignment risk is automatic. If hovering, close short position on last day of trading. |
| Short: ITM | Assigned | Auto-exercise occurs when ITM > $0.01. Close short position on last day if you want to avoid assignment. |

> "Consider your best alternative and plan ahead. Whether you hold a long or short option
> position, it's best to actively manage the position. This usually means exiting it before
> the close on the last trading day." — Duarte (Remember callout, p. 223)

- After a short option closes even a penny ITM, expect assignment — at $0.01 ITM or more,
  auto-exercise kicks in for long contract holders
- When assignment creates a new position, two choices: (1) exit Monday morning, or (2) hold
  if you have sufficient funds
- Broker may not have shares available for a short stock position — exiting may be your only
  choice

### European-Style Options at Expiration [SHOULD KNOW]

- **European-style options** advantage for short contract holders: no early assignment risk,
  no share delivery concern
- Completely avoid assignment by buying the option back at any time prior to expiration to
  offset the short position
- If the option is ITM at expiration, your account will be **debited the difference between
  the settlement value and the strike price**
- For European-style index options: know the option's last trading day and how settlement
  value is determined before trading

> **WARNING:** There may be a period of time when news affects an index settlement. If you
> are short OTM SPX puts at the end of trading on Thursday, bad news overnight could result
> in a strong drop in the morning when the SET is determined. This may result in OTM puts
> becoming ITM.

---

## Breaking It Down: American-Style Index Options [SHOULD KNOW]

- Most popular American-style index option: **S&P 100 Index (OEX)** contract
- Can exercise rights anytime (long) or be assigned anytime (short)
- American-style index options **settle in cash** — an index can't be bought or sold

### OEX Settlement Rules (CBOE specification)

- **OEX SET at expiration:** Uses the last (closing) reported sales price of each component
  stock in the index on the last business day before expiration
- **OEX settlement for early exercise:** Uses the last closing reported primary market sales
  price of each component stock **on the day the exercise instructions are submitted**

### Exercising Rights (American-Style Index, Long) [SHOULD KNOW]

- Must know exercise cutoff times for **OEX, XAU, SOX** — settlement value may be determined
  by opening (SOX) or closing values (OEX and XAU)
- Consider time value when deciding whether to sell the index option in the market or exercise
- Credit amount = determined by strike price + index settlement value + multiplier
- One reason to exercise early even with time value remaining: **news event after 4:00 p.m.
  Eastern** that could impact index value by more than remaining time value

### Meeting Obligations (American-Style Index, Short) [SHOULD KNOW]

- As short contract holder, your only decision is whether to buy back to avoid assignment
- **Early assignment is possible every day you hold the position** — not just the last trading
  day; consider this daily
- If assigned, obligation met in cash using: strike price + index settlement value + multiplier

> "Always, *always* check contract specifications prior to trading both European-style and
> American-style index options. Then plan accordingly, pay close attention to the markets
> and the news, and stay ahead of the game." — Duarte (Remember callout, p. 225-226)

---

## Chapter 10: Protecting Your Portfolio with Options

**Chapter preview:** Protective strategies using options for both portfolios and trading
positions. Also covers **adjusted options** — contracts with non-standard deliverable packages
due to corporate actions — which add risk even to conservative protective strategies.

- Options are described as "the perfect trading vehicle for uncertain times" — providing
  protection for portfolios and trading positions under any market condition
- Key principle: have contingency plans ready before situations arise

---

## Putting Protection on Long Stock [MUST KNOW]

### Combining Puts with Long Stock

- **Married put:** stock and put purchased together simultaneously
- **Protective put:** stock and put purchased separately (different timing)
- The two positions are functionally identical; book uses "protective put" throughout
- Ratio: one long put for every 100 shares of stock held

> "A purchased put option gives you the right, but not the obligation, to sell the underlying
> stock at the contract strike price until the expiration date. You can also sell this put
> option right in the market up until this time." — Duarte (Remember callout, p. 228)

### Protection Considerations [MUST KNOW]

- The market has a tendency for all stocks to rise together in a bull run ("A rising tide lifts
  all boats") — and fall together in a bear market regardless of individual stock quality
- **Goal:** lock in a sell price for a long-term holding without necessarily intending to
  exercise — can simply offset losses with option gains
- Options buy time — prevent hasty decisions like selling and trying to buy back on dips

**Two key decisions before selecting protective puts:**
1. Term for the protection (expiration month)
2. Level of protection (strike price and option price)

**TIP:** Make options part of all stock investment decisions. When choosing between two stocks
with equal growth potential, prefer the one with options available — protection is a
differentiator.

### Time Horizon Selection [MUST KNOW]

- For temporary protection (30–60 days): evaluate options with **60–90 days to expiration**
- This gives flexibility to exit before accelerated theta decay kicks in (30 days prior to
  expiration)
- For bearish period through Oct: use Oct and Jan puts to cover the full bearish window
- Protecting at current appreciated price vs. original purchase price = a personal question
  you face every time

### Table 10-1: Put Option Chain Data for XYZ (Stock at $37.50) [MUST KNOW]

| Month | Strike Price | Bid | Ask | OI |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Oct | 30.00 | 0.20 | 0.25 | 36,287 |
| Oct | 32.50 | 0.30 | 0.35 | 1,965 |
| Oct | 35.00 | 0.60 | 0.70 | 24,641 |
| Oct | 37.50 | 1.25 | 1.05 | 1,338 |
| Jan | 30.00 | 0.50 | 0.60 | 45,795 |
| Jan | 32.50 | 0.75 | 0.85 | 156,657 |
| Jan | 35.00 | 1.25 | 1.35 | 52,734 |
| Jan | 37.50 | 2.00 | 2.15 | 24,225 |

*OI = Open Interest = total number of contracts outstanding for a specific option contract*

### Table 10-2: Put Short List for XYZ (Stock purchased at $34.00) [MUST KNOW]

| Month | Days to Exp | Strike Price | Ask | Delta | Exercise Net Profit |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Oct | 60 | 35.00 | 0.35 | −0.186 | $65 |
| Oct | 60 | 37.50 | 1.05 | −0.460 | $245 |
| Jan | 150 | 35.00 | 1.35 | −0.291 | ($35) |
| Jan | 150 | 37.50 | 2.15 | −0.440 | $135 |

**Exercise Net Profit formula:**
- Put Strike Price – Put Purchase Premium = **Put Breakeven**
- (Put Breakeven – Purchase Price) × 100 = **Net Profit/Loss**

*Example: Oct 37.50 Put @ $1.05 → Breakeven = $37.50 – $1.05 = $36.45 → ($36.45 – $34.00)
× 100 = $245 net profit if exercised*

- Use **delta** as a resource to check the probability the option will be ITM at expiration
- An ITM put has a higher breakeven point on the downside (less time-value decay risk), but
  also reduces upside potential
- Recommended selection for bearish Oct period: **Jan 37.50 put** — provides coverage for
  the full time period

**TIP:** The more time remaining to expiration, the more uncertainty about the stock price at
expiration. ITM option has more time to become OTM and vice versa. Use delta to check
probability of being ITM at expiration (Chapter 3).

### Accelerated Time Decay [MUST KNOW]

- **Theta** = the option Greek identifying daily loss of option value associated with the
  current price of the option
- Example: XYZ Oct 37.60 put trading at $1.05 has theta = –0.0078 → loses $0.0078 per share
  per day, all else equal
- Time decay **accelerates** as expiration approaches, particularly within the last 30 days

> **WARNING:** The impact of time decay accelerates the last 30 days of an option's life.
> This means extrinsic value will decline more quickly along with the value of the option —
> assuming all other conditions remain the same.

**Rule:** Consider exiting a long option position **30 days prior to expiration** to avoid
accelerating losses to its extrinsic value.

### Table 10-3: Theta Values for XYZ Oct 37.50 Put [MUST KNOW]

| Days to Expire | Ask | Theta |
|---|---|---|
| 60 | 1.05 | −0.0078 |
| 30 | 0.75 | −0.0117 |
| 10 | 0.45 | −0.0216 |
| 5 | 0.30 | −0.0314 |

- At 10 days to expiration, $0.0216/day = 4.8% of the contract's $0.45 remaining value per day
- Theta nearly doubles from 60 days out (−0.0078) to 30 days out (−0.0117)
- Then more than doubles again from 30 to 10 days (−0.0216)

Use the OIC Options Industry Council website (www.optionseducation.org) under Tools and
Resources for option calculators to obtain theta values.

### Figure 10-1: Long Stock & Long Put Risk Graph [MUST KNOW]

[CHART: chart_p235_01.png]

This risk graph shows the combined profit/loss profile of holding long stock + long put:
- **Y-axis:** Profit/Loss
- **X-axis:** Price of Underlying Stock
- **Dashed line (upper-left going down):** Standalone long put — profits as stock falls,
  value goes to zero when stock is above strike
- **Solid line (rising from lower-left to upper-right):** Long stock position — unlimited
  upside, unlimited downside
- **Combined position:** The flat dashed line running horizontally to the left shows the
  CAPPED loss — losses are limited at the put strike minus premium paid. Annotation:
  "Put Reduces Risk"
- **Key principle:** The combined position retains all upside of the stock while the put
  creates a floor on the downside. Maximum loss is capped regardless of how far the stock falls.

*Image credit: Optionetics. Figure 10-1: Risk graph for a long stock-protective put position.*

### Weighing Protection Cost versus Time [MUST KNOW]

- For a short, specific time horizon: selecting expiration month is straightforward
- For longer-term protection: ATM options may be OTM by expiration (stock appreciated) —
  may provide minimal effective protection on the stock's appreciated value since purchase
- Alternative: **rolling series of short-term puts** — adjusts strike along the way as stock
  appreciates; downside is higher cost

> "The investment process requires you to balance risk and reward. Without risk there is no
> reward, but it doesn't mean you have to risk it all. Consider protective positions as a
> means of limiting your losses while letting your profits run." — Duarte (Remember callout,
> p. 236)

### Long-Term Protection Cost Analysis [SHOULD KNOW]

- Example: XYZ at $37.50, bought at $34.00. ATM Jan 37.50 put costs $2.15 → 5.7% of stock
  value for 5 months of protection
- Buying puts regularly could sacrifice stock returns — but ignoring protection entirely
  could cost a large chunk of initial investment
- **No universal answer:** must find balance between protection cost and returns

**Three-factor framework when selecting a long-term protective put:**
1. Net exercise value and level of protection provided
2. Statistical chance the option will be ITM at expiration (delta)
3. Cost of protection and its future returns from stock price appreciation

> "Think of the value of the entire position — the stock and the put — and the effect of
> changes in price to the whole amount. When purchasing puts to protect your investments, be
> sure to balance the cost of protection versus net returns for the protective put position."
> — Duarte (Remember callout, p. 236)

### Cost Per Day Calculations [SHOULD KNOW]

Formula: **Option Premium × 100 ÷ Days to Expiration = Daily Cost of Protection**

- XYZ Oct 37.50 Put @ $1.05 → $1.05 × 100 = $105 ÷ 60 days = **$1.75/day**
- XYZ Jan 37.50 Put @ $2.15 → $2.15 × 100 = $215 ÷ 150 days = **$1.43/day**

The Jan 37.50 put is cheaper on a per-day basis ($1.43 vs $1.75), and covers 2.5× longer.

**TIP:** Be careful about buying seemingly cheap puts that don't offer adequate protection
and will likely expire worthless. Consider cost of protection over your entire stock holding
period.

> "Be careful of letting your emotions run away with you. Do what you can to manage your
> positions by responding to market conditions, not overreacting to them. For example,
> waiting to buy put protection until after the market suffers a severe correction will
> ensure that you pay a high price for that protection. Indeed, it's better to buy
> homeowner's insurance before the house burns down." — Duarte (Tip callout, p. 238)

**Rule:** Complete your analysis when markets are closed whenever possible — avoids emotional
decision-making.

---

## Limiting Short Stock Risk with Calls [SHOULD KNOW]

- Long puts protect long stock positions; **long calls protect short stock positions**
- Use case: periodically trading inverse ETFs or other strategies that use short stock
  positions held overnight
- Long call can protect from losses due to overnight gaps upward
- Requires individual stock options to hedge individual stock shorts; ETF call options to
  hedge short ETF or inverse ETF positions

### Protecting a Short Stock Position [MUST KNOW]

- A **long call** gives you the right (not obligation) to buy stock at a specific strike
  price by expiration — exercise to close out a short position if the stock rises quickly
- Short stock positions are generally held for less time → protective call option selection
  is simpler
- Typically evaluate options with the least time to expiration or those in the following month
- **Near month options:** closest to expiration; **Next month options:** expire right after
- Try to use options that match your maximum loss criteria for the short position

### Further Reducing Short Stock Risk — Long Put vs. Short Stock [MUST KNOW]

If bearish, compare owning a long put to shorting the stock outright:

### Table 10-4: Bearish Positions for XYZ on Aug 22nd (XYZ @ $37.50) [MUST KNOW]

| Position | Entry Cost | Max Risk | Max Reward |
|---|---|---|---|
| Long 1 Oct 37.50 Put | $105 | $105 | $3,645 |
| Short 100 Shares XYZ | $3750 | Unlimited | $3,750 |

**Analysis of each position:**

- **Stock entry cost:** Short selling requires 150% margin — 100% credited from stock sale,
  remaining 50% cash you must have available. Entry cost = 100% of stock price = $3,750.

- **Stock maximum risk:** Theoretically unlimited — stock can rise without limit. A stop order
  helps but overnight gaps can exceed stop levels.

- **Option maximum risk:** Premium paid = $105. This is the absolute maximum loss.

- **Option maximum reward:** If stock falls to $0 — intrinsic value = $37.50 (strike). OCC
  converts deliverable to cash value of strike price. $37.50 – $1.05 (premium) = **$36.45
  per share × 100 = $3,645**.

- **Option breakeven level:** Put Strike – Option Price = $37.50 – $1.05 = **$36.45**

> "Puts increase in value when a stock decreases and represent a bearish position. Although
> they are wasting assets that are negatively impacted by time decay, they have limited risk
> and limited, but high, reward potential." — Duarte (Remember callout, p. 240)

**Key conclusion:** Buying puts is a less risky bet than shorting stock when bearish — maximum
loss is capped at the premium ($105 vs. theoretically unlimited for short stock). On a
percentage return basis, a long put can far exceed the short stock reward if the stock drops
significantly. The main cost: time decay erodes the put's value; the short stock has no
equivalent decay.

<!-- pp. 241-260 -->

## Chapter 10 (continued): Hedging, Protecting Portfolios, and Adjusted Options

### Hedging Your Bets with Options

[MUST KNOW]
- Two primary options-based hedges:
  - **Long put** paired with a **long stock position** (protective put)
  - **Long call** paired with a **short stock position**
- **Hedge**: a position used to offset losses in a security resulting from adverse market moves
- Option can be exercised to close the stock position, but exercising forfeits remaining **time-value** — generally not recommended
- Gains in the option can be used to offset losses in the stock without exercising

### Perfect Hedge vs. Partial Hedge

[MUST KNOW]
- **Perfect hedge**: a position that includes one security that gains the same value lost by a second security — the gain exactly offsets the loss ($1 down in XYZ = $1 up in hedge)
- Perfect hedges are expensive
- ATM put options have a delta near –0.50, so **two ATM puts** have a collective delta close to –1.0, the mirror image of the stock's +1.0 delta
- Problem: the time-value for ATM options only protects 1-for-1 AFTER deducting the premium cost

### Two-Step Hedge Decision Framework

[SHOULD KNOW]
- Step 1: Calculate protection the hedge will buy — figure net sale price if put is exercised; assess whether that suits your individual stock position or entire portfolio
- Step 2: Figure out the premium you will pay
- Combine both steps into a final decision: **"How much am I paying to protect my portfolio with these options?"**

> "REMEMBER: The option Greek delta obtained using an option calculator provides the expected change in the option's per-share value given a $1 change in the underlying stock."

### Protecting a Portfolio … Partially

[SHOULD KNOW]
- A partial hedge occurs when you own a security that gains value when the hedged position loses value, but NOT on a one-to-one basis
- Example: a $1 gain in one stock may correspond to only a $0.75 loss in the paired security
- When two securities move in opposite directions but not in equal magnitude, you have a partially hedged position
- **TIP: Delta can be used to help construct partially or completely hedged positions**

### Hedging Stock with Stock Options (Worked Example)

[MUST KNOW]
- XYZ Oct 35.00 put has a delta of –0.186
- You own 100 shares of XYZ; the Oct 35.00 put delta calculation:
  - Formula: (Change in Underlying) × (Delta) = Change in Option
  - (–1) × (–0.186) = +0.186
- When XYZ drops $1 to $37.50, the Oct 35 put moves up $0.19 to ~$0.35
- Stock position lost $100; option position gained ~$19 — **partial hedge only**
- This lessened but did not fully cushion the loss

**Key insight on partial → perfect hedge:**
- Partial hedges can become perfect hedges at expiration after time-value has fully decayed to zero, IF the put option is **in-the-money**
- In-the-money put options have a delta of –1.0, the same as stock

> "WARNING: Listed index options have different characteristics than listed stock options. For instance, an index is not a security, so it's not something you can buy and sell. As a result, index options settle in cash rather than the transfer of a physical asset."

### Hedging a Portfolio with Index Options

[MUST KNOW]
- Individual stock options OR **index options** can protect a portfolio, assuming the portfolio is well correlated to a specific index
- Some portfolios may require both: index options for stocks correlated to the index, individual stock options for others that don't correlate well

**Correlation** — key concept for index hedging:
- Describes the relationship between data sets (stocks vs. index)
- Values range from –1 to +1:
  - **+1 (perfectly positively correlated)**: stocks move in the same direction by the same magnitude
  - **–1 (perfectly negatively correlated)**: stocks move in the opposite direction by the same magnitude
  - **0 (not correlated)**: stocks do not move consistently in terms of direction and magnitude
- Correlation determines how many contracts you need to pay to protect your position

### OEX Index Option Hedge Example

[SHOULD KNOW]
- Scenario: $225,000 portfolio well correlated to the **OEX** (S&P 100 Index), OEX trading at ~1040
- OEX index options available for different months in **five-point strike price increments**
- When OEX is at 1042, a 1040 call has $2 of intrinsic value (moneyness works the same for index and stock options)
- Option multiplier for index options: also 100 (same as stock options)
- Total option premium for March 1040 put: $205.50 × 100 = **$20,550**
- Option package value (deliverable): 1040 × 100 = **$104,000**

**TABLE 10-5: Put Option Chain Data for the OEX**

| Month | Strike Price | Bid    | Ask    | Delta* | OI    |
|-------|-------------|--------|--------|--------|-------|
| Mar   | 1040        | 203.50 | 205.00 | –0.321 | 1,663 |
|       | 1050        | 210.00 | 210.50 | –0.361 | 3,277 |
|       | 1060        | 211.30 | 212.10 | –0.406 | 748   |
|       | 1070        | 213.20 | 213.90 | –0.455 | 2,883 |

*Delta using the Ask value

> "REMEMBER: The option multiplier is the contract value used to determine the net option premium (Option Market Price × Multiplier) and the deliverable value of the option package (Option Strike Price × Multiplier)."

### Strike Price Selection for Index Hedge

[MUST KNOW]
- Goal: protect against declines greater than 2%
- Formula to find starting strike price:
  - 1040 – (1040 × 0.02) = 20.8
  - 1040 – 20.8 = **1019.20**
- Both the 1040 and 1050 strike prices can be considered
- Using the 1040 put:
  - 1 put: 1 × 1040 × 100 = $104,000 protection
  - 2 puts: 2 × 1040 × 100 = $208,000 protection
  - Portfolio protected: $208,000 ÷ $225,000 = **92.44%**

**How protection works as OEX moves:**
- If OEX drops below 1040, puts gain intrinsic value at the rate of the put's delta
- The further the OEX declines, the closer puts approach a 1:1 move with the index
- Once put is in-the-money, it gains intrinsic value **1 for 1** with the index
- Time remaining until expiration also affects actual gains made by the hedge

> "REMEMBER: ATM puts and calls have deltas that are approximately 0.50. Once an option moves from ATM to ITM, delta increases. When an option moves OTM, delta decreases. The option Greek that provides you with a feel for just how much delta decreases is *gamma*."

### Option Package Exercise Value

[SHOULD KNOW]
- When put contract rights are exercised, the put option holder receives the **strike price × multiplier** (usually 100)
- This amount = **option package exercise value**
- Also called: option package assignment value; option package deliverable value
- All terms refer to the same thing: money exchanged when call or put rights are actually exercised

> "TECHNICAL STUFF: A stock option package generally represents 100 shares of the underlying stock. When using strike price and multiplier of 100 to value the option package, it's best to consider the option package value as simply: Strike Price × Multiplier. This is best practice especially for index options or adjusted stock options."

### Protecting a Portfolio … Completely

[MUST KNOW]
- Delta ranges (alternate notation for hedging discussions):
  - Call: 0 to +100
  - Put: 0 to –100
- One share of stock has a delta of **+1**; therefore 100 shares = **+100 delta**
- To perfectly hedge 100 shares, position delta should be as close to 0 as possible (long stock delta + put delta = 0)

**XYZ perfect stock hedge example:**
- XYZ at $37.50; goal is to hedge 100 shares, ~$5,000 combined position
- Jan 35 put has delta of –29.1
- Three puts won't cover 100 shares; evaluate **four puts**:
  - Position Delta = 4 × (–29.1) = **–116.4**
  - 116 shares × +1 delta = +116 deltas
  - 4 puts × –29.1 delta = –116.4 deltas
  - Combined Position Delta = +116 + (–116.4) = **–0.4 deltas** (near-perfect hedge)
- Position Cost:
  - 116 shares × $37.50 = $4,350
  - 4 puts × $1.35 × 100 = $540
  - **Total position cost = $4,890**

**Why this hedge doesn't last:**
- Every time XYZ moves $1, delta changes approximately by its **gamma** value
- As stock price moves, puts shift from partial to full hedge and back — requiring rebalancing
- TIP: ATM calls generally have deltas slightly greater than 0.50; ATM puts generally slightly less than 0.50

> "REMEMBER: Options are referred to as a *variable delta* security. Delta for an option changes by gamma for each $1 change in the underlying. One share of stock, on the other hand, is a *fixed delta* security — it stays constant at +1 delta. Delta is the middleman between the price of the underlying and the value of gamma."

### Portfolio Hedge (Index)

[SHOULD KNOW]
- Hedging a portfolio is more inexact than hedging an individual position
- Challenge: not all portfolios are perfectly correlated to an index — creates strategic problems
- **Perfect portfolio hedge is elusive** because the option delta changes when index value changes AND the correlation between index and portfolio is imperfect

**Delta approach for $225,000 portfolio (vs. strike price estimate):**
- Mar 1050 puts are ITM by 10 points (OEX at 1040)
- Market price $210.50; assumed delta = –0.549
- Purchase two Mar 1050 puts:
  - 2 × 1050 × 100 = $210,000 (package value)
  - 2 × –0.549 = **–1.10** (combined delta)
- For each 1-point OEX decline, combined puts increase by 1.10 → provides 1.1× protection
- $210,000 × 1.1 = **$231,000** portfolio protected
- As the put remains in-the-money, hedge strengthens as time-value decays (intrinsic value becomes larger share of option price; intrinsic value is always a perfect 1-for-1 hedge)

---

### Avoiding Adjusted Option Risk

[MUST KNOW]
- **Adjusted options**: options that existed when certain corporate actions took place; contract terms were required to adjust to reflect those actions
- Corporate actions that trigger option adjustments:
  - Stock splits
  - Large cash dividend distributions
  - Mergers and acquisitions
  - Spinoffs
- Most **regular dividends** do NOT result in option contract adjustment
- **Special dividends** cause strike prices to be adjusted down by the amount of the special dividend

### Justifying Option Adjustments

[SHOULD KNOW]
- Two main reasons options are adjusted after corporate actions:
  1. To ensure existing contracts retain their proper value
  2. So that the contract reflects the corporate action in its deliverable package
- Without adjustments: you could see a call lose all its value after a stock split, or a put double in value after a large cash dividend — extreme and unsustainable volatility

### Corporate Action 1: Stock Splits

[MUST KNOW]
- **2:1 stock split**: you receive one additional share for each share you own on the **record date**
- On the day of the split, stock price is divided by 2
- Option adjustments for a 2:1 split:
  - Number of contracts held is **doubled**
  - **Strike price is cut in half**
  - A new option contract is created with a new symbol
  - You'll see two contracts of the new option for each contract you previously owned
- Adjustment for **3:2 split** is more complex — requires changing both the multiplier (to 150) and the strike price (to two-thirds of original)

> "WARNING: When you exercise a put without holding the underlying stock in your account, you create a short position. This is because the put rights allow you to sell the underlying stock at the contract strike price. Selling a stock you don't own reverses the typical order for a stock transaction and creates a short position."

### Corporate Action 2: Mergers, Spinoffs, and Dividends

[MUST KNOW]
- Mergers, acquisitions, spinoffs, and large cash dividends all change the underlying option package
- Original 100 shares may now represent:
  - **Merger**: 100 shares original + shares of acquired stock
  - **Spinoff**: 100 shares original + shares of new stock
  - **Large Cash Dividend**: 100 shares original + cash amount
  - **Acquisition** (company acquired): No original stock + shares of acquiring stock
- In the last case, the original underlying stock may no longer exist

> "TIP: If you think you found an option deal that seems too good to be true, you may have very well stumbled upon an adjusted option. Traders on the exchanges are very familiar with corporate actions — there's no free money on Wall Street, so don't jump into these options without fully understanding them."

**Critical rule:**
- Never, *never* create a new position using an adjusted option contract you don't completely understand
- Contact your broker if a contract you own gets adjusted

> "TIP: Whenever a combined position (stock plus option) you own is adjusted, be extremely careful about exiting the stock or option position separately. The combined position maintains the proper stock-option ratios initially created, but by selling any portion of the adjusted stock position, you may be creating high risk in the option position. Call your broker to discuss any position changes."

### Adjusting from Adjustments (What to Do)

[SHOULD KNOW]
- Two key actions:
  1. When you note any adjusted option in your account, check the contract specifications so you understand your new rights or obligations
  2. Be aware of adjusted options when establishing **new** positions so you properly value the securities you trade and know your rights and obligations
- If an option quote doesn't seem right, check the contract details; call your broker if uncertain
- Avoid creating new option positions using adjusted options — no hidden money, just extra complexity

### Detecting an Adjusted Option

[SHOULD KNOW]
- The **Chicago Board Options Exchange (CBOE)** reports on adjusted options and pertinent changes (www.cboe.com/tradtool/contracts.aspx)
- Things to check for adjusted options:
  - Changes in the **symbol** for the option series
  - Changes in the **number of shares** per contract (e.g., split stocks may go from standard 100 to 150 shares)
  - Whether the option has changed **style** (American vs. European) and how that affects ability to exercise
  - **Possible changes in strike prices**
- When something doesn't seem right in terms of a quote or volume, dig deeper

### Valuing Split-Adjusted Options

[SHOULD KNOW]
- 2:1 split: new contracts valued the same way as regular 100-multiplier options — straightforward
- **3:2 split**: atypical, requires more attention
  - Change the **multiplier to 150**
  - Change the **strike price to two-thirds of original value**
  - Four-step valuation process:
    1. Use adjusted strike price and multiplier to calculate exercise value (e.g., XYX 60 Call: 60 × 150 = 9,000)
    2. Determine the deliverable value in the market using current quotes (150 shares JKL × $62 = $9,300)
    3. Subtract deliverable value from exercise value to obtain **intrinsic value** ($9,300 – $9,000 = $300)
    4. If current price of 60 call is $3: extrinsic value = ($3 × 150 = $450) – $300 = **$150**

---

## Chapter 11: Increasing Profit Potential and Decreasing Risk

**IN THIS CHAPTER:**
- Reducing risk with options
- Combining options for trend trades
- Benefiting from strong directional moves

### Chapter Overview

[MUST KNOW]
- Trading stocks is conceptually simple — pick direction, pick vehicle
- Long position for rising prices; short position for falling prices
- Options add more choices and more work, but provide:
  - Ability to profit from both up and down moves
  - Chance to make money in other ways (e.g., volatility changes, time decay income)
  - **Sizeable reward potential with less capital risk**
- Single option positions can be combined to further reduce costs
- Key concept: **rewiring your brain** to trade for less money and less risk through leverage

### Leveraging Assets to Reduce Risk

[MUST KNOW]
- The counterintuitive truth: options can **leverage your assets while decreasing the amount of investment at risk**
- The premium you pay locks in a price for a stock without putting up 100% of its value
- With options: less capital at risk, less money spent, more upside potential
- Caveat: no guarantee the stock moves in the desired direction — same uncertainty as stock trading, but for less money

### Determining Your Total Dollars at Risk

[MUST KNOW]
- Core principle: **options reduce risk because you invest less money**
- Your initial investment = maximum potential loss (for long options and long stock)
- Exception: if you buy stock **on margin**, you can lose twice your initial investment
- Any stock can drop to zero — so any long stock or call position can also go to zero
- Time constraint caveat: when trading options, you can't wait indefinitely for your anticipated move — **time decay works against you**, requiring closer attention to price action and news

> "TIP: The risk for a long stock position is considered limited, but high. That's because a stock cannot fall below zero. Unfortunately, there's a lot of room for losses between zero and the price of some stocks."

### Calling Risk Out When Bullish

[MUST KNOW]
- When bullish, you can:
  - Create a long stock position
  - Create a long call position
- Both profit when stock rises, but key difference: **risk is reduced with a call** because total investment is smaller
- The tradeoff: profits accrue more slowly with the call option vs. the stock (delta < 1)

**Figure 11-1: Long Stock vs. Long Call Option (Risk Graph Overlay)**

[PAGE IMAGE: page_259.png — visible in PDF rendering above]

This figure shows a risk graph overlay comparing two positions on XYZ stock:
- **Dashed line** = Long stock position (breakeven at $36, the stock purchase price)
- **Solid line** = Long call option position (breakeven at $40, reflecting premium paid)

Key features of the graph:
- X-axis: Price of Underlying Stock (scale anchored at 36 and 40)
- Y-axis: Profit/Loss (scale from –3,900 to above 0, with –300 noted near breakeven)
- The stock position has a MUCH steeper loss profile as price falls — losses extend to near –$3,900 as the stock approaches zero
- The call option's maximum loss is capped near –$300 (the premium paid), represented by the flat floor on the solid line
- Above the option breakeven ($40), both lines are roughly parallel — profits accrue in both, but the stock accrues faster (higher delta)
- "Stock Breakeven" labeled at ~$36; "Option Breakeven" labeled at ~$40

**Teaching point:** The significant difference between the two positions is in the **losses**: long stock has large, uncapped downside; long call caps loss at the premium. Profits accrue faster with the stock position (higher slope), but the option tradeoff — slower gains above breakeven in exchange for dramatically reduced downside — is worth taking.

> "Because there are a series of tradeoffs in this business, it makes sense to take the slower accrual of gains in an options trade because of the decrease in total risk. It is certainly possible for the stock to remain dormant for months, causing us to exit the position for a loss only to then have it begin a serious upward move. Again, it's a tradeoff we are willing to take."

> "TIP: A risk graph provides a very efficient way for you to understand the risks, rewards, and breakevens associated with a particular strategy."

### Three Factors That Change Option Value

[MUST KNOW]
Option value increases or decreases as:
1. **Stock price moves** (up or down with the underlying)
2. **Implied volatility (IV)** increases or decreases — higher IV = higher contract value
3. **Time passes** — always decreases option value (theta decay)

- Price alone doesn't dictate an option's price — **IV also plays a critical role**
- Higher IV = higher contract values
- Time decay seen daily is small, but the cumulative effect can significantly erode value

> "REMEMBER: Establish long option positions when implied volatility (IV) is relatively low to increase the probability for profits and minimize losses due to decreases in IV. Also, keep in mind that a relatively low IV environment does not guarantee IV will rise over the life of the option."

### Using LEAPS for Long-Term Option Positions

[MUST KNOW]
- **LEAPS** = **Long-term Equity AntiCiPation Security**
- Not a new type of instrument — just an option with a long time to expiration
- Time to expiration: **anywhere from nine months to three years**
- Not all stocks with options have LEAPS available; for those that do, expiration month is almost always **January**
- Different root symbols distinguish LEAPS from regular options

**How LEAPS work:**
- Created in May, June, or July depending on the option's cycle
- New contracts expire in January ~2½ years from the creation date
  - Example: by August 2014, LEAPS available for both January 2015 and January 2016
- When new LEAPS are rolled out, the **closest January LEAPS becomes a regular option** as the Options Clearing Corporation (OCC) revises the symbol to include the regular option root

**LEAPS symbol example:**
- Alphabet (GOOGL) January 15, 2016 440 call symbol: **GOOGL16A15430.0**
  - "16A15" = January 2016 expiration
  - "430.0" = strike price

**Where to find LEAPS:**
- CBOE website (www.cboe.com) — complete listing, delayed quotes (good for paper trading)
- Broker or online charting/quote service — real-time pricing in option chains

**LEAPS cost and benefit:**
- More time to expiration = **more money you pay**
- Higher risk due to increased cost, but greater chance of holding a contract that is **ITM at expiration**
- LEAPS are:
  - Available for some stocks and indexes that have regular options
  - An investment alternative providing up to **2½–3 years** to benefit from contract rights

<!-- pp. 261-280 -->

## Chapter 11: Using Options for Income and Hedging (continued)

### LEAPS Option Chain Example — Alphabet Inc. (GOOGL) [MUST KNOW]

- To illustrate LEAPS pricing, the author presents a **partial LEAPS option chain** for **Alphabet Inc. (GOOGL)**, assuming the stock trades at $597.78
- LEAPS provide more time for investing strategies AND serve as extended warranties (protection) on the asset protection side
- Combining a **LEAPS put with long stock** significantly reduces the cost per day for protection
- Trade-off: as the stock rises over time, put value decreases while strike price remains fixed — you must balance reduced cost with desired protection level

**TABLE 11-1: Partial Option Chain for Google with LEAPS**

| Call Contract Name | Bid | Ask | Strike Price | Put Contract Name | Bid | Ask |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| GOOGL\16A15\260.0 | 337.10 | 342.00 | 260.00 | GOOGL\16M15\260.0 | 0.15 | 1.10 |
| GOOGL\16A15\270.0 | 327.20 | 332.00 | 270.00 | GOOGL\16M15\270.0 | 0.20 | 1.65 |
| GOOGL\16A15\280.0 | 317.70 | 322.50 | 280.00 | GOOGL\16M15\280.0 | 0.35 | 1.95 |
| GOOGL\16A15\290.0 | 308.00 | 312.90 | 290.00 | GOOGL\16M15\290.0 | 0.45 | 2.20 |

*Quotes courtesy of www.cboe.com*

- The call contracts expire in April 2016 (16A15), puts expire in January 2016 (16M15)
- Note the wide spread between call bids/asks (~$5) vs very tight put spreads (~$0.15–$0.70) at these deep ITM strike prices
- **TIP:** Stocks that are more volatile generally have a larger number of strike prices available each month because there is a greater chance the stock will reach a strike price that is farther away
- **TIP (CAUTION):** Options that expire in January could potentially exist for 2½ years — they have the highest potential for being adjusted due to corporate actions. Be especially careful when trading Jan options with quotes that seem "off." Check contract specifications for details on the underlying package.

---

### Putting Limits on a Moving Bear Market [MUST KNOW]

When bearish on a stock, two main choices:
- Create a **short stock position**
- Create a **long put position**

Both profit if the stock goes down. Rewards are limited because a stock can only move to zero — but potentially high if the stock becomes worthless.

**Figure 11-2: Short Stock vs Long Put Option — Overlay Risk Graph**

[PAGE IMAGE: page_263.png]

The risk graph overlays the short stock position (dashed diagonal line) against the long put position (solid line with flat floor). Key features visible:
- Y-axis: Profit/Loss ranging from about –300 to +3600
- X-axis: Price of Underlying Stock, with key price markers at 33 and 36
- **Short stock** (dashed line): descends diagonally to lower right (profits as price falls), but rises diagonally to upper right with theoretically unlimited loss as price rises — "Stock Breakeven" labeled at upper right
- **Long put** (solid line): flat at –300 (max loss = premium paid) to the right of the strike; slopes upward to the left as stock falls below breakeven; labeled "Option Breakeven" at ~33
- Breakeven for short stock: entry price (~36)
- Breakeven for long put: ~33 (strike minus premium)

Two main takeaways from the risk graph:
1. **The significant difference in losses** — a short sale is a very bad situation if the stock rises rapidly and in large intervals (unlimited upside risk for the short seller)
2. **The less-than-significant difference in gains** — both positions have similar profit profiles on the downside

> **Trading Rule:** The long put caps maximum loss at the premium paid, while short stock exposes you to unlimited upside loss. For bearish positions, the long put offers defined risk.

---

### Relying on Market Timing [MUST KNOW]

Trading is difficult, and the more you do it, the more you come to accept that fact. It's pretty hard to identify the future direction of a stock, let alone how far it will go and by when. Selecting a proper time frame for an option is clearly an important part of trading these securities.

**The three requirements for basic option trading:**
1. Correctly predict the **direction** of the underlying move
2. Correctly predict the **magnitude** of the move
3. Correctly predict the **maximum time** it will take for the move to occur

For stock trading, all three are also required — but the difference is you can hold a long stock position for months as it trades sideways (dead money, but not a realized loss). With options, time decay makes sideways movement actively costly.

**TIP:** There are times when a stock breaks out of a limited-range, sideways channel, only to return back into the channel. As an investor, you may not do anything, but as a trader, if you've created a directional position based on the breakout, you **must exit the position** (stock or option) if the stock returns to the channel, since the conditions that justified the trade no longer exist.

**Strike a balance by:**
- Recognizing the role probabilities play in trading stocks and options
- Being prepared to be "wrong" and limit your losses
- Paying the right amount of premium for moves that are realistic

---

### Predicting Proper Direction [MUST KNOW]

To capitalize on either a stock or single option position, you must correctly identify the direction of the underlying stock move. Since it seems reasonable to favor a security that uses less of your capital — options — here are general rules of thumb for increasing probability of success:

- **Trade with the trend** when using technical tools
- Or (for proficient contrarians) **trade against the trend when momentum is weakening** and your indicators point to a pending turn
- **Trade undervalued stocks that are gaining some positive attention** when using fundamental tools
- **Limit your losses in every trade** with unbiased exit strategies

---

### Predicting the Magnitude of the Move [MUST KNOW]

**Time risk** is the main disadvantage to trading options, but **magnitude risk** is another that requires discussion:
- You can be correct about the direction AND timing of a stock move and still have the magnitude be too small to make your option position profitable
- This happens to all option traders

**How to minimize magnitude shortfalls:**
- Use tools — technical or fundamental — that provide estimated **price projections**
- Stay vigilant about what the market is doing relative to your position
- Focus on **higher probability trades** (higher deltas indicating the move is more likely to occur) over lower-probability "home run" trades
- Allow small to moderate gains to accumulate over time

**TIP:** Don't get greedy. Consider taking a portion of your profits off the table by exiting part of your total position when the move you anticipated is partially complete.

**Option pricing models help identify higher probability trades by providing:**
- **Expected movement** implied by the option price (implied volatility)
- **An estimate of the probability** the option will be ITM at expiration

By using these components in your trade analysis, you can determine whether the option price is relatively expensive or inexpensive given the stock's history, past option pricing, and market conditions.

---

### Predicting the Right Time [MUST KNOW]

The forced time limits for an option provide newer traders with their first rules-based system when risk is properly managed. This means both:
- The trade represents a reasonable portion of the account
- The position is exited **prior to the acceleration of time decay**

A long option position has a clear, built-in exit rule. But this shouldn't be the only guideline you use to exit a position.

**There are no one-size-fits-all criteria** for selecting expiration periods — they can vary by strategy and trading style. The most straightforward time horizon for option trading is associated with **scheduled releases of news or reports** that can prompt strong movement by a specific date:
- Economic or industry reports (e.g., unemployment figures, semiconductor orders)
- Earnings releases

Some technical tools also provide estimated time projections, including price patterns or cycles. **Identify your time horizons first, then check option chains.**

---

## Combining Options to Reduce Risk [MUST KNOW]

A put option combined with long stock protects by limiting position risk. A call option added to a short stock position does the same. In both cases, position cost increases.

**REMEMBER:** The breakeven level for a stock is simply the entry price. Because option premiums represent a cost to you above and beyond your strike price contract rights, a breakeven value must be calculated using both the strike price and option price.

**When creating positions focused on specific market outlooks, you can combine:**
- Call and/or put options with stocks
- Different call options together
- Different put options together
- Calls and puts together

Adding long puts or calls to stock are the only combined positions discussed so far, but **short options can also be used to reduce risk** by:
- Further reducing the net cost of the position and/or
- Increasing the potential directions the underlying can trade while still realizing profits

### Covered vs. Naked Short Options [MUST KNOW]

When a short option is properly combined with the underlying stock or a long option of the same type, it is said to be **covered** — because your risk (obligations) under the short contract can be satisfied using the stock or by exercising your rights under the long contract.

Without such protection, the short contract is referred to as **naked** — "That's kind of a good visual on your exposure and risk of injury in nasty places such as the market."

**Naked options:**
- Receive a credit when the position is opened (equal to the option premium)
- If all goes well, the option expires OTM and you keep the credit
- Many newsletters promote naked option strategies as a way to bring in monthly revenue — **seller beware**

> **WARNING:** Going naked with a call option is the riskiest position you can create. Rather than creating a limited-risk, unlimited-reward position consistent with good risk management, a naked call is an **unlimited-risk, limited-reward position**.

- What often happens: months of smaller credits get wiped out with losses from just one or two trades that go against you
- Risk can be limited by combining options for **credits or debits using covered option positions**

---

### Vertical Spreads — Spreading the Risk with a Debit Trade [MUST KNOW]

A **vertical spread** is a position that combines two options — one long and one short option of the same type (calls or puts) — having the same expiration month and different strike prices.

**Why "vertical"?** That's how the strike prices line up when you look at an option chain.
**Why "spread"?** It spreads the risk by using two positions based on the same stock.
**Each option position** in a vertical spread is referred to as a **leg**.

A vertical spread can be created for an **initial debit** or an **initial credit**. In each case, the position has **limited risk and limited reward**.

**Two types of vertical debit spreads:**

- **Bull call spread:** Purchase a call option and simultaneously sell another call option that expires the same month. The short call has a **higher** strike price. Because the price of that higher-strike call is less expensive, you pay a net debit for the trade. The short call reduces the price of the long call, so this spread has less risk than buying a long call option alone.

- **Bear put spread:** Purchase a put option and simultaneously sell another put option that expires the same month. The short put has a **lower** strike price. Because the price of that lower-strike put is less expensive, you pay a net debit for the trade. The short put reduces the price of the long put, so this spread has less risk than buying a long put option alone.

> **WARNING:** Going naked with a put option is a very risky position, even if you're willing to buy the stock at the short put strike price. Short-put assignment generally occurs when the stock is declining or bad news is released. Purchasing a stock in the market or through assignment at a time like this goes against reasonable risk-management principles.

---

### Assessing Risk and Reward for a Call Debit Spread (Bull Call Spread) [MUST KNOW]

Maximum risk = the initial debit paid to create it (similar to a basic long call position).
Because the position combines a short call to reduce the long call cost, it also reduces risk. But reducing risk comes at a price: **reduced rewards**.

**Example:** ABC is trading at $37.65 and you are bullish:
- Buy 1 Jan 35 Call @ $4.20
- Sell 1 Jan 40 Call @ $1.50

**Calculations:**
- Debit = Max Risk = ($4.20 – $1.50) × 100 = **$270**
  - Max loss occurs when ABC closes at $35 or less at expiration (both calls worthless)
- Max Reward = [($40 – $35) × 100] – $270 = **$230**
  - Max reward occurs when ABC trades at $40 or higher at expiration
- Breakeven = $35 + ($4.20 – $1.50) = $35 + $2.70 = **$37.70**
- Actual gain or loss may be somewhere between max risk and max reward if ABC closes between 35 and 40

**Figure 11-3: Risk Graph for ABC Jan 35–40 Bull Call Spread**

[PAGE IMAGE: page_270.png]

The Optionetics Platinum risk graph shows two panels:
- Left panel: GILD stock price chart showing price history over time, with a horizontal blue line marking approximately $34–35 — the lower strike price
- Right panel: The risk/reward diagram with:
  - X-axis: GILD Stock Price ($20 to $55)
  - Y-axis: Profits ($) from –300 to +250
  - Lower horizontal line at –$270 (max risk)
  - Upper horizontal line at +$230 (max reward)
  - Dark vertical line at $37.70 (breakeven)
  - Diagonal line rising from lower strike ($35) to upper strike ($40), then flattening
  - "0d" marker showing the at-expiration profile
  - Blue vertical line showing current stock price position

**The risk chart identifies:**
- Max risk of $270 — lower horizontal line
- Max reward of $230 — upper horizontal line
- A breakeven stock price of $37.70 — dark vertical line
- A range from losses to profits displayed by a diagonal line extending from the lower strike price to the higher strike price

> **TIP:** Because a vertical debit spread is a net long position, its value will suffer the same accelerated time decay within 30 days to expiration as a basic long option position. Incorporate a method to exit the spread prior to this time if the position is at risk of losing value this way.

> **TIP:** Risk, reward, and breakeven calculations for vertical spreads are completed by assuming you're assigned on your short obligation and exercise your long rights.

---

### Assessing Risk and Reward for a Put Debit Spread (Bear Put Spread) [MUST KNOW]

Maximum risk = the initial debit paid to create it (similar to a basic long put position).
The position combines a short put to reduce the long put cost, also reducing risk.

**Example:** XYZ is trading at $50.85 and you are bearish:
- Buy 1 Jan 50 Put @ $2.75
- Sell 1 Jan 45 Put @ $1.30

**Calculations:**
- Debit = Max Risk = ($2.75 – $1.30) × 100 = **$145.00**
- Max Reward = [($50 – $45) × 100] – $145.00 = **$355.00**
- Breakeven = $50 – ($2.75 – $1.30) = $50 – $1.45 = **$48.55**
- Maximum reward occurs when XYZ trades at $45 or lower at expiration

**Figure 11-4: Risk Graph for XYZ Jan 45–50 Bear Put Spread**

[PAGE IMAGE: page_272.png]

The Optionetics Platinum risk graph shows two panels:
- Left panel: BAC stock price chart over time, with a horizontal blue line at approximately $50; price has ranged from ~$45 to ~$60
  - Date: 2007-08-27, Close: 50.87, Open: 51.77, High: 51.77, Low: 50.76
- Right panel: Risk/reward diagram with:
  - X-axis: Stock Price ($40 to $65)
  - Y-axis: Profits ($) from –200 to +400
  - Upper horizontal line at +$355 (max reward)
  - Lower horizontal line at –$145 (max risk)
  - Dark vertical line at $48.55 (breakeven)
  - Blue vertical line at current stock price (~$51)
  - Diagonal line from higher strike ($50) down to lower strike ($45), then flat

**The risk chart identifies:**
- Max risk of $145 — lower horizontal line
- Max reward of $355 — upper horizontal line
- A breakeven stock price of $48.55 — dark vertical line
- A range from losses to profits displayed by a diagonal line extending from the higher strike price to the lower strike price

---

### Summarizing Debit Risks and Rewards [MUST KNOW]

In both vertical debit spreads, risks and rewards are limited. Each spread has less risk than its corresponding basic long option position because you reduce the initial debit by the price of the short option. The decreased risk comes at a cost: **significantly reduced rewards**, because the short option position also caps your profits.

**TABLE 11-2: Risk, Reward, and Breakeven for Vertical Debit Spreads**

|  | **Bull Call Spread** | **Bear Put Spread** |
|---|---|---|
| Risk | Initial debit | Initial debit |
| Reward | [(Higher strike – lower strike) × multiplier] – initial debit | [(Higher strike – lower strike) × multiplier] – initial debit |
| Breakeven | Long strike price + (long option price – short option price) | Long strike price – (long option price – short option price) |

**Execution tips:**
- **TIP:** A trade risk graph provides specific risks, rewards, and breakevens associated with a particular trade
- When placing a new vertical debit spread order, use a **limit amount less than the quoted price** for the combined position to reduce slippage impact — you likely can get the order filled if you shave a little off the debit amount
- **TIP:** *Jim Fink's* ***Velocity Trader*** *newsletter* is all about spreads and is worth looking into if you find this type of trading appealing

**SPREAD TRADE FLOOR APPEAL (sidebar):**
> Traders on exchange floors manage their risk by staying hedged in the market. When they make a market for your long call order, they end up with a short call position — then buy shares of stock or other long calls to cover. When you enter an order for a vertical spread for a debit, the trader can execute your order without having to worry about hedging — they basically just create a vertical spread for a credit. Vertical spread orders are appealing to floor traders because when they execute the trade, they're already hedged. That's why you can usually get a vertical spread order executed below the debit quoted in the market.

- **TIP:** Consider entering a vertical debit spread when there are at least **60 days to expiration** to give the position time to become profitable
- **WARNING:** Never exit the long leg of a vertical spread without also exiting the short side — otherwise you are significantly changing your risk profile. This applies **even when it appears the short leg will expire worthless**.

---

### Spreading the Risk with a Credit Trade [MUST KNOW]

Debit spreads are not the only type of spread trade using calls or puts. You can switch which strike price is purchased and which is sold in the debit spreads to create a **credit spread** instead. The spread still requires buying one option and selling another of the same type expiring the same month.

**Two types of vertical credit spreads:**

- **Bear call spread:** Purchase a call option and simultaneously sell another call option that expires the same month. The **short call has a lower strike price**. Because the price of a lower-strike call is more expensive, you **receive a credit** for the trade. The long call ends up covering the short call, so this spread has significantly less risk than a naked short call option.

- **Bull put spread:** Purchase a put option and simultaneously sell another put option that expires the same month. The **short put has a higher strike price**. Because the price of a higher-strike put is more expensive, you **receive a credit** for the trade. The long put ends up covering the short put, so this spread has significantly less risk than a naked short put option.

---

### Assessing Risk and Reward for a Call Credit Spread (Bear Call Spread) [MUST KNOW]

Maximum risk = (difference between option strike prices) minus the credit received.
The position uses the long call to limit the short call risk, which by itself is unlimited.

**Example:** Instead of an XYZ bear put spread for a debit, you can create an XYZ bear call spread for a credit:
- Buy 1 Jan 55 Call @ $0.95
- Sell 1 Jan 50 Call @ $3.20

**Calculations:**
- Credit = Max Reward = ($3.20 – $0.95) × 100 = **$225.00**
- Max Risk = [($55 – $50) × 100] – $225.00 = **$275.00**
- Breakeven = $50 + ($3.20 – $0.95) = $50 + $2.25 = **$52.25**

A bear call spread position reduces risk by capping losses for the short call. Reducing risk this way means rewards are reduced. **Maximum reward** is the initial credit — this occurs if XYZ closes below the short call strike price at expiration, resulting in both options expiring worthless.

**TIP:** Use this spread if you are bearish on the underlying stock.

**Figure 11-5: Risk Graph for XYZ Jan 50–55 Bear Call Spread**

[PAGE IMAGE: page_276.png]

The Optionetics Platinum risk graph shows two panels:
- Left panel: BAC stock price chart, with stock currently at ~$50.87; horizontal blue reference line at ~$50
  - Date: 2007-08-27
- Right panel: Risk/reward diagram with:
  - X-axis: Stock Price ($40 to $65)
  - Y-axis: Profits ($) from –300 to +250
  - Upper horizontal line at +$225 (max reward — the credit received if both options expire worthless)
  - Lower horizontal line at –$275 (max risk)
  - Dark vertical line at $52.25 (breakeven)
  - Blue vertical line at current stock price
  - Diagonal line descending from upper flat region (below $50) through breakeven to lower flat region (above $55)

**The risk chart identifies:**
- Max risk of $275 — lower horizontal line
- Max reward of $225 — upper horizontal line
- A breakeven stock price of $52.25 — dark vertical line
- A range from losses to profits displayed by a diagonal line extending from the lower strike price to the higher strike price

> **WARNING:** Pay attention to the expiration date. If the underlying stock is near the short strike price on the last trading day before expiration, you run the risk of being assigned on the short option over expiration weekend, but may no longer have the ability to exercise your long contract rights. **Close a vertical credit spread for a debit on the last trading day before expiration if the price of the underlying is near the short strike price.**

---

### Assessing Risk and Reward for a Put Credit Spread (Bull Put Spread) [MUST KNOW]

Maximum risk = (difference between option strike prices) minus the credit received.
The position uses the long put to significantly reduce the short put risk, which is high.

**Example:** Instead of an ABC bull call spread for a debit, you can create an ABC bull put spread for a credit:
- Buy 1 Jan 35 Put @ $1.70
- Sell 1 Jan 40 Put @ $4.10

**Calculations:**
- Credit = Max Reward = ($4.10 – $1.70) × 100 = **$240.00**
- Max Risk = [($40 – $35) × 100] – $240.00 = **$260.00**
- Breakeven = $40 – ($4.10 – $1.70) = $40 – $2.40 = **$37.60**

A bull put spread reduces risk by capping losses for the short put. Maximum reward is the initial credit — this occurs if ABC closes above the short put strike price at expiration, resulting in both options expiring worthless.

**TIP:** Use this spread if you are bullish on the underlying stock.

**Figure 11-6: Risk Graph for ABC Jan 35–40 Bull Put Spread**

[PAGE IMAGE: page_278.png]

The Optionetics Platinum risk graph shows two panels:
- Left panel: GILD stock price chart over time; stock price has risen from ~$20s to ~$38–42 range; horizontal blue reference line at approximately $38
  - L: O=37.09, H=37.40, L=36.70, C=36.73, –1.5%, Date: 2007-08-27
- Right panel: Risk/reward diagram with:
  - X-axis: GILD Stock Price ($20 to $50)
  - Y-axis: Profits ($) from –300 to +250
  - Upper horizontal line at +$240 (max reward)
  - Lower horizontal line at –$260 (max risk)
  - Dark vertical line at $37.60 (breakeven)
  - Blue vertical line at current stock price
  - Diagonal line from upper flat region (above $40) descending through breakeven to lower flat region (below $35)

**The risk chart identifies:**
- Max risk of $260 — lower horizontal line
- Max reward of $240 — upper horizontal line
- A breakeven stock price of $37.60 — dark vertical line
- A range from losses to profits displayed by a diagonal line extending from the higher strike price to the lower strike price
- The cost of slippage is included in this risk graph

> **REMEMBER:** Always monitor conditions for the stock after the close of trading on the last day of trading before expiration. You never want to allow a limited-risk position to turn into a high- or unlimited-risk position because you failed to manage the trade through the end.

---

### Summarizing Credit Risks and Rewards [MUST KNOW]

The risk graph for a vertical credit spread is similar to that of the vertical debit spread — both risk and reward limited. It significantly improves the short call or short put risk graph by capping risks that are either unlimited or limited but high. This is accomplished by creating a position that **covers the short option** rather than leaving it naked.

**TABLE 11-3: Risk, Reward, and Breakeven for Vertical Credit Spreads**

|  | **Bear Call Spread** | **Bull Put Spread** |
|---|---|---|
| Risk | [(Higher strike – lower strike) × multiplier] – initial credit | [(Higher strike – lower strike) × multiplier] – initial credit |
| Reward | Lower initial credit | Lower initial credit |
| Breakeven | Short strike price + (short option price – long option price) | Short strike price – (short option price – put option price) |

> **WARNING:** Although you can often execute a spread trade at a price more favorable than the current market price, always remember that **if your risk parameters signal you should exit a position, just exit it.** This can almost always be accomplished by placing a marketable limit order.

---

### Key Spread Trading Rules Summary [MUST KNOW]

**Debit spreads (you pay to enter):**
- Bull call spread → bullish outlook
- Bear put spread → bearish outlook
- Max loss = debit paid; Max gain = (strike spread × 100) – debit

**Credit spreads (you receive premium to enter):**
- Bear call spread → bearish outlook
- Bull put spread → bullish outlook
- Max gain = credit received; Max loss = (strike spread × 100) – credit

**Universal spread rules:**
- Always exit both legs together — never exit just the long leg without the short leg
- Enter debit spreads with at least 60 days to expiration
- Close credit spreads for a debit on last trading day if near the short strike price
- Use limit orders; slippage can often be managed below the quoted spread price
- Vertical debit spreads suffer accelerated time decay within 30 days of expiration — plan exit accordingly

<!-- pp. 281-300 -->

## Chapter 12: Combination Strategies: Spreads and Other Wild Things

**Topics covered:**
- Covering options with stock
- Reducing the cost of protection
- Expanding on vertical spreads
- Changing the spread time horizon

- Basic option positions reduce risk by reducing position cost; combining them with stock and other options adds a new layer of protection and potential profit [MUST KNOW]
- This chapter begins with **covered positions** (options combined with stock) and extends to **collars**, then to variations of vertical spreads: **calendar spreads** and **diagonal spreads**
- **Calendar spread**: same strike price as a vertical spread, but varying the expiration month for the two options — adds time flexibility
- **Diagonal spread**: varying both strike prices AND expiration months — provides even more flexibility for almost any short-term to long-term outlook

---

### Combining Options with Stocks

- When you protect a short option position with stock or a long option of the same type, it is said to be **covered**
- You can sell calls when holding a long stock position to reduce cost and bring in income
- A **short put** can be sold against short stock to boost returns
- Both positions reduce risk slightly by reducing the stock's cost basis
- Neither option offers much downside protection for the stock price — for that, you need a **collar**

---

### Creating "Covered" Positions [MUST KNOW]

- A **covered position** includes a short option with an obligation that you satisfy with stock or a long option of the same type for the same underlying
- Rather than unlimited or high risk (naked options), covered positions significantly reduce your risk
- Strategy: sell options against the underlying to bring in additional income AND reduce position risk by changing the cost basis

#### Covered Calls

- When you own stock, you can sell calls against the shares to bring in additional income [MUST KNOW]
- The credit from selling the call also reduces risk of the long stock position
- **Major downside**: you are capping potential gains — if the stock rises above the strike price of the call, you'll be obligated to sell at the lower strike price

> WARNING: There is an optimal time to sell calls, usually within **30–45 days to expiration**. If you need to hold onto a stock position (capital gains or similar reasons), do not sell calls against it. Otherwise, the covered call behaves just like a naked call — a position with unlimited risk.

**Use long stock with a covered call:**
- To reduce long stock risk incrementally by the short call credit
- As an income strategy for a portfolio position

- Best scenario for a covered call: you own a stock that is currently **moving sideways** in price
- By selling the call, you give yourself an advance for any further gains as limited by the strike price
- Best outcome: stock rises above strike price and gets called away — then buy the stock again after expiration and sell another covered call
- *The short call strike price should be above the long stock purchase price*
- Always keep in mind that risk with such a position is still pretty high — a bullish outlook doesn't guarantee the stock will go up

> REMEMBER: It's best to have a **long option** when the impact of time decay is at a minimum, and it's best to have a **short option** when time decay accelerates — when there are less than 30 to 45 days to expiration.

#### Covered Puts

- When you are short stock, you can sell puts against the position to bring in additional income
- The put credit reduces the cost basis of the short stock position, resulting in slightly reduced risk
- **Downside**: you're capping potential gains
- Better to capitalize on a bearish outlook with a long put or bear put spread

> REMEMBER: The short put position is an obligation, not a right. You cannot elect to have the stock put to you to offset the short stock position. The put doesn't serve as protection — you would need to buy a call to change the position risk from unlimited to limited.

- For risk/reward maximization: focus on **vertical spreads** rather than covered puts

---

### Covering the Covered Call Position

- The option leg is covered — not the stock, which maintains high risk for the covered call
- Short options come with obligations, not rights

#### Covered Call Strategy [MUST KNOW]

- A short call is a bearish position created for a credit
- Time decay works in your favor — establish with **30–45 days or less** until expiration
- Two reasons to create this position:
  1. You own the stock and you're long-term bullish on it, but moderately bearish in the short-term
  2. As part of a trading strategy: you're short-term bullish on a stock and seek to boost returns by selling a call and being assigned on the stock

> TIP: The covered call position makes sense when you have a **moderately bullish, short-term outlook** for the stock. If you are extremely bullish in the short term, a long call is a better strategy because it allows unlimited gains while the covered call position caps gains.

- Rewards are capped: if the stock rises above the short call strike price by expiration, you will likely be assigned and forced to sell your shares

#### Covered Call Risk Profile [MUST KNOW]

- Purchase stock and sell a short call when you have a short-term bullish outlook
- Short call strike price should be **above the stock purchase price** so the stock gets called away for a profit
- Ideal: stock closes just below the strike price at expiration → original call expires worthless → sell another call
- Risk for long stock–short call position is similar to basic long stock: limited but high
- Maximum reward is capped by the short option; after stock rises above the short call strike, risk of assignment

**Formulas:**
```
Max Risk = (Stock Purchase Price × # of shares) – Call Premium
Max Reward = [(Call Strike Price – Stock Purchase Price) × 100] + Initial Credit
Breakeven Level = Stock Purchase Price – Call Price
```

- Breakeven price = stock purchase price minus the option price when the position is sold
- Below this level, losses accrue

> WARNING: Never allow a short call that is part of a covered position become uncovered by selling the underlying stock. This turns a limited-risk position into an unlimited one. If you want to exit the long stock position, you must **buy the call back first** or exit both at the same time using a combination order.

**Worked example — ABC covered call:**
- ABC trading at $37.72, short-term bullish, 30 days to expiration
- Near-term 40 strike call: $0.50 ($50 credit per contract)
- Position: 100 shares of ABC stock + one short call

```
Max Risk = ($37.72 × 100) – 50 = $3,722
Max Reward = [($40 – 37.72) × 100] + 50 = $228 + 50 = $278
Breakeven = $37.72 – 0.50 = $37.22
```

[PAGE IMAGE: page_287.png]

**Figure 12-1: Risk graph for long ABC stock with a short call**
The risk graph shows the covered call position with four time-decay lines: today (32 days left, red), 22 days left (blue), 11 days left (green), and expiry/0 days left (black). The x-axis shows stock price (20–40 range), y-axis shows profit/loss. The profit line descends steeply to the left (stock decline = full stock loss), crosses zero at the $37.22 breakeven (shown as vertical line), and flattens out above $40 as a capped plateau at roughly $278 maximum profit. The curves show that as expiration approaches, the short call's time value decays favorably. The position clearly illustrates the "limited but high" risk on the downside vs the capped upside.

> WARNING: A short call that is covered by long stock only provides **minimal protection** for the long stock. The position risk remains similar to long stock alone.

---

### Reducing Protected Stock Costs

- Covering a short call obligation and protecting a long stock position are not the same thing
- The covered call strategy leaves you exposed to risk very similar to long stock
- Another way to manage stock risk for less money than a protective put strategy: create a **collar** on the stock

**Collar components:**
- Long stock
- Buying a protective put on the underlying stock
- Selling a call on the underlying stock

- By selling a call in combination with a protected stock position, you **reduce the cost of that protection**
- Your only obligation is from the short call (the put represents a right)
- The short call remains covered by the stock

> REMEMBER: A short option represents an obligation that is only considered covered if there are **no other obligations or requirements** for the associated stock or long option.

#### Defining a Collar [MUST KNOW]

- You create a **collar** by purchasing a put and selling a call for a new or existing stock position
- It is a **limited-risk, limited-reward** position that:
  - Significantly reduces long stock and covered call risk from limited, but high, to simply limited
  - Significantly reduces long stock rewards from unlimited to limited

- **Covered call strategy**: short-term trade or income generator
- **Collared position**: a protected position
- The collar offers two different risk-management dynamics that together work better if your goal is maximizing protection
- The main goal of the short call in a collar is to **reduce the cost of protection** (slightly reduces the risk of a protected put position)

> WARNING: Once you sell a call short, you are obligated to sell the underlying stock when assigned. Do not create a collar around a position unless you're willing and able to part with the underlying stock.

#### Collaring Long Stock [MUST KNOW]

- A collar is a hedged position that has limited risk and limited reward
- Risk limited to the downside by the **put strike price**; reward limited to the upside by the **call strike price**
- If stock goes below the put strike price, you don't necessarily need to exercise — you can sell the put for a profit instead

> REMEMBER: The **long put and short call strike prices** create a cap on long stock risks and rewards, respectively. The two option positions in combination provide a collar around the stock price.

**Best use case for collars:**
- Protecting gains in a stock late in the calendar year without selling the stock and incurring a taxable event
- Most collars are short-term positions initiated late in the calendar year, then removed at the beginning of the following year so the stock can be sold with the tax due only in the following year

> TECHNICAL STUFF: The term **peg** is used to describe the cap a short call creates on long stock appreciation. You may see this applied to the short call position when reviewing account balances.

#### Profiling Collar Risks [MUST KNOW]

- Risk associated with a collar strategy is significantly less than long stock
- The long put caps the risk while increasing the position's cost
- This cost is slightly offset by the credit received when selling the call

**Worked example — ABC collar:**
- Own 100 shares of ABC at $37.86, bearish over next 1.5 months, want protection
- Creating a collar using next month options (expire in 45 days)
- Buy 37.50 strike put for $1.20; sell 40 strike call at $0.70

```
Net Debit = (1.20 – 0.70) × 100 = $50
Max Risk = [(Stock Purchase Price – Put Strike Price) × 100] + Net Debit
         = [($37.86 – $37.50) × 100] + 50 = $86
Max Reward = [(Call Strike Price – Stock Purchase Price) × 100] – Net Debit
           = [($40 – 37.86) × 100] – 50 = $164
Breakeven = Stock Purchase Price + Net Debit = $37.86 + 0.50 = $38.36
```

- Risk = difference between stock purchase and put strike price, plus net cost of options
- Maximum reward: after stock rises above short call strike, risk of assignment; if strike > stock purchase price + premiums, profit when assigned

[PAGE IMAGE: page_292.png]

**Figure 12-2: Risk graph for long ABC stock with a collar**
The collar risk graph displays the characteristic "flat-bottom, rising middle, flat-top" shape. On the left side (stock below ~$37.50 put strike), the profit line is flat at approximately –$86 maximum loss — the put protection kicks in and stops further losses. In the middle zone (between put strike and call strike ~$40), the position rises with stock price, crossing zero near $38.36 (the breakeven). Above $40 (short call strike), the reward line flattens again at the maximum reward of ~$164. Multiple time-decay lines (today: 32 days, 22 days, 11 days, expiry) show how the position evolves. The collar's limited-risk / limited-reward profile is clearly visible — it's a much more contained risk shape compared to the covered call graph in Figure 12-1.

---

### Varying Vertical Spreads [MUST KNOW]

- A **vertical spread** combines a long option and a short option for the same underlying that:
  - Are the same type (call or put)
  - Expire in the same month
  - Have different strike prices

- Can create a vertical spread for a net credit or a net debit, depending on your outlook and current implied volatility
- By changing the strike prices, you can change the risk profile for a given vertical spread

> WARNING: A calendar or diagonal spread, **based on strike price**, is long the closer month option and short the further month option and is equivalent to holding a naked position. These are not strategies consistent with good risk management because of the potential for large losses.

- **Calendar spread**: varies the time until expiration rather than strike price — same strike price, different expiration months
- **Diagonal spread**: varies both expiration month AND strike prices — refers to the diagonal line that can be drawn between strike prices on an option chain

**Calendar and diagonal spreads with long option in the later month = limited-risk positions:**
- Call calendar and diagonal spreads: unlimited reward potential after expiration of the short option
- Put calendar and diagonal spreads: limited but high reward potential after expiration of the short option

#### Changing Your Vertical Spread Risk Profile [SHOULD KNOW]

- You can change the risk profile for a given vertical spread by changing the strike prices used in it, while maintaining your outlook for the stock
- Calculating the reward-risk ratio for different spreads is one way to obtain an apples-to-apples comparison for different alternatives

> REMEMBER: You can create vertical spreads for a debit or a credit. To keep the outlook and credit/debit result clear, consider the outlook for the **more expensive option**. A short call is bearish and brings a credit. The short call option in a bear call spread is more expensive, so this is a **credit spread**.

#### Volatility Skews [SHOULD KNOW]

- When identifying vertical spreads, you may uncover a **volatility skew** that makes one particular position stand out
- **Volatility skew**: a condition in the option markets where options for the same underlying have implied volatilities (IVs) that are significantly different from each other — happens when demand factors impact option prices

**Two types of volatility skews:**
- **Price skew**: Options expiring in the same month have IVs that deviate from normal conditions (e.g., in-the-money options with higher IV than out-of-the-money options)
- **Time skew**: Options expiring in different months have IVs that deviate from normal conditions (e.g., options expiring sooner that have IV higher than options expiring later)

- **Capitalize on volatility skews**: sell the option with atypically high IV and/or buy the option with atypically low IV as part of the strategy

> TIP: When uncovering a volatility skew, be sure to check the news for the company to determine if there is a specific reason for the condition.

---

### Spreading Time with Calendars [MUST KNOW]

- To buy yourself time during a period where the market is undecided and your outlook is hazy, create a **calendar spread** by combining a long option and a short option for the same underlying that:
  - Are the same type (call or put)
  - Expire in different months
  - Have the same strike price

- The longer-term option costs more than a shorter-term option with the same strike price → position created for a **net debit**

**When to use a calendar spread instead of a vertical spread:**
- Short-term outlook is neutral to bearish, long-term outlook is bullish → **call calendar spread**
- Short-term outlook is neutral to bullish, long-term outlook is bearish → **put calendar spread**

**When the strategy is NOT appropriate (if you're strongly bearish or bullish):**
- The short-term option will be assigned
- The long-term option will lose too much value

- Your risk is limited when using the long option as the longer-term leg of the spread for both call and put calendar spreads

> TIP: When a debit spread position includes two options that expire in different months, the reward and breakeven levels are **estimates** based on the price of the underlying and volatility at the earlier expiration for the short option.

#### Assessing Calendar Risk and Reward [MUST KNOW]

- Call calendar spread: buy a **longer-term call** + sell a **shorter-term call**, both at the same strike price
- The longer-term call is more expensive → position created for a net debit
- **Initial debit = maximum possible risk**
- Managing this trade is different from a vertical spread: you need to consider **two time horizons**

**Three scenarios to consider at expiration for the short option:**

**Scenario 1:** Stock moved significantly higher than the calendar strike price; short call was assigned.
Options:
- Exercise your long call option to satisfy the short call assignment
- Buy shares in the market and sell your long call if time values remain
- Create a bull call spread by holding the long call and selling a higher strike call option for the same month

**Scenario 2:** Stock moved significantly below the calendar strike price; short call expired worthless.
Options:
- Sell your long call option if it still has value
- Create another spread with the long call if bullish
- Always consider your outlook for the stock when deciding whether to maintain the long call position. If you're bearish, exit the position.
- Important: identify a **downside exit price** for the stock prior to creating a calendar spread

> REMEMBER: A **near month option** is one that is the closest to its expiration date. A **next month option** is one that expires in the month that follows the near month option.

**Scenario 3:** Stock is near the calendar strike price; short call expired worthless.
Options:
- Sell your long call
- Create another calendar spread using the existing long call and selling another closer-term option at the same strike price
- Create a bull call spread using the existing long call and selling a higher strike call option for the same month

- After the short term has expired, you may be left with an unlimited reward (long call) or limited but high reward (long put) position

#### Profiling Calendar Spread Risk [SHOULD KNOW]

- Calculating potential rewards and breakeven levels is difficult for calendar spreads with a later-month long option — because of the different expiration months for the two legs
- Advantage: you can identify a **limited, maximum risk** for the position
- Options analysis applications can estimate rewards and breakeven levels using probabilities based on historical and implied volatilities → can be extended to risk graphs

[PAGE IMAGE: page_298.png]

**Figure 12-3: Stock chart and theoretical risk graph for a call calendar spread**
This combined display shows a stock price chart (left panel, 08/06 to 09-14-07, price in the $40–$55 range) alongside the theoretical risk graph (right panel). The risk graph has a distinctive **bell-curve / tent shape** — profits on the x-axis (ranging from –200 to +100), stock price on the y-axis. Four time lines are shown: today (30 days left, red), 24 days left (blue), 12 days left (green), expiry 0 days left (black). The maximum profit occurs when the stock is AT the calendar strike price at expiration of the short option. Profits taper off symmetrically as the stock moves significantly above or below that strike. The black expiry line shows how the tent collapses — above the strike price, the reward potential is unbounded for the long call after the short call expires. This graph teaches that calendar spreads are most profitable when the stock stays near the strike through short-option expiration — they're a "stay put and let time work" strategy.

> REMEMBER: You must always understand the risks and margin requirements for positions you create. Paper trading helps you better appreciate risk. Contact your broker to be sure you truly understand option margin requirements.

---

### Defining Diagonal Spreads [MUST KNOW]

- **Diagonal spreads** are a combination of vertical spread and calendar spreads — they have BOTH different strike prices AND different expiration months
- You can vary the spread risks and rewards by varying the strike prices used
- Structure: same type (call or put), expire in different months, have different strike prices
- The longer-term option may or may not cost more than the shorter-term option — depends on the strike prices and expiration months selected
- Both **debit and credit spreads** are possible when using a diagonal-spread strategy

> TIP: Diagonal spreads have potential built-in strategic variations beyond your initial plan and can morph into different strategies after the short option is exited or expires.

- Because so many diagonal-spread combinations are possible, it's more difficult to categorize short-term versus long-term views — this is a comment on the **flexibility** of diagonal spreads

**When to use a diagonal spread instead of a calendar spread:**
- Short-term outlook is slightly more bullish than neutral + long-term outlook is bullish → **call diagonal spread for a debit**
- Short-term outlook is slightly more bearish + long-term outlook is bullish → **call diagonal spread for a credit**
- Short-term outlook is slightly more bearish than neutral + long-term outlook is bearish → **put diagonal spread for a debit**
- Short-term outlook is slightly more bullish + long-term outlook is bearish → **put diagonal spread for a credit**

- A diagonal spread may be more appealing than a calendar spread if the long option could benefit from more time or if a **volatility time skew** exists

> WARNING: Always think of the downside or what could happen when you exit a position early or your options run their time course. If you exit or allow a long option position that covers a short option to expire, you have a naked option position. Risk ranges from limited but high to **unlimited**.

- You limit your risk when using the long option for the later month leg of the spread for both call and put diagonal spreads
- Reward and breakeven values should be considered **estimates** rather than absolutes when using options-analysis applications
- If considering a diagonal spread that uses the **short option for the later month expiration**: think of it as holding a naked position

#### Assessing Risk and Reward for Diagonal Spreads [MUST KNOW]

**Worked example — call diagonal spread:**
- Stock trading at $46.64; moderately bullish short-term, believes market strengthening in a couple of months will give a nice boost
- Want: purchase a call that is near the money, finance the trade with a short call that expires sooner
- To reduce assignment chance and give appreciation potential, use a diagonal spread instead of a calendar spread

**Trade details:**
- Next month $50 strike price call: expires in 35 days, bid at $1.80 (IV of 34.6)
- Three-months-later $47.50 strike price call: offer price $3.10 (IV of 32.4)
- Note: modest **time skew** between next month options and those expiring three months later (IV 34.6 vs 32.4)

```
Initial Debit (Max Risk) = $3.10 – $1.80 = $1.30 × 100 = $130.00
Reward and breakeven points are variable
```

[PAGE IMAGE: page_300.png]

**Figure 12-4: Stock chart and theoretical risk graph for a call diagonal spread**
This figure shows the same combined stock chart / risk graph format as Figure 12-3. The stock price chart (left panel, 08/06 to 09-14-07) shows the stock hovering in the $45–$50 range with a recent sharp decline to approximately $46. The right panel shows the theoretical risk graph with four time lines: today (35 days left, red), 24 days left (blue), 12 days left (green), expiry 0 days left (black). The shape is similar to the calendar spread graph but **asymmetric** — the peak profit zone is to the right of center (near the $47.50 long call strike, not a shared strike). The x-axis runs from –200 to +300 showing much greater upside reward potential. The black expiry line curves dramatically upward to the right showing that after the short option expires, the remaining long call provides potentially large unlimited upside. Downside risk is capped at the $130 initial debit. This illustrates how a diagonal spread provides both time benefit (like a calendar) and directional bias (like a vertical), with the asymmetry created by the different strike prices.

- If the stock is below $50 at the closer-term expiration and the short call expires worthless, a few alternatives are available for the remaining long call (same decision tree as calendar spread Scenarios 2 and 3)

<!-- pp. 301-320 -->

## Chapter 13: ETFs, Options, and Other Useful Tricks

**IN THIS CHAPTER:**
- Understanding exchange-traded funds
- Reducing volatility with sectors
- Implementing ETF strategies in your portfolio
- Trading ETFs and ETF options

- **Exchange-traded funds (ETFs)** are an integral part of the investment
  scene and are extremely useful tools for professionals and individual
  investors [MUST KNOW]
- Through ETFs you can trade a wide variety of markets and sectors
  individually or with options
- ETFs can also reduce volatility — though for options traders,
  volatility is part of the game and has a place in many strategies
- ETFs give access to certain asset classes (e.g., commodities)
  previously available only to larger investors

---

### Exploring the Exchange-Traded Fund

- An **ETF** is a mutual fund that trades like a stock [MUST KNOW]
- Made up of different component stocks, bonds, and/or commodities;
  typically designed to track a specific index or segment of the market
- The exception: the **leveraged ETF**, made up of derivatives to
  achieve a stated goal (usually to move at a multiple of the daily
  trading range of the index it tracks)
- There are ETFs more akin to managed portfolios, but the majority
  are still index-inspired vehicles
- Because ETFs track a group of securities, **ETF volatility tends to
  be less than that of its component stocks** — a strong decline in
  one security may be offset by less severe declines or gains in others
- Key advantage: You can own an ETF (unlike an index), which expands
  the option strategies available to you

> **TIP:** ETFs are similar to mutual funds, but trade like a stock.
> This means you don't have to wait until the end of the day to exit
> a position.

---

### Comparing ETFs to Indexes

- An **index** is a measurement of the market value for a group of
  component stocks, bonds, and/or commodities; an ETF is a security
  that allows you to own that measurement [SHOULD KNOW]
- Most ETFs actually track a specific index
- One of the best characteristics ETFs and indexes share: **both have
  options available for trading**
- Because you can own an ETF, you can create combination positions
  to include index-like products via the ETF

> **TIP:** Check with your accountant to fully understand the tax
> implications of investing in and trading ETFs.

#### Connecting the Common Ground

ETFs share the following characteristics with indexes:
- Available for a variety of asset classes, sectors, and regions
- Both impacted by the index construction weighting method
- Offered by a variety of financial service firms

#### Weight Management for ETFs

> **TIP:** ETF managers use weightings similar to the index-weighting
> method to achieve similar returns.

- The **weighting method** used for index construction determines the
  impact a component security has on the index value [SHOULD KNOW]
- Although an ETF won't always track an index exactly, the weighting
  method affects ETF value changes given component changes:
  - **Market-capitalization weighted index:** impacted more by
    higher-capitalization stocks
  - **Price-weighted index:** impacted more by higher-priced stocks
  - **Equal-weighted index:** impacted equally by all component stocks

#### Checking Out the Differences

Key differences between ETFs and indexes:
- You can own an ETF but **not** an index
- The actual components used to create an index may be different than
  the component securities in an ETF (can include futures and swap
  arrangements)
- ETFs can be leveraged or have an inverse relationship with the
  index it tracks

- There may not be a perfect correlation between an ETF and its
  accompanying index — ETFs are not composed of the exact basket
  of component securities; can have moderate daily fluctuations
  known as **premium** or **discount** trading [SHOULD KNOW]
- Some ETFs possess additional risk of catastrophic losses if the fund
  company used more exotic trading instruments (such as swaps) and
  if not hedged properly
- A **swap** is an option of sorts — a contract between two parties
  with regard to an investment proposition that details the outcome
  of a trade. When certain previously agreed-upon contractual points
  are reached, one party must pay the other. Swaps often add
  significant amounts of leverage to investment transactions [SHOULD KNOW]
- ETFs that use leverage often add that leverage with swaps — you
  might be buying an ETF assuming you're getting one thing when in
  fact you're getting another

> **REMEMBER:** Stock risk is high, but limited to the total amount
> invested when purchased in a cash account. This option-related risk
> is leveraged but balanced because the amount of risk capital is
> smaller.

---

### ETFs and Risk [MUST KNOW]

- Your risk with an ETF is the same as with stock ownership: limited
  but high, depending on the price of the ETF and whether it was
  purchased with cash or on margin
- An index-based ETF will closely mirror the performance of its
  associated index
- Managed ETFs (not directly linked to an index, based on a manager's
  analysis) are as unpredictable as any managed portfolio

**Notable ETF examples:**
- **QID** = Proshares Ultrashort QQQ ETF: tracks the inverse daily
  percentage move of the Nasdaq 100 Index and multiplies the move
  by two through the use of leverage — creates a second means of
  making a limited-risk bearish position for some indexes
- Put debit spreads and call debit spreads remain the top alternatives
  for bearish positions
- **WARNING:** Beware options on leveraged ETFs — many have very
  low liquidity. Because options offer leverage by design, there is
  no point in trading options in leveraged ETFs

> **TIP:** Always know what you're investing in. Many ETFs are
> passively managed and based upon a specific index such as the S&P
> 500. Some newer ETFs are actively managed by portfolio managers who
> select specific securities. Always check the ETF prospectus or tear
> sheet to determine which index, if any, the ETF tracks.

---

### Pinpointing ETF Resources

- ETFs trade on major U.S. stock exchanges — buying/selling involves
  the same process as buying/selling stocks
- A good place to get excellent independent ETF advice: www.etf.com

**TABLE 13-1: ETF Resources**

| Sponsor | Site | Access |
|---------|------|--------|
| NASDAQ | www.nasdaq.com/etfs/ | Excellent summary of ETF information and performance analysis |
| ETF Database | http://etfdb.com/screener/ | ETF screening tool |
| State Street Global Advisors (SSGA) | www.sectorspdr.com | Listing of family-specific sector ETFs |
| Blackrock | www.ishares.com | Listing of widely held Blackrock ETF products |

- Avoid "analysis paralysis" — first identify your objectives then
  match the ETF that fits best
- **Risk can be reduced with ETF option strategies** because the
  initial investment is significantly reduced

---

### Distinguishing ETF and Index Options [MUST KNOW]

Index options and ETF options both provide a way to use option
strategies on a group of related securities. The two products differ
in three important respects:

1. Because ETF options have an underlying security you can own, they
   **lend themselves to combination strategies**
2. **Index options are cash-settled**; ETF options are settled using
   the underlying instrument
3. **Index options are European style or American style**; ETF options
   are only American style

- If you want to avoid assignment on all but the exercise day, then
  an index option may be your only alternative

> **WARNING:** Leverage is a double-edged sword. It can magnify your
> losses just as it magnifies gains.

> **TIP:** Avoid low-liquidity ETFs even if they offer options. You
> could be the only active trade, and that will make it hard to sell
> when it's time.

**Similarities between index options and ETF options to consider:**

- **Contract liquidity:** Not all options are actively traded. Be sure
  spreads don't significantly impact your slippage costs
- **Impact of dividends:** Certain groups of stocks provide higher
  dividend payouts. Be sure to incorporate dividends in option pricing
  calculators
- **Volatility:** Because both represent baskets of securities, they
  tend to be less volatile than their components. This may not be as
  applicable to sector-specific ETFs in some cases

> **TIP:** Index and ETF values are both affected by dividends, and
> as a result, so are the options for them. As soon as dividend
> announcements are made, these values are priced into calls and puts
> currently available. Option calculators allow you to incorporate
> dividend payments that occur during the life of the option.

---

### Identifying ETF Option Advantages [MUST KNOW]

- The number-one advantage of ETF options over index options is the
  opportunity to access **combination strategies** — ETF options are
  more flexible because you can own the underlying security and
  design covered strategies
- ETF option characteristics make them more straightforward: all
  American style — no worrying about different exercise, expiration,
  or last trading days
- If you have already traded stock options, ETF options are a natural
  next step

> **WARNING:** Watch out for expenses. ETFs have expense ratios just
> like mutual funds. Because so many ETFs are passively managed, they
> are generally lower than mutual fund fees, but compare ETFs to be
> sure you're accessing one with reasonable ones. A growing trend in
> ETFs is toward managed products, which can levy higher fees.

- By using ETFs you quickly and inexpensively access a group of
  securities that can reduce the fluctuations (volatility) in your
  portfolio — diversification, often at a reduced cost
- ETF options provide this at an even more significantly reduced cost,
  reducing your risk

**Additional ETF option risks:**
- Not all ETFs have options; not all that do will have **LEAPS**
  (Long-Term Equity AntiCiPation Security) available
- LEAPS = long-term option contracts with expirations ranging from
  more than 9 months to 2½ or 3 years
- Additional risks ETF options and ETF LEAPS introduce:
  - Time risks — securities can expire worthless
  - Potential leveraged losses on the downside magnified if using
    options on leveraged ETFs

> **REMEMBER:** Although some brokers (Fidelity, E-trade) offer
> proprietary ETF trading with no commission to their account holders,
> you will pay commissions on most other ETF purchases and sales.
> Many mutual funds can be bought and sold without a commission (no
> load) if held a minimum period of time. Read the fine print.

---

### Accessing Combination Strategies

ETFs give you access to a larger number of potential strategies than
index options or mutual funds. Strategies to manage risk:

- **Protective put position:** Long ETF combined with a long put.
  Limits ETF downside risk to the put strike price and slightly
  increases the cost of the ETF. A high but limited risk is turned
  into a limited-risk position. Potential rewards remain unlimited
  above the price of the ETF plus ETF option. [MUST KNOW]

- **Covered call position:** Long ETF combined with a short call.
  Reduces the cost of the position, moderately reducing risk. As with
  a stock-based covered call, this is an income-generating strategy
  for a short-term bearish outlook on a long-term holding. Potential
  rewards are capped by the call strike price, so a previously
  unlimited reward position becomes limited. [MUST KNOW]

- **Collared position:** Long ETF combined with a long put and a short
  call. Limits the ETF's downside risk to the put strike price and
  increases the cost of the ETF. Net increase to cost is less than a
  protective put because the credit brought in by the call slightly
  offsets the put cost. High but limited risk is turned into a
  limited-risk position. Potential rewards are capped by the call
  strike price. [MUST KNOW]

> **REMEMBER:** Not all ETFs have options available for trading. When
> researching ETFs for investing or trading, be sure to check whether
> options trade for the underlying and how liquid the fund and option
> contracts are.

---

### Reducing Portfolio Volatility with ETFs

- **Volatility** is a measure of security movement and varies by asset
- An ETF is less volatile than one of its component stocks [SHOULD KNOW]
- Even when using longer-term ETF option strategies for investing, you
  still have to purchase long options when the volatility conditions
  are right
- Check relative **historical volatility (HV)** and **implied
  volatility (IV)** levels for the options
- Without IV chart access, use **Bollinger Bands** on a price chart
  for a quick view of relative volatility conditions for an ETF
  - Bollinger bands shrink around prices when volatility decreases
  - Bollinger bands expand away from prices when volatility is rising

> **TIP:** In addition to the important role IV has in option pricing,
> it's not a good idea to ignore HV. Analyzing the relative levels
> for HV and IV will help an individual select a strategy appropriate
> for market conditions.

---

### Revisiting Volatility [MUST KNOW]

- An ETF's **historical volatility (HV)** calculation: uses past price
  changes over a certain period of time to quantify the range an ETF
  travels. Allows apples-to-apples comparison to:
  - Different HV time horizons for the ETF
  - The implied volatility of the ETF
  - The HV of another security

- **Implied volatility (IV)** is the 12-month expected future option
  volatility implied by current option prices. IV quantifies different
  market participants' expectations for the ETF, along with demand
  factors for that particular option.
- IV is a **plug** figure in option pricing models — the value needed
  to correctly price an option after all known values (strike price,
  ETF price, etc.) are considered
- **Vega** is the option Greek that measures the expected change in
  option value for every 1% change in IV

- Getting just a current IV reading is usually not enough to gain a
  sense of relative levels — you need to compare current IV to
  previous levels via a chart to gauge whether readings are high,
  low, or average

> **REMEMBER:** Trends and volatility are two primary factors
> impacting market conditions. Three possible trends: upward,
> downward, or sideways. In terms of volatility, the markets can be
> quiet, moderate range, or more explosive with wider ranges.

**Figure 13-1: Two-year implied volatility (IV) chart for SPY**

[PAGE IMAGE: page_313.png]

The chart (Standard & Poors Dep Rec — ATM Implied Volatility Chart,
Image by Optionetics) shows SPY's at-the-money implied volatility
over the period 01/06 to 04/07. Four lines track different expiration
periods:
- 7-30 day = 17.23% (red)
- 30-60 day = 18.20% (blue)
- 60-90 day = unk (green)
- >90 day = 18.65% (black)

The chart shows IV spiking periodically above 20 (at several points
during 2006) before settling back down to low levels around 10-12.
A significant spike appears in the right portion (around 04/07),
with all expiration lines rising sharply. Chart date: Currently:
09-21-07.

*Trading rule illustrated:* Compare current IV to its own historical
range to determine whether IV is relatively high or low. High IV =
sell options (expensive premium). Low IV = buy options (cheap
premium). The spike at right indicates rising fear/uncertainty —
option sellers should be cautious; buyers of options may find
relatively good value at the troughs. [MUST KNOW]

**Figure 13-2: Two-year historical volatility (HV) chart for SPY**

[PAGE IMAGE: page_314.png]

The chart (Standard & Poors Dep Rec — Statistical (Historical)
Volatility Chart, Image by Optionetics) shows SPY's HV over the
same period 01/06 to 04/07. Four lines track different lookback
periods:
- 6 day = 21.19% (red)
- 10 day = 16.70% (blue)
- 20 day = 18.84% (green)
- 100 day = 16.69% (black)

The shorter-term lines (6-day, red) are far more jagged, spiking
to ~30 at several points during volatile markets. The 100-day
(black) line is smoother and tracks the general trend. Multiple
spikes appear across 2006 and a major spike at right (~04/07).

*Trading rule illustrated:* Compare HV to IV. HV measures what has
actually happened; IV measures what the market expects. When IV >
HV, options are relatively expensive (market expecting bigger moves
than history suggests). When IV < HV, options may be cheap. An
"unusual" development in this comparison signals either a trading
opportunity or a pothole to navigate around. [MUST KNOW]

---

### Volatility and Price Risk [MUST KNOW]

- Volatility is a measure of risk and reward because the moves a
  security makes have a direct impact on your returns
- Bigger price swings in an ETF → bigger potential risk (declines
  occur rapidly), but gains can also accumulate quickly
- When buying options, you pay more for those based on a more volatile
  underlying security because its past movement (HV) is a major
  component in the option's IV
- **IV is part of the time value portion of an option** — the portion
  that decays each day as you get closer to expiration
- The fact that volatility increases an option's cost makes it
  critical to understand both HV and IV
- **Rule:** Minimize cost by purchasing volatility when IV levels are
  relatively low and selling it when time-value decay accelerates
  near expiration [MUST KNOW]

**Figures 13-3 and 13-4 illustrate the relationship between ETF price,
option price, and IV:**

[PAGE IMAGE: page_315.png]

**Figure 13-3: SPY ETF price action December 2016–February 2017**
(Chart courtesy of StockCharts.com)

Daily candlestick chart showing SPY price ranging from approximately
$223 to $237 over Dec 2016 to late Feb 2017. The price was
flattening out in late December, then moved sideways into January
before trending upward into February 2017, closing at $236.74 on
Feb 24, 2017.

[PAGE IMAGE: page_316.png]

**Figure 13-4: SPY April 21, 2017 ATM Call option and IV**
(From Investools.com)

Shows the SPY April 21, 2017 strike 236 Call option price (bid-ask
lines) over the same Dec–Feb period. Upper panel: option price
ranging from roughly $1.00 up to $3.75 (last = 3.76/3.82). Lower
panel: HV/Implied Volatility (HistImpVol — last = 8.97), and volume/
open interest (OpenInt last = 25,886).

Key observations from the chart:
- IV for the option fell as SPY price was flattening in late December
- Option price was falling along with IV during the same period
- IV stabilized in January as ETF price continued sideways
- Notice the tight correlation between SPY price action and the
  option price + IV

*Sound analytical practice:* Monitor the ETF price and the option
price simultaneously and wait for IV to bottom out. Then buy a call
option on SPY when the price of the ETF starts to rise along with IV.

**Figures 13-3 and 13-4 illustrate five key principles of
successful options trading:** [MUST KNOW]

1. A simple application of technical analysis is a major component
   of options trading
2. The price of the underlying asset sets the tone for the price
   of the option
3. Let the underlying asset guide your strategy
4. Buy options when IV is low (risk is low)
5. Sell options when IV is high (risk is high)

> **WARNING:** Don't make false assumptions. HV (also referred to as
> statistical volatility or SV) does **not** predict the future
> volatility of an asset. It uses past data to quantify movement of
> the asset and allow for an apples-to-apples comparison to other
> assets.

---

### Security Risk [SHOULD KNOW]

- Risk varies by security type and is inherent in the stock, bond,
  and commodities markets
- Diversification alleviates some market risk, but does not create
  a risk-free investment — investors accept market risk to address
  the risk that savings will not outpace inflation

> **WARNING:** You must always understand the risks associated with
> the securities you choose for investments and trading. If you don't
> thoroughly understand these risks, continue to use securities you
> do understand.

**TABLE 13-2: Relative Risk Levels**

| Level | Proxy or Security | Volatility |
|-------|-------------------|------------|
| Market | Broad-based index or ETF | Low relative to sectors and stocks |
| Sector | Sector index or ETF | Generally higher than market but less than individual stock |
| Stock | Individual stock | Generally high relative to markets or sectors |

- Higher risk for an individual stock → higher volatility → greater
  potential rewards

---

### Investing with ETFs [SHOULD KNOW]

- ETFs give inexpensive access to a diversified group of stocks via
  broad-based index ETFs or a combination of sector ETFs, similar
  to mutual funds
- What ETFs have that mutual funds don't: **protection via option
  strategies** and the ease of trading at any time during the
  trading day

**ETF investing opportunities available to option traders:**
- Investment in diversified market portfolio via broad-based sector ETFs
- Development of a diversified market portfolio via sector ETFs
- Protective put positions using ETFs
- Collared positions using ETFs
- ETF LEAPS portfolios

- The best thing ETFs bring to your investment plan is the opportunity
  to **protect the portfolio with options**

> **TIP:** Portfolio management includes the allocation of assets
> across and within asset classes. Seek to diversify holdings by
> investing in different types of assets (stocks, bonds, commodities)
> while diversifying within those assets as well.

> **TIP:** Consider your time frame before making decisions. Traders
> managing investments should complete investment analysis and trading
> analysis at separate times because holding period time horizons are
> different for each. It's difficult to exit a short-term position on
> market weakness and not think about similarly exiting your
> longer-term holdings.

---

### Selecting ETFs for Investment (Illustrative) [SHOULD KNOW]

Although an ETF may track a major index, it doesn't mean that
particular ETF is your best choice. Things to look for when
selecting an ETF:

- **How well it tracks the index or benchmark:** The most thorough
  way is to perform a correlation analysis for the returns of the
  benchmark and the ETF. Requires access to daily closing values for
  an extended period (minimally a year). Websites exist for ETF
  correlation analysis; one example is www.ETFreplay.com (free and
  subscription options) — lets you backtest, research, and analyze
  ETF strategies

- **The liquidity of the ETF:** For longer-term holds, liquidity is
  less of an issue. For more active trading, consider ETFs with
  volume of one million daily shares as a quick rule of thumb. Two
  problems with illiquid ETFs:
  1. You may end up paying more for shares than you should
  2. Some ETFs fail — investment is not at risk of total loss but
     could run into tax consequences or other issues if the ETF
     closes down

- **Whether options are available and option liquidity:** For both
  ETFs and options, the bid-ask spread can be used as a relative
  measure of share or contract liquidity. Large spreads indicate less
  liquid; small spreads indicate more widely traded. Use option chain
  data to check spreads for the highest open interest contracts

- **The expense ratio for the ETF:** Available via the broker's
  research section, prospectus, or tear sheet data. Generally,
  illiquid ETFs tend to have higher fees. Illiquid ETFs and high
  fees tend to go together

- **What instruments the fund uses to obtain its results:** Some funds
  use exotic derivatives. Read the prospectus. ETFs that use leverage
  (2X or 3X the underlying index) tend to use **swaps** — swaps add
  a new measure of risk. Only trade these if you're comfortable with
  rapid price swings

> **WARNING:** Although Wall Street usually pushes a 60% stock / 40%
> bond asset mix, your asset allocations depend on your individual
> needs, constraints, and risk tolerance, as well as your outlook on
> different groups. There is no one-size-fits-all for investing.

---

### Assessing Market Conditions (Illustrative) [SHOULD KNOW]

- Suppose your main investment goal is to obtain results similar to
  the S&P 500 Index (SPX). Using the SPX as your portfolio benchmark,
  you want to select an ETF that serves as a good proxy — **SPY** fits
  the bill
- Example scenario: Fall 2007 — you just added $10,000 to an
  investment account and are exploring investment possibilities.
  Primarily focused on whether to allocate to a broad-based market
  index now or wait for the next portfolio assessment scheduled in
  a few weeks
  *(Note: This is illustrative only — not a recommendation, as
  everyone's situation is different)*

