### Figure 13-7: VIX Weekly Chart with SPX Overlay and Relative Strength [MUST KNOW]

[CHART: chart_p325_01.png]
**Figure 13-7** — ProfitSource, VIX weekly chart (Market Volatility, CBOE-no options).
Data: 9/21/2007, VIX Close: 6.19 (H: 27.08, L: 18.37), Chg: -5.92, CP: -23.76%.
Two panels:

**Upper panel — VIX (black jagged line) vs. SPX (gray smoother line), scaled separately:**
- Left y-axis (price scale ~1000–1520) = SPX. Right y-axis (11–29) = VIX
- Strong **negative correlation** clearly visible: when VIX spikes up, SPX is declining; when VIX
  drops, SPX is rising
- SPX recently made a high, pulled back sharply (VIX spiked), then SPX recovered (VIX heading down)
- VIX currently heading downward from its spike — suggesting the SPX bottom from last month may hold

**Lower panel — Relative Strength Comparison (SPX / CBOE VIX):**
- Shows the ratio of SPX to VIX plotted over time
- At the right edge, the ratio is rising (SPX strengthening relative to VIX) — a bullish confirmation

**Teaching point:**
- VIX is a measure of implied volatility for SPX options — also called the **"Fear Index"**
- Declining VIX after a spike historically coincides with or follows bottoms in SPX
- Use VIX direction alongside SPX trend to gauge whether a recovery is genuine

